2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Philip Of Savoy, duke of Nemours (1490-1533)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1528 Francis gave him the duchy of Nemours and married him to Charlotte of OrleansLongueville. He died on the 25th of November 1533 His son, James (1531-1585), became duke of Nemours in 1 533. He distinguished himself at the si
2 CONC eges of Lens and Metz (1552-1553), at the battle of Renty (1554) and in the campaign of Piedmont (1555). He was a supporter of the Guises, and had to retire for some time into Savoy in consequence of a plot. On his return to Franc
2 CONC e he fought the Huguenots, and signalized himself by his successes in Dauphine and Lyonnais. In 1567 he induced the court to return from Meaux to Paris, took part in the battle of Saint Denis, protested against the peace of Longju
2 CONC meau, and repulsed the invasion of Wolfgang, count palatine of Zweibriicken. He devoted his last years to letters and art, and died at Annecy on the 15th of June 1585.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Anne of Este, the widow of Francis, duke of Guise, the duke left a SOD, Charles Emmanuel (1567-1595), who in his youth was called prince of Genevois. Involved in political intrigues by his relationship with the Guises,
2 CONC  he was imprisoned after the assassination of Henry, duke of Guise, and his, brother the cardinal of Lorraine, in 588, but contrived to escape. He fought at Ivry and Argues, and was governor of Paris when it was besieged by Henry 
2 CONC IV. After quarrelling with his half-brother Charles of Lorraine, duke of Mayenne, he withdrew to his government of Lyonnais, where he endeavoured to make himself independent. He was imprisoned, however, in the château of Pierre-En
2 CONC cise by the archbishop of Lyons. After his escape he attacked Lyons, but was defeated owing to the intervention of the constable de Montmorency. He died at Annecy in July 1595.
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother Henry (1572-1632), called originally marquis de Saint-Sorlin, succeeded him as duke. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques of Savoy, 2nd Duke of Nemours
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques of Savoy, 2nd Duke of Nemours (October 12, 1531 – June 15, 1585) became Duke of Nemours in 1533.
2 CONT
2 CONT He distinguished himself at the sieges of Lens and Metz (1552–1553), at the battle of Renty (1554) and in the campaign of Piedmont (1555).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a supporter of the house of Guise, and had to retire for some time into Savoy in consequence of a plot. On his return to France he fought the Huguenots, and signalized himself by his successes in Dauphiné and Lyonnais. In 1
2 CONC 567 he induced the court to return from Meaux to Paris, took part in the battle of Saint Denis, protested against the peace of Longjumeau, and repulsed the invasion of Wolfgang, count palatine of Zweibrücken. He devoted his last y
2 CONC ears to letters and art, and died at Annecy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Anne d'Este, daughter of Duke Ercole II of Ferrara and Renée of France, and widow of Francis, Duke of Guise. He was succeeded as Duke of Nemours by his son, Charles Emmanuel de Savoie, 3rd Duc de Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fictionalized version of the 2nd Duke of Nemours serves as the love interest in the early novel La Princesse de Clèves, which takes place at the court of Henry II of France.
2 CONT
