1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_de_Cr%C3%A9quy
2 CONT
2 CONT François, chevalier de Créquy and marquis de Marines (1625 – 3 February 1687) was marshal of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT François was a member of the aristorcatice and military Créquy family. He was the youngest son of Charles II (died 1630) who was the second son of Charles I de Blanchefort (1578-1638) a Marshal of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Créquy took part as a boy in the Thirty Years' War, distinguishing himself so greatly that at the age of twenty-six he was made a maréchal de camp, and a lieutenant-general before he was thirty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Créquy was regarded as the most brilliant of the younger officers, and won the favor of Louis XIV by his fidelity to the court during the second Fronde.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1667 Créquy served on the Rhine, and in 1668 he commanded the covering army during Louis XIV's Siege of Lille, after the surrender of which the king rewarded him with the marshalate. In 1670 he overran the Duchy of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after this Turenne, his old commander, was made marshal-general, and all the marshals were placed under his orders. Many resented this, and Créquy, in particular, whose career of uninterrupted success had made him over-con
2 CONC fident, went into exile rather than serve under Turenne. After the death of Turenne and the retirement of Condé, he became the most important general officer in the army, but his overconfidence was punished by the severe defeat of
2 CONC  Conzer Bruck (1675) and the surrender of Trier and his own captivity which followed.
2 CONT
2 CONT However in the later campaigns of the Franco-Dutch war Créquy showed himself again a cool, daring and successful commander, and, carrying on the tradition of Turenne and Condé, he was in his turn the pattern of the younger general
2 CONC s of the stamp of Luxembourg and Villars. He died in Paris on 3 February 1687.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois de Créquy had two sons, whose brilliant military abilities bade fair to rival his own. The elder, François Joseph, marquis de Créquy (1662-1702), already held the grade of lieutenant-general when he was killed at Luzzara 
2 CONC on 13 August 1702; and Nicolas Charles, sire de Créquy, was killed before Tournai in 1696 at the age of twenty-seven.
