1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>William Johnstone, 2nd Earl of Annandale and Hartfell, 1st Marquess of Annandale KT</strong> (17 February 1664 – 14 January 1721) was a Scottish nobleman. He was the son of James Johnstone, 1st Earl of Hartfell and Henrie
2 CONC tta Douglas. He succeeded to the Earldom of Annandale and Hartfell on the death of his father in 1672...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...He was a friend of Monmouth and nominally supported Revolution, but joined "The Club" of Jacobite malcontents and was imprisoned in connection with Montgomery plot. He was restored to favour on making confession and was created
2 CONC  an Extraordinary Lord of Session in 1693, and a Lord of the Treasury. He received a pension for services in connection with Glencoe inquiry...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...He was created Marquess of Annandale in 1701, Lord High Commissioner to the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland in 1701 and 1711, Keeper of the Privy Seal of Scotland in 1702, and president of the Privy Council of Scotla
2 CONC nd from 1692 to 1695, 1702-4 and 1705-6. He was appointed a Knight of the Thistle in 1704, joint Secretary of State from March–September 1705. He opposed the Union, but later served as a representative peer from 1709-13. He was Ke
2 CONC eper of the Great Seal of Scotland from 1714-16...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...William was married twice: first on 1 January 1682 to Sophia Fairholm, daughter of John Fairholm of Craigiehall, and second on 20 November 1718 to Charlotte Van Lore Bempde, daughter of John Vanden Bempde of Hackness."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'William Johnstone, 1st Marquess of Annandale', <em>Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia</em>, 22 January 2012, 22:24 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=William_Johnstone,_1st_Marquess_of_
2 CONC Annandale&oldid=472691202> [accessed 29 September 2012]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Notes ==
2 CONT *  Daniel Defoe described him as "tall, lusty and well shaped, with a very black complexion; extremely carried away by his private interests, possessing both good sense and a manly expression, but not much to be trusted" and "of n
2 CONC o reputation on either side because steady to none."3
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT * son of James Johnstone, 1st Earl of Hartfell and Henrietta Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 
2 CONT # on 1 January 1682 to Sophia Fairholm, daughter of John Fairholm of Craigshall
2 CONT # on 20 November 1718 to Charlotte Van Lore Bempde, daughter of John Vanden Bempde of Hackness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of first marriage by Sophia Fairholm
2 CONT * Lady Henrietta Johnstone (11 November 1682 – 25 November 1750) married on 31 August 1699 to Charles Hope, 1st Earl of Hopetoun
2 CONT * Mary Johnstone (15 June 1686 – died an infant)
2 CONT * James Johnstone, 2nd Marquess of Annandale (1687– 21 February 1730) died unmarried
2 CONT * John Johnstone born in 1688 and died young
2 CONT * William Johnstone (1696 – 24 December 1721)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of second marriage by Charlotte Van Lore Bempde
2 CONT # George Johnstone, 3rd Marquess of Annandale (29 May 1720 – 29 April 1792) died unmarried
2 CONT # John Johnstone (8 June 1721 – October 1742) died unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT == Links ==
2 CONT * [http://thepeerage.com/p2740.htm#i27400 the peerage] ,[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Johnstone,_1st_Marquess_of_Annandale Wikipedia]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 2nd marriage - to Charlotta Van Den Bempde (1700-1762)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Johnstone, 2nd Earl of Annandale and Hartfell, 1st Marquess of Annandale KT (17 February 1664 – 14 January 1721) was a Scottish nobleman. He was the son of James Johnstone, 1st Earl of Annandale and Hartfell and Henrietta 
2 CONC Douglas. He succeeded to the Earldom of Annandale and Hartfell on the death of his father in 1672. He was a friend of Monmouth and nominally supported Revolution, but joined "The Club" of Jacobite malcontents and was imprisoned in
2 CONC  connection with Montgomery plot. He was restored to favour on making confession and was created an Extraordinary Lord of Session in 1693, and a Lord of the Treasury. He received a pension for services in connection with Glencoe i
2 CONC nquiry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created Marquess of Annandale in 1701, Lord High Commissioner to the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland in 1701 and 1711, Keeper of the Privy Seal of Scotland in 1702, and president of the Privy Council of Scotland 
2 CONC from 1692 to 1695, 1702-4 and 1705-6. He was appointed a Knight of the Thistle in 1704, joint Secretary of State from March–September 1705. He opposed the Union, but later served as a representative peer from 1709-13. He was Keepe
2 CONC r of the Great Seal of Scotland from 1714-16. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William was married twice: first on 1 January 1682 to Sophia Fairholme, daughter of John Fairholme of Craigiehall, and second on 20 November 1718 to Charlotte Van Lore Bempde, daughter of John Vanden Bempde of Hackness. https://en
2 CONC .wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Johnstone,_1st_Marquess_of_Annandale
2 CONT
2 CONT Earldome of Hartfell
2 CONT
2 CONT His father died when William was 8 years old so William was heir to the Earldome of Hartfell and Annandale at that time. William was 18 and Sophia was 14 when they married. William was appointed an Extraordinary Lord of Session on
2 CONC  23 Nov 1693. In 1695 he was appointed President of the parliament of Scotland (May 1695 to July 1696) and appointed one of the Lords of the treasury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Promise to Anne Harris
2 CONT
2 CONT Description
2 CONT
2 CONT Court case involving Sir William Johnstone's wife v. Anne Harris to enforce promise made to the unmarried Harris.  See document in Memories section.  
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a often cited case Annandale v. Harris.  This case was brought by William Johnstone widow against Anne Harris.  Sir William apparently gave Anne Harris a sealed (stamped with an official seal) written contract (bond) for 
2 CONC either money or property as consideration for her cohabitation with him.  She was found by the Court to be a virtuous women prior to being seduced by Sir William.  The presumption in law was that such contracts are void and unenfo
2 CONC rceable.  But because Anne Harris was found to be a moral woman, the contract was enforced.- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Contributor
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 March 2016 byKeith Hendricks 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Marquess Of Annandale Johnston, "Scotland Marriages, 1561-1910" William Marquis Of Annandale Johnston in entry for John Johnston, "Scotland Births and Baptisms, 1564-1950" William Marquis Of Annandale Johnston in entry for
2 CONC  John Johnston, "Scotland Births and Baptisms, 1564-1950" William Marquis Of Annandale Johnston in entry for Mary Johnston, "Scotland Births and Baptisms, 1564-1950" [Move] William Johnstone, 1st Marquess of Annandale
