1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===MAJOR-GENERAL JOHN JOHNSTONE===
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Hon. John Johnstone , second son of James Johnstone, the 1st Earl of Annandale, entered the army.  He was one of the revolting captains in 1689 (after Killicrankie), and with his maternal uncle, George Douglas (Earl of Dunbar
2 CONC ton, Colonel of Dunbarton regiment), and many other officers left Scotland and entered the French service, where he served with distinction, until the death of William III (1702).  He returned to Scotland and died, leaving 4 sons 
2 CONC and 1 daughter.  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Some Prominent Virginia Families" by Louise Pecquet du Bellet
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT Town of Andrews Situated On Land of Annandale Scions: Andrews, South Carolina 29510 < [https://members.tripod.com/~andrews_sc/history1790.html link] >
2 CONT
2 CONT Information on the large "Hartfell" estate, once situated on the Black River in the vicinity of the Georgetown-Williamsburg border, whose owners were said to be heirs to the marquisette and earldoms of Annandale, have been supplie
2 CONC d to The News and Courier by James D. Johnson, III, of Lexington, VA. The data, following, were contained in a copy of a letter sent by Mr. Johnson to Percy LaBruce of The Georgetown Times which has been publishing articles on old
2 CONC  Georgetown families and plantations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The peculiar history of the American Johnstones of Annandale begins properly with Captain the Hon. John Johnstone, second son of James, Earl of Annandale and Hartfell and Lady Henrietta Douglas and brother of William first Marques
2 CONC s of Annandale. Raised in the home of his uncle, the Duke of Hamilton, John’s ardent support of the fated Stuart cause (which monarchs, besides being related to had ennobled his forebears) led his descendants into many strange adv
2 CONC entures. John was married to Elizabeth, daughter of Gabriel Belchier in 1698 and went to France with the deposed James II, serving "with distinction" under Louis XIV. The Battle of Boyne found him still further out of grace with t
2 CONC he existing government, but the influence of his brother, who had espoused the cause of William and Mary, saved his life. Shortly afterward he was enabled to return to the ancestral Scottish border estates of Annandale. Here, for 
2 CONC a while, he and his sons resided at "Stapleton," an ancient fortress-tower overlooking a branch of the Annan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel, eldest son of John, inaugurated "The Craftsman," a Jacobite political journal, in collaboration with his relative William Johnston, Earl of Bath, and Bolingbroke. By influence thus gained he was in 1724, at the instance o
2 CONC f Lord Wilmington, sent to North Carolina as royal governor to succeed the unpopular Burrington. This fact was to be of great importance in the subsequent history of the Annandales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert, John’s second son, married to his Irish cousin Caroline Johnstone of Armagh, entertained his father sentiments, regarding the then rulers of Great Britain as usurpers. Prince Charles Edward’s rebellion of 1746 found Gilbe
2 CONC rt, "General Stapleton," commanding the vanguard of the Bonny Prince’s army. On the failure of the Battle of Culloden, in which Stapleton was injured, he and his son Gilbert, Jr., were forced to flee for their lives – first to Ire
2 CONC land and then to North Carolina, where Governor Gabriel’s greatest brother was to inveigle the colonists into paying quit-rents. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Gabriel Johnstone had built at "Brompton," on the Cape Fear, an imposing mansion which he intended to occupy, as soon as it was complete. He failed to reckon, however, on falling in love with Penelope, the step-daughter o
2 CONC f Governor Charles Eden and in her own right a large heiress. By the time Brompton was completed, he found himself permanently settled on his wife’s plantation at Edenton. Thus to Gilbert’s joy at escaping the fate of being drawn-
2 CONC and-quartered along with the gallant Balmerino and others, and at once more seeing his brothers including the youngster Samuel, whose son and namesake was one day to be almost unanimously elected president of the Continental Conve
2 CONC ntion, was added that of finding an estate complete to thoroughbred horses ready for his immediate occupancy: here he died in 1775, leaving a family that fairly filled the spacious halls of Brompton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But the hatred of the "usurping Hanovers" had not died out with the less violent times. The following memorable year, Gilbert, second after cousin "Sam Johnston" had succeeded in exhorting the people of his state to be the first t
2 CONC o declare independence of a Britain turned tyrant, organized at his own expense a squadron of partisan light horse, and commanded them "without pay or bounty" throughout the war. He served at first in North Carolina under Folsome 
2 CONC and later in South Carolina with Francis Marion, the plans for whose famous corps had been made in his house. He carried the war to the enemy in the traditional way of his moss-trooping ancestors, who bore as their crest a "flying
2 CONC  spur," won on horseback in saving the life of Bruce. With Gilbert were his sons, Gilbert III and Hugo, acting as troop commanders. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The war over and their objects achieved, the Gilberts began to pine for the green lowland country into which their campaign with the Swamp Fox had taken them. Sheltering Brompton was bade farewell. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The year 1790 found the infant America on its feet, Gilbert II and Gilbert III settled on "Hartfell," and Gilbert IV celebrating his ninth birthday. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A home rose at "Potato Bed Ferry," looking over the Black into Williamsburg, ;and became the hub of a twenty-odd thousand acre plantation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then came the news and cousin George, third and last Marquess of Annandale, Earl of Annandale and Hartfell, Viscount Annand, Baron de Joinville, Lord Johnstone of Lochwood, Lochmaben, Moffatdale and Evansdale, Heretable Constable 
2 CONC of the King’s Palace of Lochamben and Steward of Annandale, had died unmarried, and with his death the male line of William, elder brother of John of Stapleton, became extinct. Several writers to the Signet are known to have come 
2 CONC to Carolina with the news. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether the Gilberts seriously considered returning to a Scotland under foreign rule we do not know. Contemporary records show, however, that shortly thereafter new grants were made out to Gilbert Johnston. . . of lands bordering 
2 CONC on the Black . . . ." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert, fourth, was married to Hannah Ford, daughter of Isaac Ford, whose grandfather was Lord High Sheriff of London; and secondly, to Harriet, daughter of B. Allston Tillman. At Potato Bed Ferry House in 1806, his first son Jos
2 CONC eph Benjamin, was born. John Thomas, by his second wife, was born twenty years later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Benjamin, educated abroad, returned to his home when about thirty to practice surveying and planting, and to become a militia officer. His choice of a mate was Elizabeth Green, of one of the first settled families of Prince
2 CONC  Frederick, and descended from the ancient Greenes of Boketon, in Northamptonshire. Her father was Captain Richard G. Green, son of the youthful Captain Richard Green who was at one time with Sergeant McDonald in harboring no love
2 CONC  for the unfortunate Major Gainey, as the following incident relates. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Major Gainey, with another Tory visited Green’s mother’s house one night when Richard and James happened to be on furlough. They decamped at once with the boys in hot pursuit. This Major Gainey was the same man who carried the ba
2 CONC yonet in his back into Georgetown. Being asked how it felt, he replied that it gave a great pain but it was not half so bad as when Mr. Green’s rifle ball knocked nearly all his teeth down his throat!" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Green was a grandson of John Green, original owner of "Mansfield" plantation, near Georgetown, known as the home of the distinguished Parker and Man families. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Benjamin Johnstone changed the spelling of his name to "Johnson" a few years prior to the War Between the States, because of the frequent confusion with the family of Archibald Johnstone of nearby "Annandale" plantation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Not spoiling the 100 per cent Confederate record of Georgetown, Joseph Benjamin himself, until his death in 1861 and his four sons all donned the gray immediately after the firing on Fort Sumter. The eldest son, Richard Green, was
2 CONC  a member of the Hampton Legion. Gilbert joined Company E, Tenth regiment, South Carolina Volunteers; John was in Company L, Seventh regiment, South Carolina Volunteers; and Jasper, too young to be sent to the front, served as a p
2 CONC rison guard at Camp Marion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Green Johnstone’s eldest son, by Carolina Barrineau of a Charleston Huguenot family, was Joseph Benjamin, second, born at Indiantown into an era of Reconstruction, to see the fortunes of his family already swept away. When
2 CONC  still a boy, he took an ax in his hands, and began to build eventually to own his own "Indian Hut" and other plantations. His wife was Ida Allene Young, a daughter of James Hugh Heyward Young and Julia-Jeannete Scott McElveene Jo
2 CONC hnston. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the Hartfell lands, in the meantime, came into the possession of Richard Kellehan who succeeded in making a fortune from them before they were divided up into innumerable small holdings, and the town of Andrews rose where 
2 CONC once the scions of Annandale had planted cotton and indigo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A number of personal belongings of the people herein mentioned are still in the possession of their descendants. Among these are a brace of silver crested pistols used by the first Gilbert Johnstones in the Scottish wars and in th
2 CONC e American Revolution, engraved cuff-links and bookplates, and a great secretary-bookcase said to have been the property of the Thomas Lynches. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Article appeared on April 20, 1941 in The News & Courier - ( which is now: The Post & Courier ) Charleston, South Carolina 
2 CONT
2 CONT Web Page Created by:  Copyright 1997 Andrews Revitalization Association- All Rights Reserved Above Article is property of The News & Courier, Charleston, SC 
2 CONT
2 CONT All of the above was found at http://members.tripod.com/~andrews_sc/history1790.html
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Betty Matthews sent me this info many years agoVirtually all my Johnstone information is from "The Johnstone Papers", complied by James D. Johnstone of Georgetown, SC. These papers are available on microfilm through the Mormon Chu
2 CONC rch.It included a letter from''' Gilbert (Jr.)''' to his daughter Susannah. I have a rather poor copy of it which is below.
2 CONT
2 CONT "My grandfather John Johnstone of Stapleton, officer in Scotch regiment in French service, married Elizabeth, her father Gabriel Belchier, French Protestant. Their children were 
2 CONT *1 John, he and only son died in north Britain. 
2 CONT *2. Gabriel, Governor of North Carolina. 
2 CONT *3. Gilbert, my father. 
2 CONT *4. Samuel lived in Onslow N. Carolina. 
2 CONT * 5. Elizabeth married Thomas Keenan our house Armagh. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My father married Caroline, her grandfather George Johnstone Armagh 1724. Children, '''Gilbert''', Henry, Caroline, Gabriel, Robert, William, Isabel, John. 
2 CONT *I [Gilbert Jr.] married Margaret Warburton north Carolina 2d June 1750. Children, Hugo, Gilbert, Jean, Isabel, Henry died Catawba County, son James col. in war. 
2 CONT *Caroline married William Williams, son William. 
2 CONT *John lived in Yadkin County, now Bertie, NC. 
2 CONT * Gabriel married Janet McFarland, son Francis killed, lieut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother and Aunt Francis died Brompton. My father to Ireland after 1715. Got my lands through George Gould. Barfield tories burned my house to cellar. Was at Culloden with father, he wounded. Came Cape Fear 1746. My father died 177
2 CONC 5. Marion, two Horrys and Francis Huger met Folsome and Giles my house. All chose Marion bar Folsome. Hugo took my men with Marion 1780 all horsemen. Franics Huger and James often at my house. John Rutherford a tory.Writ by my han
2 CONC d for Susanna 8th day March 1790Gilbert Johnston, Gent."(Folded and addressed on back) "To Susanna Johnston by Stephen"
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT This family line has long claimed to be of the Annandale Johnstones. In 1830 there was a Peerage case regarding the issue of the Annandale Johnstones. The Johnstone and Kenan families of South Carolina have long claimed to be dece
2 CONC ndents of the Annandale Johnstones, also Thepeerage.com has another marriage for Major General John Johnstone to Catherine Christine von Ahlefeldt, daughter of Count Detlev Sievert Graf von Ahlefeldt, a Polish nobleman. An interes
2 CONC ting line and worthy of inclusion and further investigation. E.H.D
2 CONT John Henry Goodinge Johnstone's Petition claiming the Earldom of Annandale.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * '''Military service''': French Service and Officer in a Scotch Regiment
