2 CONT Notes  	
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 - Henry Erskine, third Lord Cardross (1650'961693), was a covenanter.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Erskine was the eldest son of David Erskine, 2nd Lord Cardross, by his first wife, Anne, who was fifth daughter of Sir Thomas Hope, king's advocate. The title was originally conferred on the first Earl of Mar, and, in accordan
2 CONC ce with the right with which he was invested of conferring it on any of his heirs male, it was granted by him to his second son Henry, along with the barony of Cardross. By his father young Erskine had been educated in the princip
2 CONC les of the covenanters, and at an early period distinguished himself by his opposition to the administration of Lauderdale. In this he was strongly supported by his wife, Catherine, youngest of the two daughters and coheiresses of
2 CONC  Sir William Stewart of Kirkhill.
2 CONT     On account of his wife's determination to have a Presbyterian chaplain to perform worship in her own house he was fined 4,000l. of which he paid 1,000l., and after an attempt to obtain a remission for the balance he was, 5 Aug
2 CONC ust 1675, committed to the prison of Edinburgh, where he remained for four years. In May of the same year, when, during his absence in Edinburgh, conventicles were being held near Cardross, a party of guards in search of a covenan
2 CONC ter named John King entered his house at midnight, broke into his chests, and after acting with great rudeness towards his wife placed a guard on it.
2 CONT     Complaints To The King
2 CONT     Their complaints that the conventicles then being held had his encouragement were the chief causes why his fine was not relaxed. On 7 Aug. 1677, while still in prison, he was fined in one half of his rent for permitting his tw
2 CONC o children to be christened by unlicensed ministers. In 1679 the king's forces in their march westwards went two miles out of their way to quarter on his estates of Kirkhill and Uphall, West Lothian. He obtained his release from p
2 CONC rison, 30 July of that year, on giving bond for the amount of his fine, and early next year went to London, where he laid before the king a narrative of the sufferings to which he had been exposed. This proceeding gave great offen
2 CONC se to the Scottish privy council, who sent a letter to the king accusing Cardross of misrepresentation, the result being that all redress was denied him. Thereupon he emigrated to North America, where he established a plantation a
2 CONC t Charlestown Neck, South Carolina. On 28 October 1685 his estate in Scotland was exposed to sale by public roup, and was bought by the Earl of Mar at seventeen years' purchase. Cardross, having been driven from the settlement in 
2 CONC Carolina by the Spaniards, went to Holland, and in 1688 he accompanied the Prince of Orange to England.
2 CONT     In the following year he raised a regiment of dragoons and served under General Mackay against Dundee. An act was passed restoring him to his estates, and he was also sworn a privy councilor and constituted general of the mint
2 CONC . In July 1689 the Duke of Hamilton, the king's commissioner, at a meeting of the council, fell 'with great violence' on Lord Cardross, asserting that it was by his dragoons that the episcopal minister of Logie had been prevented 
2 CONC from entering his church; but Cardross denied all knowledge of anything asserted to have happened.
2 CONT     Service Of The King[edit]
2 CONT     Cardross was engaged in the Battle of Killiecrankie, of which he sent an account to Lord Melville in a letter of 30 July. When the Duke of Hamilton proposed a new oath to the council, Cardross objected to it as contrary to the
2 CONC  instrument of government, and also 'because the maner of swering by the Bible is nether the Scotish nor the Presbiterian forme, and seems to raise the Bible as more than God'. In the instructions sent by King William on 18 Dec. 1
2 CONC 689 to 'model three troops of dragoons,' Cardross was proposed as lieutenant-colonel and captain of the first troop. In 1690 he was appointed one of a commission to examine into the condition of the universities. In October 1691 h
2 CONC e went to London along with the Earl of Crawford to support the proceedings of the Scotch council against the episcopalians.
2 CONT     He died at Edinburgh on 21 May 1693. He had four sons and three daughters. His eldest son, David Erskine, 4th Lord Cardross, succeeded to the earldom of Buchan in 1695.
2 CONT     [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Erskine,_3rd_Lord_Cardross]
2 CONT
2 CONT     2 - Birth 1650 • Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT     Arrival 1670-1685 • South Carolina
2 CONT     Marriage 03 Mar 1671 • Caldross, Midlothia, Scotland to Catherine Stewart Co-Heiress Of Kirkhill & Strabrock (1668'961725)
2 CONT     Imprisonment 1675 - 1679 • Scotland
2 CONT     He was imprisoned, after opposing the administration in Scotland of the Duke of Lauderdale.
2 CONT     Migration 1683 • Stewarts Town near Port Royal, South Carolina, USA
2 CONT     30 Scottish Presbyterians led by Henry Erskine prepared for the coming of nearly 150 Scots who would arrive the following year. But a few years later most of the settlers were murdered by Spaniards from Florida and the remaind
2 CONC er fled to Charleston.
2 CONT     Arrival 1684 • Charleston, South Carolina
2 CONT     Return To England 1688 From North Carolina he went to Holland and was reunited with his wife. The opportunity to return home was presented in 1688 when, with the other exiled Scots, he accompanied William III, Prince Of Orange
2 CONC  to England.
2 CONT     Henry returned to Scotland and took the governmental seal of Stuart Town with him. 100 years later, his great grandson presented the seal to Thomas Pinckney, the U.S. Minister to the court of St. James. Today the seal resides 
2 CONC in the Museum of Charleston.
2 CONT     Death 21 May 1693 • Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT     Burial Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT     Buchan Title 1695 • Scotland
2 CONT     The death of William Erskine 8th Earl of Buchan opened the succession to the title and estates of Buchan to Henry's son David.
2 CONT     [Michelle Erskine's Ancestry.com tree CLAN ERSKINE - MBE Owner: erskinem2]
2 CONT
2 CONT     3 - ERSKINE, HENRY, Lord Cardross, born 1646 son of David Erskine and Anne Hope, married Katherine Stewart 1671, imprisoned Edinburgh Castle 1675-1679, emigrated from Gourock on the
2 CONT     «i»Carolina Merchant, «/i»170 tons, 16 guns, arrived at Charleston on the Ashley River, South Carolina, 2.10.1684, with 148 Scots, settled in Stuartstown, Port Royal, returned to Scotland before 4.1690, died 21.5.1693.
2 CONT     (The Original Scots Colonists of Early America. Supplement 1607-1707)
2 CONT
