2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jane Douglas, b. 17 Mar 1698, d. 22 Nov 1753 at Crosscauseway, near the Windmill, Edinburgh, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents: James Douglas, 2nd Marquess of Douglas (c.1646 – 25 February 1700) & Mary Kerr
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT # 4 Aug 1746 at Drumsheugh, Edinburgh, to Sir John Steuart of Grandtully, Baronet (d. 14 Jun 1764 at Murthly).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT # Archibald James Edward Steuart Douglas, b. 10 Jul 1748, Lord Douglas of Douglas, d. 26 Dec 1827, 
2 CONT #  Sholto Thomas Steuart, b. 10 Jul 1748, d. 5 May 1753.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Did She Buy Her Babies?==
2 CONT
2 CONT * from http://douglashistory.co.uk/history/janeladydouglas.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jane Douglas had born twins, Archibald and Sholto Stewart, under suspicious circumstance while in France in 1748 and at the age of 47. Her brother Archibald, Duke of Douglas, refused to acknowledge them as his heirs. Furtherm
2 CONC ore, he cut off his sister's allowance and subjected them to abject poverty. Lady Jane died in 1753 being preceded by the younger of the twins, Sholto. It seems that near the end of his life the Duke felt some remorse for his hars
2 CONC h treatment of his sister and in his last illness executed a deed admitting the right of his nephew, Archibald Stewart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Considerable doubt was expressed over the births of the 'twins' and led to the legal case called [http://douglashistory.co.uk/history/articles/douglas_cause.htm The Douglas Cause] - which was won by her son Archibald Stewart, late
2 CONC r 1st Lord Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it seems the twins really were bought.  Documents discovered, in summer 2008, in the archive of the present Earl of Home suggest that Lady Jane lied, that she connived in her husband’s planned deceit, and that she regrett
2 CONC ed the falsehood ever after. The writer Karl Sabbagh, who has been researching the Douglas Cause for several years, has unearthed a document in Lady Jane’s handwriting which, he says, suggests that she was guilty, and she was well
2 CONC  aware of the enormity of her crime.
2 CONT
2 CONT “In the light of the ambiguous results of the years of legal investigation and court hearings, I didn’t expect to find much more than picturesque detail in the archives,” Sabbagh said. “I came across various letters written in a f
2 CONC irm and measured way by Lady Jane about aspects of the case. But one document in her own hand is very different. It is a litany of guilt, written hastily and full of emotion to her God, in a barely legible scrawl.”
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://sites.google.com/site/fivegateways/alphabetical-index-d/douglas
2 CONT * http://douglashistory.co.uk/history/janeladydouglas.htm
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Douglas-3569
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/150250401/jane-stewart
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jane Douglas was a Scottish noblewoman. She married secretly and had twins abroad at the age of fifty who would inherit the family's riches. This birth was thought incredible and the ensuing long and expensive court case was 
2 CONC fought in three countries, and it was settled in her favor by the House of Lords in 1769, over 15 years after her death.
