1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Golden Fleece - Knights: Spanish Branch===
2 CONT ===Louis-Bénigne marquis de Clairvaux baron de Scey, Marquis de Mirebeau et de Listenois de BAUFFREMONT, chevalier de la Toison d'Or (branche espagnole) 1684-1755===
2 CONT * [http://webcache.googleusercontent.com/search?q=cache:dBOtD_SxcF0J:en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bauffremont+&cd=4&hl=en&ct=clnk&lr= '''family - Bauffremont''']
2 CONT * [http://gw4.geneanet.org/pierfit?lang=en;p=louis+benigne;n=de+bauffremont '''Louis Bénigne, Marquis of Bauffremont and Prince de Listenois''']
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Courtenay was a dynasty in medieval France originating from the castle of Courtenay in the Gâtinais (Loiret), in the 10th century. The dynasty descended from Athon, the first lord of Courtenay, apparently himself a de
2 CONC scendant of the Counts of Sens and from Pharamond[citation needed], reputed founder of the French monarchy in 420.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their male-line descent from Louis VI of France induced the impoverished 17th-century members of the Courtenay family to seek to be acknowledged as "Princes du Sang" (Princes of the Blood Royal) and "cousins to the king". Three ki
2 CONC ngs in a row - Henry IV, Louis XIII, and Louis XIV - turned down their petitions. Although the Courtenays protested, their claims to the princely title were never acknowledged by the Paris Court of Accounts.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last male member of the French Courtenays committed suicide in 1727. However his sister married Louis de Bauffremont, and their descendants assumed the dubious title of Prince de Courtenay, which they bear to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre Emmanuel Louis de Bauffremont - Courtenay (1773–1833), son of Louis served under the Bourbons. He fled France during the French Revolution and settled in the United States. He later returned to France and was made a Coun
2 CONC t of the French Empire by Napoleon. Louis XVIII made him a peer of France in 1817, and duke in 1818.
2 CONT
2 CONT The current head of the house, Prince Jacques de Bauffremont-Courtenay, 8th Duke of Bauffremont (born 1922), is the president of the Institut de la Maison de Bourbon, the organisation which supports the claims of Louis Alphonse, D
2 CONC uke of Anjou to the throne of France.[1] His sister Princess Claude de Bauffremont-Courtenay was honorary superintendent of the houses of education of the Legion of Honour.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The head of the house uses the following titles: prince-duc de Bauffremont, prince de Courtenay et de Carency, prince et marquis de Listenois et de Marnay, comte et duc de Pont de Vaux, vicomte de Marigny et de Salins, cousin du r
2 CONC oi. The cadet members of the house use the titles, prince de Bauffremont, prince de Marnay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two members of the family have been members of the '''Order of the Golden Fleece''': 
2 CONT * '''Louis Bénigne, Marquis of Bauffremont and Prince de Listenois (1684–1755) in 1711 
2 CONT * and Charles Roger, Prince of Bauffremont-Listenois (1713–1795) in 1789.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Marquis de Listenois
2 CONT
2 CONT Chevalier , marquis de Clairvaux, de Mirebeau-sur-Bèze, de Listenois etde Clairvaux, comte de Poligny, de Rans et de Dusnes, baron de Traves,de Ruffey, de Montsogeon et de Scey
2 CONT Prisonnier de la Bastille (1717-1717)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Né en 1684/1685 et mort en 1755, chevalier de la Toison d'or (Esp.) en 1711 et chevalier de Saint Louis en 1715. Il (ou plutôt son père) acquit en 1686 après la mort en 1681 du dernier des Gorrevod, Philippe-Eugène, marquis de Mar
2 CONC nay, duc de Pont-de-Vaux, prince du Saint-Empire, les biens et titres de cette famille. Lieutenant général des armées, maréchal de camp. Marquis de Bauffremont, de Listenois, de Clairvaux, de Marnay, vicomte de Marigny, seigneur d
2 CONC e Mirebeau. Il épousa le 5 mars 1712 Hélène de Courtenay dernière descendante de la maison capétienne de Courtenay après la mort de son frère.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comme le constate Saint-Simon, « cette Maison de Courtenay s'est éteinte, vraiment et légitimement de la Maison royale, sans avoir pu jamais être reconnue quoiqu'elle n'en doutât pas, ni le feu Roi non plus. » Il eut pour enfants 
2 CONC Louis qui suit, Charles Roger, Joseph qui suit et Pierre.
