1 NAME Charles /Campbell/
2 GIVN Charles
2 SURN Campbell
2 _MARNM Campbell
1 NAME Col. Hon. Charles Campbell
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1660
2 PLAC Campbeltown, Argyll, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Colonel, MP for Campbelltown (1700), MP and Commissioner for Campbeltown
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Col. Hon. Charles Campbell:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p2087.htm#i20861
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Hon. Charles Campbell [1]
2 CONT *M, #20861, 
2 CONT *b. after 1660
2 CONT *Last Edited=26 Jan 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=1.47%
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Hon. Charles Campbell was born after 1660. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Sophia Lindsay, daughter of Alexander Lindsay, 1st Earl of Balcarres and Lady Anne Mackenzie, in 1678.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Betty Bowles.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll and Lady Mary Stuart.[1] 
2 CONT *In 1685 he was forfeited for participation in his father's rebellion.[1] 
2 CONT *In 1689 he was pardoned, and his attainder reversed.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Campbeltown [Scotland] in 1700.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S8] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 106th edition, 2 volumes (Crans, Switzerland: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 1999), volume 1, page 105. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetag
2 CONC e, 106th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The House of Commons 1690-1715" by Eveline Cruickshanks and Stuart Handley:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.cl/books?id=YM0YnDhXMnYC&pg=PA904&lpg=PA904&dq=Hon.+Charles+Campbell+Campbelltown&source=bl&ots=upysvrdFj-&sig=F2UgF-Avr1EOMSDs-TxlLXiOUI8&hl=es-419&ei=8ZFqTo_gM8ijtgfwz_XiBQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnu
2 CONC m=3&ved=0CCsQ6AEwAg#v=onepage&q&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the former commissioners to the Scottish parliament, there were three potential candidates in 1708, two of whom had sat for Scotland in the first Parliament of Great Britain. But Dougal Stewart (Rothesay) set his sights on the 
2 CONC counties of Buteshire and Perthshrie; and Daniel Campbell (Inveraray) looked to his local interest with Glasgow Burghs. It is conceivable that, with stronger backing, John Muir (Ayr) might have stood. He was a Court supporter, who
2 CONC  earned a secret service payment of 100 pounds for his role in the passage of the Union. Yet no challenge by him is known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The commissioners to the last Scottish parliament for Campbeltown and Irvine were completely out of the running: the former, Hon. Charles Campbell, was an uncle of Argyll, whereas the latter, George Munro, was a local merchant who
2 CONC se lacklustre support for the Union had faded into absenteeism. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The preparations for the 1708 election were therefore relatively straightforward. The Duke of Argyll, having decided to nominate another of his uncles, Hon. James Campbell, obtained the support of the 1st Earl of Bute (d. 1710). C
2 CONC ampbell was made burgess of Rothesay to capacitate him for election as commissioner for the burgh; he thereafter voted in his favor at the district election, where we was assured of the votes of Campbeltown and Inveraray. In a fiv
2 CONC e-burgh district, this united front by three burghs made the election a foregone conclusion, rendering Ayr's role as presiding burgh of no account.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Frasers of Lovat and Strichen by Clan Fraser of Canada, regarding Charles Campell's descendants interaction with Clan Fraser:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.clanfraser.ca/lovat&.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of the first marriage, Thomas Fraser (b. 1691), settled in Campbelltown and married Margaret Campbell, d/o Colonel Charles Campbell by his 2nd wife, Elizabeth Bowles.  Colonel Charles Campbell, whose first wife was Lady So
2 CONC phia Lindsay [d/o Alexander, 1st Earl of Balcarres], was the third son of Archibald, 9th Earl of Argyll (1629-1685) by Lady Mary Stewart [eldest d/o James, 5th Earl of Moray], and brother german to Archibald, 10th Earl & 1st Duke 
2 CONC of Argyll (c1658-1703) and John Campbell of Mamore (c1660-1729) who was the father of Primrose Campbell (1710-1796) who in 1733 married Simon Fraser, 11th Lord Lovat (c1668-1747).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known why his son Thomas by his 1st wife Elizabeth Cockburn, was passed over as the heir of Alexander Fraser, 5th of Strichen, in favour of his sons James and Alexander by his 2nd wife Æmelia Stewart.  James Fraser, 6th 
2 CONC of Strichen, was presumed to have died unmarried or without issue, before 1725.   Alexander Fraser, 7th of Strichen (c1699-1775) was later known as Lord Strichen, a law lord who married in 1731 Lady Anne Campbell (c1695-1736), wid
2 CONC ow of James, 2nd Earl of Bute, and d/o Archibald Campbell, 10th Earl & 1st Duke of Argyll by Elizabeth Tollemache [d/o Elizabeth, Countess of Dysart & Duchess of Lauderdale by her first husband Sir Lionel Tollemache or Talmash].  
2 CONC The Duke and Duchess of Argyll later separated because he was a notorious philanderer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also of interest is the fact that the other daughter of Colonel Charles Campbell, and the sister of Margaret Campbell who married Thomas Fraser [eldest s/o Alexander Fraser, 5th of Strichen and half-brother of Lord Strichen] was C
2 CONC harlotte Campbell who married Thomas Fraser, 6th of Ardachy (1694-1754), Collector of Customs at Campbelltown, who was descended from James, youngest son of Alexander Fraser, 4th Lord Lovat (1527-1557), and brother of Thomas Frase
2 CONC r of Knockie, Tutor of Lovat (1548-1612) who acquired the estate of Strichen by marriage with the widow of Thomas Fraser of Strichen [3rd s/o Sir Alexander Fraser, 7th of Philorth].
2 CONT
2 CONT In Some Fraser Pedigrees (1934) covering the chapter on the Frasers of Ardachy (p. 125), Duncan Warrand refers to Thomas Fraser, then living at Campbelltown, who was brother to Alexander Fraser of Strichen:
2 CONT
2 CONT “This Thomas Fraser married a sister of the wife of Collector Thomas Fraser, and had two daughters, Elizabeth, wife of Lieut. W. Fordyce, and Emilia, wife of W. Abernethy.  Thomas Fraser, Collector of Customs married Charlotte, da
2 CONC ughter of Colonel the Hon. Charles Campbell (third son of Archibald, ninth Earl of Argyll), by his second wife, Elizabeth Bowles”   - per Thomas Fraser-Campbell, Esquire, Lonsdale, Helensburgh.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Charles Campbell (member for Campbeltown)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll and Mary Campbell, Countess of Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT * Husband of Lady Sophia Campbell (Lindsay)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Brother of:
2 CONT # Lady Jane Campbell, Marchioness of Lothian
2 CONT # Anne Maitland (Lady Campbell)
2 CONT # Duke Archibald Campbell, Duke of Argyll
2 CONT # James Campbell
2 CONT # Hon. John Campbell of Mamore, MP for Dunbartonshire
2 CONT
2 CONT * Half brother of Colonel Lord William Campbell
2 CONT
2 CONT ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Charles Campbell was a Scottish soldier and politician of the seventeenth and eighteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT He was the third son of Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll, and his wife Mary, daughter of James Stewart, 4th Earl of Moray. He was in Holland with his father, and attended the meeting of Scottish refugees at Amsterdam on 17 A
2 CONC pril 1685.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same year he accompanied his father on an expedition to Scotland as part of Argyll's Rising, and was sent ashore when they arrived off the coast of Argyllshire to bring intelligence of the disposition of the gentlemen and comm
2 CONC on people. He was then sent ashore a second time to levy men but, falling ill, was seized by the Marquess of Atholl, who by virtue of his justiciary power resolved to hang him, sick or well, at the gate of Inveraray Castle. The Pr
2 CONC ivy Council, however, at the intercession of several ladies, stopped the execution, and ordered him to be carried prisoner to Edinburgh. He was brought before the Justiciary Court on 21 August 1685, forfeited on his confession, an
2 CONC d sentenced to banishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT The forfeiture was rescinded in 1689, and in 1700 he was elected a burgh commissioner in the Parliament of Scotland for Campbeltown on its elevation to a royal burgh. He held the seat up to the time of the Union, a measure which h
2 CONC e steadily supported.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, probably in 1678, Sophia, second daughter of Alexander Lindsay, 1st Earl of Balcarres, his father's step-daughter, who was the means of accomplishing the Earl of Argyll's escape from Edinburgh Castle. Of this marriage 
2 CONC no descendants in the male line exist. Marriage two to Betty Bowles with issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: "Campbell, Duke of Argyll" by Donald C. V. Campbell, in The Scots Peerage, volume I (Edinburgh, 1904) edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 367.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Campbell_(member_for_Campbeltown)]
