1 TITL Field Marshal
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''don't confused with''' [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Keith/6000000179906458939] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Rev-James-Keith/6000000034122409231]!
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Francis_Edward_Keith
2 CONT
2 CONT Field Marshal James Francis Edward Keith was born on 4 June 1696. He died on 14 October 1758 at age 62 at Hochkirck, killed in action. He was the son of William Keith, 8th Earl Marischal and Lady Mary Drummond. He fought in the Ja
2 CONC cobite Rising in 1715. In 1716 he was attainted for participating in the 1715 Uprising. He gained the rank of Colonel between 1719 and 1728 in the service of the Spanish Army.1 He gained the rank of General between 1728 and 1747 i
2 CONC n the service of the Russian Army. He was decorated with the award of Order of Black Eagle of Prussia. He was decorated with the award of Order of St. Andrew of Russia. He gained the rank of Field Marshal in 1747 in the service of
2 CONC  the Prussian Army, under Frederick II. He fought in the Battle of Hochkirch on 14 October 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT Keith died unmarried, but had several children by his mistress, Eva Merthens, a Swedish prisoner captured by him in the Russo-Swedish War. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  http://thepeerage.com/p34645.htm#i346448
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hochkirch The Battle of Hochkirch]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://fi.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Keith
2 CONT
2 CONT Significant Scots James Keith from the Daily Mail, Saturday, January 8, 2000 by Alastair Robertson
2 CONT
2 CONT  Field Marshal James KeithIt has long since ceased to be a talking point for the locals though inquisitive children can occasionally be seen swung high in a parent's grip to get a better look at the tall, grey statue erected at th
2 CONC e heart of Peterhead, Aberdeenshire. Their gaze travelling upwards, past the riding boots, the three-quarter length coat, the just visible hilt of a sword and onto the square-jawed face they behold the image of Field Marshal James
2 CONC  Keith.Hundreds of miles away on the Continent a similar scene is acted out beneath an identical image which stands in Berlin. Constructed at the request of Wilhelm I of Prussia, it is the image of  aman who, in exile from his nat
2 CONC ive land, had led armies over much of Europe, inspired the lust of one of Europe's most powerful women and was to die, as he had lived, by the sword - the one true constant in his extraordinary life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1696, in Invergurie Castle, two miles west of Peterhead, James Francis Edward Keith bore in his name the evidence of the family beliefs that were to force him and his elder brother into exile.Named after James Francis Edwa
2 CONC rd Stuart, the Old Pretender, the second son of the 9th Earl Marischal was a Jacobite by birth and on the encouragement of his mother Lady Mary followed his elder brother George in a passionate pursuit of a cause to which his fami
2 CONC ly were to lose everything.
2 CONT
2 CONT Keith spent his youth and childhood at Inverugie Castle, a more welcoming edifice than the Keiths' imposing ancestral pile at Dunnottar, near the fishing port of Stonehaven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wealthy with lands across north east Scotland, the Keith family had held sawy over Scotland through the centuries but as Keith grew to adulthood their Jacobite politics cast them as rebels not rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was George not James who first threw in his lot with the Jacobites. James, destined for a career in law, was less eager to enter the battlefield and stake everything on the dream of another man's power. But his initial reluctan
2 CONC ce was to turn into a passion which outweighed that of his brother.
