1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == JAMES GLEN OF LONGCROFT ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4 September 1777: Instrument of cognition and sasine in favour of Elizabeth, Countess of Dalhousie, as heir to James Glen of Longcroft, formerly Governor of South Carolina, brother of Andrew Glen, her father, in tenements in Li
2 CONC nlithgow. Registered B.R.S. Linlithgow, 4 September 1777. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD45%2f16%2f2648&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=andrew+glen&ko=p&r=&ro=m&df=&dt=&di=n National Re
2 CONC cords of Scotland, Papers of the Ramsay Family, Earls of Dalhousie, reference GD45/16/2648]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=James_Glen&oldid=1188762187 Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Glen''' (1701 - July 18, 1777) was a Scottish politician in the Province of South Carolina. He was appointed Royal Governor of South Carolina in 1738 but did not arrive in the province until December 17, 1743. He served a
2 CONC s governor until June 1, 1756, and was succeeded by William Henry Lyttelton. On June 21, 1761, Glen returned to Europe and died in London. He is buried in St Michael's Parish Church, Linlithgow, Scotland.  (Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0
2 CONC )
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT * "James Glen." ''Wikipedia'', [https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=James_Glen&oldid=1188762187 revision] of 7 December 2023. < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Glen link] > Accessed 19 April 2025.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''[https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/glen-james/ South Carolina Encyclopedia: James Glen]'''
