1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir James Steuart, Baronet [S.], of Goodtrees and Coltness</strong>, only s. and h., b. 10 Oct. 1713 ; suc. to the Baronetcy and estates in Aug. 1727, but subsequently, in 1756, sold the estate of Goodtrees. By the death,
2 CONC  12 June 1773, of his cousin, Sir Archibald Steuart-Denham, 5th and 6th Baronet [S. 1693 and 1698], he suc. as 7th Baronet to the Baronetcy of Steuart [S. 1698], in which title this Baronetcy then merged, and so continued till bot
2 CONC h became merged or extinct in 1851. See Steuart, Baronetcy [S.], cr. 29 Jan. 1698."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n453/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 435
2 CONT
2 CONT "<strong>Sir James Steuart, afterwards (from 1776) Steuart-Denham, Baronet [S. 1698 and 1705], of Westshield, and Coltness</strong>, co. Lanark, cousin and h. male, being s. and h. of Sir James Steuart, 1st Baronet [S. 1705], of G
2 CONC oodtrees, sometime (1709) Solicitor Gen. [S.], by hie 1st wife Anne, da. of Sir Hew Dalrymple, 1st Baronet [S. 1647], was b. 10 Oct. 1713 ; ed. at North Berwick School, and subsequently at the Univ. of Edinburgh; suc. to the Baron
2 CONC etcy [S. 1705], and the estates of Goodtrees and Coltness (at the age of 14) on the death of his father, in 1727; admitted as Advocate, 1734, and after a prolonged travel abroad, applied himself diligently to his profession. He jo
2 CONC ined the Jacobite rising in 1745, but was pardoned in 1771, and was presented to the King 21 March 1772. He had meanwhile sold the estate of Goodtrees in 1756. By the death, 12 June 1773, of his cousin Sir Archibald Steuart-Denham
2 CONC , Baronet [S. 1693 and 1698], he suc. to the Baronetcy of Steuart [S. 1698], in which his paternal Baronetcy [S. 1705], then merged. Finally by the death in 1776 of his somewhat distant kinsman, Sir William Lockhart-Denham, 6th an
2 CONC d last Baronet [S. 1693], of Westshield, co. Lanark, he, under his will, suc. to that valuable estate, and took the name of Denham after that of Steuart, though he (unlike his predecessor) had no descent whatever from the family o
2 CONC f Denham. He m. 14 Oct. 1743, at Dunrobin, co. Sutherland, Frances, 1st da. of James (Wemyss), Earl Wemyss [S.], by Janet, da. and h. of Col. Francis Charteris. He d. at Edinburgh, Nov. 1780, aged 67. His widow d. at Coltness, 29 
2 CONC June 1789, in her 67th year."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n395/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 377
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * "<em>[http://www.westminster-abbey.org/our-history/people/sir-james-stewart-denham Sir James Stewart Denham]</em>", Westminster Abbey
