1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://books.google.com/books?id=lyENAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA138#v=onepage&q=&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 138 - 141
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ernest, Count Leslie,
2 CONT
2 CONT "Eighteenth Baron of Balquhain.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Count Ernest Leslie, second son of Count George Leslie, sixteenth Baron of Balquhain, by his wife the Honourable Margaret Elphinstone, succeeded his brother James, Count Leslie, seventeenth baron, as eighteenth Baron of Balquhain
2 CONC , 29th January 1731 – he being still a minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT “We have seen that immediately after the death of his father, his mother, the Honourable Margaret Elphinstone, his mother, the Honourable Margaret Elphinstone, expressed her determination to bring up her tow infant sons as Protest
2 CONC ants, which she did accordingly, as is stated in a letter from Count Charles Cajetan Leslie to Sir James Leslie of Pitcaple, 7th July 1740, in which he says:-- ‘The possession of the lands of Balquhain has always been in the hands
2 CONC  of Catholics except the last, the late Ernest Leslie, who was educated, contrary to the inclination of his father George Leslie, in the Protestant religion.’
2 CONT
2 CONT “Count Ernest Leslie was served heir to his brother, Count James, in the lands of Balquhain, 17th July 1739, and established in his own person the proper titles both to the estates of Balquhain and to the lands of Insch and Boddam
2 CONC ; but in the retour of his sevice to the lands of Insch and Boddam he neglected to insert the prohibitive, irritant, and resolutive clauses of the entails of 1692 and 1700, against alienation of the property.  On the 26th July 173
2 CONC 9 he resigned the whole of the lands of Balquhain mentioned in the entail, excepting the lands of Greenhall and Knockenbaird, in favour of himself and the heirs-male of his body; and on the same day he got a charter, under the Gre
2 CONC at Seal, of the lands and barony of Balquhain, sasine followed thereon 16th October 1739, and was registered at Aberdeen 20th October 1739.  Thereafter he granted a procuratory for resigning the lands of Insch and Boddam in favour
2 CONC  of himself and the heirs-maile of his body, with remainder to James Gordon of Cobairdy, his half-brother, the son of his mother by her second husband Sir james Gordon of Park, and the heirs-maile of his body, with remainder to th
2 CONC e sister of the said James Gordon of Cobairdy and the other heirs therein mentioned.  This breach of the entail was made by Count Ernest Leslie at the instigation of his mother, the Honourable Margaret Elphinstone, and the consequ
2 CONC ence was that, on the death of Count Ernest without issue in 1739, James Gordon of Cobairdy took possession of the lands of Insch and Boddam, which were thus lost to the true heirs of the Balquhain estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Count Ernest Leslie is said to have been a thoughtless youth, who, having an opulent fortune unexpectedly thrown upon him by his brother's death, led a wild life, and made too free with his constitution, and thereby undermined hi
2 CONC s health.  He went abroad to travel, and when in Germany it seems he had proposed to visit his relations there; but the German branch of the family were so disobliged at their Scotch relatives, because Count Patrick had prevailed 
2 CONC on his elder son, James Ernest, Count Leslie, to renounce his birth-right to the Balquhain estates, that Count Charles Cajetan Leslie, the son of Count James Ernest, would not receive a visit from his cousin Ernest Leslie of Balqu
2 CONC hain.  Count Ernest Leslie therefore returned to Scotland, and after remaining a short time there he went abroad again.  His intemperate habits brought on disease, and he was obliged to return home again.  He died at Edinburgh 20t
2 CONC h November 1739, aged 25 years.  His remains were buried in Lord Balmerino's burial place in the chapel of Holyrood.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Count Ernest Leslie [ eighteenth Baron of Balquhain ] was the last male heir of the house of Balquhain in Scotland.  The succession to the estates was disputed by Count Charles Cajetan Leslie, and his two sons, Count Joseph Leopo
2 CONC ld and Count Anthony, and Sir James Leslie [ tenth Baron ] of Pitcaple, and was decided in favour of Count Anthony Leslie, nineteen Baron of Balquhain, by a judgement of the House of Peers, 29th April 1742."
