2 CONT
2 CONT Would appreciate any  information about John Fairley's service in the Revolutionary War, it would be welcomed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following excerpt was from a Crab O logy article August 12, 1933 p. 8, in The Daily Herald, Biloxi MS, where a group of Bond descendants were gathering at the BOND-HARVISON cemetery to discuss getting a memorial marker for COL
2 CONC . JOHN BOND Revolutionary Soldier;
2 CONT
2 CONT ....There was another soldier of that war by name of FAIRLEY, whose resting place is in the old RED HILL cemetery near Black Creek in south Perry county. But we seriously doubt if there is anyone living who knows the exact spot wh
2 CONC ere he was buried. However, while the matter of marking the resting place of COL. JOHN BOND is agitating the mind of his progeny, which are almost as numerous as the sands of the seashore, it would do no harm, and indeed be fit an
2 CONC d proper to start an inquiry with a view to locating the Fairley grave which is only about ten miles from Wiggins....
2 CONT
2 CONT The following message is from Gene Phillips who recorded and photographed the cemetery for findagrave -
2 CONT .....Several years ago, the Forest Service cleaned up and surveyed this cemetery. They found in excess of 100 graves identified by ground search radar and metal detectors. They also found off to the side what they think was a slav
2 CONC e cemetery. The cemetery was kept up and was mowed at least once a year. I was first there several years before the Forest Service did their work...
2 CONT Contributor: 
2 CONT Gene Phillips - genephillips@usa.net
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/191113870/john-fairlyfairley Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 12 2023, 3:25:51 UTC''
2 CONT
