1 NOTE {geni:about_me} notę by Natalia Gurko:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Jan Klemens Branicki and Izabela Poniatowska were married on 19 November 1748 in Warsaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT For Branicki, this was his third marriage. At the time of the wedding he was about fifty-nine years old, while Izabela was eighteen. She was the daughter of Stanisław Poniatowski and Konstancja Czartoryska. Her brother was the fut
2 CONC ure king Stanisław August Poniatowski.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage was not simply a private family matter. It was closely connected with the political plans of the powerful Czartoryski and Poniatowski group known as the “Familia”. According to the Polish historian Karol Łopatecki, on
2 CONC e of the main people behind the marriage plans was Izabela’s grandmother, Izabela Elżbieta Czartoryska née Morsztyn. She helped arrange two marriages at the same time: Jan Klemens Branicki with Izabela Poniatowska, and Michał Anto
2 CONC ni Sapieha with Aleksandra Czartoryska.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the memoirs of Stanisław August Poniatowski, Branicki himself was also interested in the marriage. The future king wrote that Branicki was attracted by Izabela’s beauty and charm and began to seek her hand. Łopatecki,
2 CONC  however, points out that there were also important political, social and financial reasons behind the marriage. The union was expected to bring Branicki closer to the Czartoryski and Poniatowski families and at the same time stre
2 CONC ngthen the political position of the “Familia”.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding was arranged very quickly, probably in connection with the Sejm of 1748. One week before the wedding, on 12 November 1748, Jan Klemens Branicki signed a detailed marriage agreement with the bride’s parents, Stanisław P
2 CONC oniatowski and Konstancja Czartoryska. The agreement was secured by a very large penalty of 500,000 Polish złotys.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding ceremony took place on 19 November 1748, Izabela’s name day, at the Church of the Holy Cross in Warsaw. It was a double wedding. At the same ceremony, Michał Antoni Sapieha married Aleksandra Czartoryska. According to 
2 CONC the research of Anna Oleńska cited by Łopatecki, both couples were married by Teodor Czartoryski, Bishop of Poznań and the younger son of Princess Czartoryska.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporary writers also noticed how quickly the marriage had been arranged. Jędrzej Kitowicz wrote that instead of the usual three announcements before the wedding, there was only one. He described the marriage with the words: “
2 CONC nie masz w tym nic osobliwego: ożenił się równy z równą”, which means roughly, “there is nothing unusual about it: an equal married an equal.” For people of the 18th century, this really was a marriage between two members of the h
2 CONC ighest level of the magnate elite.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcin Matuszewicz left a detailed description of the wedding day. The two brides travelled to the ceremony with a large procession and many carriages. Before the wedding they received the blessing of the king and queen. One espec
2 CONC ially interesting detail is that the queen herself placed the wedding crowns on both brides.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the ceremony, a large wedding celebration was held at Princess Czartoryska’s residence. She was Izabela’s grandmother and one of the main people behind the marriage. Later that same day, both brides were formally escorted to
2 CONC  the palaces of their husbands.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding was therefore not only a family celebration. It was also a public display of the wealth, status and political connections of the Czartoryski, Poniatowski, Branicki and Sapieha families.
2 CONT
2 CONT The political importance of the two marriages was clear enough to attract the attention of foreign diplomats. Stanisław August later recalled that the French ambassador, Marquis des Issarts, congratulated the family but said that 
2 CONC he would inform his court that such marriages could give the “Familia” too much influence in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. Stanisław August later believed, however, that the marriages did not bring the political results that
2 CONC  had been expected. He connected this partly with the characters and personal relations of the people involved.
2 CONT
2 CONT The financial side of the marriage was just as impressive as the wedding itself. Only three days after the ceremony, on 22 November 1748, it was officially confirmed that Izabela had received a dowry of 400,000 Polish złotys. Afte
2 CONC r this, the couple gave up any further financial claims against her parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Izabela’s grandmother, Izabela Elżbieta Czartoryska née Morsztyn, also gave her granddaughter wedding property including clothes, jewellery, furniture and other valuable items worth about 100,000 złotys. Jan Klemens himself promis
2 CONC ed his young wife an additional wedding gift of 100,000 złotys. This money became Izabela’s personal property.
2 CONT
2 CONT The financial protection given to Izabela was especially important. Branicki secured her dowry on his estates in Białystok, Choroszcz and Tykocin. He also added another 400,000 złotys as her marriage settlement. This meant that Iz
2 CONC abela’s total financial security amounted to 800,000 złotys. The couple also made an agreement giving each other lifetime rights to their property. Later, Branicki obtained royal privileges for his wife which extended her lifetime
2 CONC  rights to the starosties of Bielsk, Krosno and Mościska.
2 CONT
2 CONT These arrangements were particularly important because of the large age difference between the couple. Branicki was forty-one years older than Izabela. He had already been married twice and had no children. For the Poniatowski fam
2 CONC ily, there was therefore a real possibility that Izabela would outlive her husband and become one of the wealthiest widows in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. This is exactly what later happened.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan Klemens Branicki and Izabela Poniatowska had no children. After her husband’s death in 1771, Izabela continued to control a very large part of his property for many years because of the marriage agreements and the lifetime rig
2 CONC hts she had received.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much less is known about the couple’s personal relationship than about the political and financial side of their marriage. However, Branicki’s final arrangements suggest that by the end of his life his relations with the Poniatows
2 CONC ki family, and perhaps also with Izabela herself, had become difficult. In his will of 1769, Branicki tried to make Izabela responsible for paying his debts from the income of the estates. These obligations also included claims co
2 CONC nnected with the heirs of his first wife, Katarzyna Barbara Radziwiłł. After Branicki’s death, Izabela challenged these conditions. The court decided that the earlier marriage agreements and their mutual lifetime property rights c
2 CONC ould not be cancelled or limited by his later will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karol Łopatecki also points to an interesting detail concerning Izabela’s personal memories. Among the things that were especially important to her were miniature portraits of Andrzej Mokronowski and her father Stanisław Poniatows
2 CONC ki, a ring belonging to her brother King Stanisław August, and a lock of Prince Józef Poniatowski’s hair. No personal keepsakes connected with Jan Klemens Branicki were listed among her most treasured possessions. Łopatecki sees t
2 CONC his as another indirect sign that the relationship between the couple may have become rather cold over the years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding of 19 November 1748 was one of the more important magnate weddings of its time. A young woman from the Poniatowski family married one of the wealthiest magnates of the Crown, while Branicki’s third marriage brought him
2 CONC  even closer to the Czartoryski and Poniatowski families. The grand double wedding at the Church of the Holy Cross, the participation of the king and queen, the large procession and the wedding celebration at Princess Czartoryska’
2 CONC s residence all showed the political importance of the marriage. In the long term, however, the financial results of the marriage proved more important than the political ones. Thanks to the marriage agreements, Izabela kept contr
2 CONC ol of a large part of the Branicki fortune after her husband’s death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on:
2 CONT
2 CONT Karol Łopatecki, „Nie masz w tym nic osobliwego: ożenił się równy z równą”. Ślub Jana Klemensa Branickiego z Izabelą Poniatowską – ujęcie prawnomajątkowe.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________________
2 CONT http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plik:Izabella_Poniatowska_Branicka.jpg
2 CONT
2 CONT Izabela Elżbieta z Poniatowskich Branicka (ur. 26 czerwca 1730 w Warszawie[1][2], zm. 14 lutego 1808 w Białymstoku) – polska szlachcianka, siostra króla Stanisława Augusta[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Życiorys W 1748 roku w wieku lat 18 zawarła związek małżeński z Janem Klemensem Branickim i zamieszkała w Białymstoku. Była trzecią żoną, wówczas 59-letniego, hetmana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mimo młodego wieku, jej rola w rozwoju Białegostoku była niemal równie wielka co małżonka. Była niezwykle energiczną i światłą osobą. Jej właśnie miasto zawdzięcza powstanie pierwszych szkół. Otaczała opieką finansową pensję dla c
2 CONC órek urzędników dworskich, szkołę parafialną, szkołę paziów, Szkołę Podwydziałową Zgromadzenia Akademickiego założoną w 1777 roku przez Komisję Edukacji Narodowej.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zatrudniła na dworze lekarza Michała Clementa, z pochodzenia Węgra oraz twórcę podręczników z dziedziny położnictwa Jakuba Feliksa Michelisa, który założył w Białymstoku Instytut Akuszerii[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT Była też mecenasem kultury i sztuki. Sprowadzała do Białegostoku artystów o światowej sławie z Monachium, Mediolanu czy Londynu.
2 CONT
2 CONT O erudycji Izabelli świadczy również jej pokaźny, jak na tamte czasy, księgozbiór biblioteczny, obejmujący ponad 200 dzieł literatury polskiej i zagranicznej. Włączywszy w to mapy, ryciny, plany architektoniczne, teleskopy, globus
2 CONC y i albumy liczba pozycji sięgała 600 egzemplarzy. A do tego jeszcze zbiór wszystkich edycji KEN Ksiąg Elementarnych wraz z podręcznikami szkolnymi i czasopismami.
2 CONT
2 CONT Po śmierci Jana Klemensa odziedziczyła w całości majątek hetmana. Ponownie wyszła za mąż za generała Andrzeja Mokronowskiego, starostę ciechanowskiego, byłego podkomendnego zmarłego męża. Zmarła w 1808 r. i została pochowana w pod
2 CONC ziemiach bazyliki archikatedralnej Starego Kościoła Farnego w Białymstoku.
