4 CONC . For the following decades, Izabela remained the lifetime holder of Białystok.
4 CONT
4 CONT The situation changed greatly after the Third Partition of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1795. Podlasie, together with Białystok, became part of the Kingdom of Prussia and was included in the new province of New East Pruss
4 CONC ia. Białystok became an administrative centre of the Prussian authorities, but at the same time it remained the private town of Izabela Branicka. This created an unusual situation: the town was within the Kingdom of Prussia and wa
4 CONC s a centre of Prussian administration, while the rights to it still belonged to a Polish noblewoman.
4 CONT
4 CONT This led to many disputes between Izabela and the Prussian administration. Branicka defended her rights to the town and in some cases appealed directly to the Prussian king Frederick William III. The main Prussian administrative a
4 CONC uthority in Białystok was the War and Domains Chamber (Kamera Wojny i Domen).
4 CONT
4 CONT On 1 March 1802, Izabela Branicka, acting as the lifetime holder of Białystok, signed a lease agreement with the War and Domains Chamber of the Białystok Department. Under the agreement, Białystok was leased to the Prussian admini
4 CONC stration for 412,000 Polish złoty a year, equal to 7,000 thalers. This brought to an end the long period when Izabela directly managed the town.
4 CONT
4 CONT Soon afterwards, the question of ownership was also settled. According to Jan Klemens Branicki’s will, the Potocki family was to receive Białystok after Izabela’s death. With her consent, they sold their rights to the town, togeth
4 CONC er with the surrounding estates, to the Prussian king. Documents concerning this transaction from April 1802 have survived in the archives.
4 CONT
4 CONT The document shown here is part of the Białystok lease agreement of 1 March 1802. It is especially important for the biography of Izabela Branicka because it shows her legal position in the last years of her life. More than thirty
4 CONC  years after her husband’s death, she was still described in official documents as the lifetime holder of Białystok and personally took part in settling relations with the new Prussian authorities.
4 CONT
4 CONT Source: Archiwum Państwowe w Białymstoku, Kamera Wojny i Domen w Białymstoku 1796-1807, sygn. 2318, k. 271-283, Akt dzierżawy Białegostoku, 1 marca 1802 r.
