1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.anamericanfamilyhistory.com/Douglas%20Familly/DouglasBenjamin1685.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Benjamin Douglas was born about 1685 in Charles County, Maryland. His parents were John Douglas and Catherine Heard. He swore in court that he was 50 years old in 1737, He was a planter in southern Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Elizabeth Land about 1707. Their children and life together are described in detail in the section on Benjamin and Elizabeth Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1714, he and his brother, Joseph, were named in their brother-in-law, Charles Brandt's, will as his nearest relative.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.anamericanfamilyhistory.com/Douglas%20Familly/DouglasBenjaminElizabeth.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Benjamin Douglas and Elizabeth Land married about 1707. They lived in Charles County, Maryland where they owned Blythswood Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1714 Benjamin sold his share in Cold Spring Manor to Francis Collier.
2 CONT
2 CONT A letter dated March 24, 1717/18 from Thomas Pallin to Walter Story mentioned that Mr. and Mrs. Benjamin Douglas were bound for Jamaica, then Liverpoole, and then Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Benjamin Douglas was born about 1707.
2 CONT # John Douglas was born about 1709.
2 CONT # Charles Douglas was born in 1714.
2 CONT # Mary Douglas Wilson was born about 1718.
2 CONT # Joseph Douglas was born about 1721.
2 CONT # Penelope Douglas was born about 1724.
2 CONT # Sarah Douglas Stone was born in 1727.
2 CONT # Thomas Douglas
2 CONT # Jesse Douglas was born in 1733.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Douglas married in 1734 in Charles County. Mary Douglas married in 1740 in Charles County.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1738 George Thomas made him the guardian of his son, Benjamin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin and Elizabeth both died about 1749.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died before February 13, 1749/50 at Causine's Manor [also known as Causin's Manor or Mount Air], Charles County, Maryland. Causine's Manor was originally 1,000 acres granted to Nicholas Causine It was in Port Tobacco Lower Hund
2 CONC red, five miles from the town of Portobacco.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin's estate was probated on October 13, 1749. His wife, Elizabeth. was the executrix. She received her dower right of one third the estate. He left his son, John, Blythswood and he left his son, Benjamin, part of Causine's M
2 CONC anor with the stipulation that his wife, Elizabeth, could use the land while she remained a widow. He passed on to his daughter, Penelope, an enslaved woman named, Jane. The remainder of the estate went to his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tax list of 1783 shows that Richard Land Douglas was taxed for 157 acres of Causine's Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1716 he witnessed the will of Elizabeth Howard. Elizabeth was his sister's, sister-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1734 he witnessed the will of his sister's, brother-in-law, William Stevens Howard.
2 CONT
2 CONT He signed his will on May 11, 1743.
