1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == LADY CATHERINE LINDSAY (aka LADY KATHARINE LINDSAY) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft#page/38/mode/1up Biographical Summary by the Scots Peerage] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Catherine, eldest daughter and coheir, married to Lieutenant John Wemyss of General Oglethorpe's regiment, afterwards Lieutenant-Governor of Edinburgh Castle. She died s. p. 28 February 1768 at Edinburgh, and he at the same pla
2 CONC ce in January 1786.[http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft#page/38/mode/1up The Scots Peerage III: pp. 38-9]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Heirs of Line == 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lady Catherine Lindsay and her younger sister Lady Mary Lindsay were the heirs of line of the deceased brother, John, twentieth Earl of Crawford, who died on 24 December 1749. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatal
2 CONC ogue/details.aspx?reference=GD20%2f1%2f887&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=earl+of+craufurd&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1700&dt=1770&di=n NRS: GD20/1/887] Heirs of line are people who succeed by force of law to the property of a deceased person. [
2 CONC https://www.dsl.ac.uk/entry/snd/heir Dictionaries of the Scots Language]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lady Catherine Lindsay is reported to have died on 28 February 1768. [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft#page/38/mode/1up The Scots Peerage III: pp. 38-9] This conflicts with the obituary published in the Annu
2 CONC al Register, which claims that she died at Edinburgh on the day following, that is 1 March 1768. [https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=e3YEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA214&lpg=PA214&dq=John+Wemyss,+Lieutenant+Governor+of+Edinburgh+Castle&source=b
2 CONC l&ots=U9hIE2xgw4&sig=wbRPs-Tf5yvhK8-52xVHjvh8YwA&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjbl-Gik9POAhWgHsAKHRJjBMkQ6AEIHjAB#v=onepage&q=John%20Wemyss%2C%20Lieutenant%20Governor%20of%20Edinburgh%20Castle&f=false The Annual Register]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the Annual Register ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1 March 1768:" Lady Catherine Wemyss, lady of Lieutenant Governor John Wemyss, at Edinburgh Castle."
2 CONT [https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=e3YEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA214&lpg=PA214&dq=John+Wemyss,+Lieutenant+Governor+of+Edinburgh+Castle&source=bl&ots=U9hIE2xgw4&sig=wbRPs-Tf5yvhK8-52xVHjvh8YwA&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjbl-Gik9POAhWgHsAKHRJjBMkQ
2 CONC 6AEIHjAB#v=onepage&q=John%20Wemyss%2C%20Lieutenant%20Governor%20of%20Edinburgh%20Castle&f=false The Annual Register]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''12 February 1752: Decree of adjudication at the instance of Patrick Lindsay junior and William Douglas, merchants in Edinburgh, against Lady Katharine Lindsay, eldest sister of deceased John, Earl of Crawfurd, and spouse of Lie
2 CONC utenant John Wemyss of General Oglethrop's late Regt, and Lady Mary Lindsay, sister of the said deceased earl and spouse of [-] Campbell of Glensaddell, in respect of the lands of Cassindillie and many others in the sheriffdom of 
2 CONC Fife, dated 12 February 1752, with 18 documents preceding or following thereon. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD20%2f1%2f828&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=Campbell+of+Glensaddell&ko=p&r
