1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Fulke_Greville Lieutenant-Colonel Robert Fulke Greville] FRS MP (3 February 1751 – 27 April 1824) was a British Member of Parliament (MP) and courtier.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Francis Greville, 1st Earl of Warwick and Elizabeth Hamilton, and brother to Charles Francis Greville, he was educated at Edinburgh University. He was commissioned as a Cornet in the 10th Dragoons in 1768, and promoted 
2 CONC to Lieutenant in 1772; he became a Captain in the 1st Foot Guards in 1775 and Lieutenant-Colonel in 1777. He was Member of Parliament for Warwick from 1774 to 1780, supporting the Tory government of Lord North, and for New Windsor
2 CONC  from 1796 to 1806.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greville served as Equerry to King George III from 1781 to 1797 and Groom of the Bedchamber from 1800 to 1818. His diaries give remarkable insight into the King's illness and recovery from porphyria. They also record the King's 17
2 CONC 94 season at Weymouth in considerable detail. He hence appears as a character in the play The Madness of George III and its film adaptation, played in the latter by Rupert Graves.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 October 1797, he married Louisa Murray, 2nd Countess of Mansfield and widow of David Murray. His son and namesake attempted to make improvements to the port of Milford Haven which his uncle, Charles Francis Greville, had fou
2 CONC nded.
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'Robert Fulke Greville', Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 12 May 2012, 11:37 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Robert_Fulke_Greville&oldid=492186899> [accessed 5 September 2013]
