3 CTRY Switzerland
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.jamesboswell.info/biography/emet-ulla-de-froment
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter of a Turkish chief-janissary, Sar Aly Oda Bachy, Emettulla was adopted after the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Ochakov_(1737) Siege of Otchakov] (1737) by George Keith, Lord Marischal. In 1763 she converted 
2 CONC to Christianity, was baptized Marie Emeté, and married Denis-Daniel de Froment, a captain in the Sardinian army, but the marriage was dissolved in 1765, and Emet-Ulla lived with her adopted father until his death in 1778.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life with Boswell
2 CONT
2 CONT Boswell first met her on June 13, 1764, when she arrived in Utrecht together with Lord Marischal. Boswell was to accompany them to Berlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although at first, she seemed to dislike Boswell, they quickly seemed to find some pleasance in each other's company. On the second day after leaving Utrecht, Boswell wrote of her ''“I find Madame de Froment very lively, although 
2 CONC she has an indolence, or, as the French say, a ‘nonchlance’, that is terrible. She does not dress. Scarcely even will she speak. I talked with her in rather too galant a strain.”'' On June 27 they discovered that they were both su
2 CONC bject to the occasional bout of serious depression. She said that ''“[e]xercise and dissipation, with moderate enjoyment, were the only remedies”.''
2 CONT
2 CONT They dined tête-à-tête on several occasions, as the Earl Marischal was often otherwise engaged due to his status.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.davidalston.info/documents/paintings-and-portraits/emet-ullah-and-the-earl-marischal-of-scotland-v1.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT … As early as 1757 the Earl Marischal had sought to make provision for Emet Ullah in the event of his death. His plan was to have the upper floor of the chateau at Colombier (Neuchâtel) repaired and to rent and replanted the nearb
2 CONC y orchard. She was to have the use of both for the remainder of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl died in 1778 leaving Emet Ullah all the money in his possession and his houses in Boulogne and Potsdam. She lived for some time in Potsdam and Berlin, corresponding with Hugh Seton of Touch, the trustee of the funds left 
2 CONC to her. Seton, born Hugh Smith, and his wife and relations were among the friends she had made during her brief stay in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her English was poor and she wrote in French.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the mid- or late-1780s Emet Ullah returned to Neuchâtel to live in a small villa on the shores of of the lake, near the lavishly magnificent Palais du Peyrou, built in 1768. The villa was still there in 1915 – a low, two-story,
2 CONC  green-shuttered white house, whose garden once, but no longer, ran down to the lake. At either end of the villa were two summer houses, where she would sit playing the flageolet. Does it survive?
2 CONT
2 CONT Emet Ullah died there in 1820, in her nineties. Her portrait, in blue satin with pearls in her hair, hung in Carberry Castle until the building was gifted to the Church of Scotland in 1961. It is now an hotel (offering a special ‘
2 CONC Mary Queen of Scots’ package). I have been unable to locate the portrait.
2 CONT
2 CONT In her biography of Frederick the Great (1970), Nancy Mitford dismissed Emet Ulla snootily with the words, ‘Her contemporaries all agree she was a tremendous bore.’ This was far from the truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.davidalston.info/documents/paintings-and-portraits/mocho-of-guinea-and-the-earl-marischal-of-scotland-v5.pdf (page 9)
2 CONT
2 CONT The trauma of enslavement
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl  Marshal’s household – Emet Ullah, Ibrahim, Stepan and Mocho – were described by him as his children and all were provided for in old age. According to Voltaire they proved ‘that men can live very well together while thin
2 CONC king differently’. Rousseau, who had written influentially on freedom and equality, regarded them as his brothers and sisters. And they were known to both David Hume and Adam Ferguson. It would be tempting to see Mocho as having e
2 CONC xperienced, or at least, glimpsed an enlightened age of liberty, equality and fraternity.
2 CONT
2 CONT But all four of the Earl’s ‘children’ had been enslaved as children and lived with the effects that trauma. We know most of Emet Ullah. James Boswell recognised that, like him, she suffered from mood swings and periods of deep dep
2 CONC ression, so that, in her words, ‘all things appeared one undistinguished black mass’. The term used in the eighteenth century to describe her condition was ‘hypochondria’. Her marriage was short lived. She converted to Christianit
2 CONC y but ‘‘still believed that the Great Prophet was sent from God’. She was rootless and at home nowhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Denis DE FROMENT
2 CONT France, Names & Stories in Newspapers
2 CONT Text:	...conservé jusqu'à la fin de sa vie les restes d'une grande beauté. La Révolution avait mis un terme aux persécutions religieuses et Denisde Froment ayant perdu ses cousines, vint mourir à Uzès en 1810. C'est lui qui rappor
2 CONC ta les portraits de ses deux oncles de Froment. ...
2 CONT Death:	1810 - Uzès, France
2 CONT Newspaper:	Bulletin - Société de l'histoire du protestantisme français
2 CONT Publication:	1916 - Paris, Paris, Île-de-France, France
2 CONT Article summary:	Denis Daniel obtient la naturalisation en 1763 et épouse Emmetah-Ulla, dont il divorce en 1765.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
