1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  THOMAS ASKEY
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 1727 in County Antrim, Northern Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 24 NOV 1807 in Howard, Centre County, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: 1807 Lick Run Presbyterian Church Cemetery, Jacksonville, Centre County, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration: BET. 1745 - 1750 from County Antrim, Northern Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Service: 1760 French and Indian Wars, Lieutenant
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Service: Maryland Revolutionary Records; pension claims/ms, bounty land applications, marriages of soldiers by Harry Wright Newman, Washington, D.C., 1938
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Service: BET. 1777 - 1782 Revolutionary War, Captain, 1st Class, 1st Co., 1st Btn., Cumberland County Militia. DAR# [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_descendants/?action=list&MyPrimary_Seqn=389279&MyLine
2 CONC ageCount=1&Control_Min_Seqn=389279 A037117]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pennsylvania Archives
2 CONT
2 CONT  By Pennsylvania Secretary of the Commonwealth, Pennsylvania Dept. of Public Instruction, Pennsylvania State Library
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=id8LAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA619&dq=thomas+askey&lr=&ei=k30oSomKLYKQNqHi8ZYL#PPA611,M1
2 CONT
2 CONT h/o Elizabeth (Baker) Askey
2 CONT
2 CONT Aka Betsy b: 18 APR 1745 in Germantown, Path Valley, Fannett Township, Cumberland County
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 12 JUN 1764 in Carlisle, Cumberland, Pennsylvania
2 CONT
2 CONT Roll Call recorded 1781
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=gxYUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA410&dq=col+thomas+askey&ei=ZXUoSuXbNpCOMoy55Z8L#PPA408,M1
2 CONT
2 CONT Wrought in Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT (Content provided by Donald Askey in the Askey-Gross Family Tree, published by Sarah Marie Askey-Hugar, 1973.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Part 1
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the Scotch-Irish Presbyterian immigrants was named Thomas Erskine, born 1727 [1] in County Antrim, Northern Ireland. Erskine settled in Path Valley, Pennsylvania, but how old he was at that time is unrecorded. It is possibl
2 CONC e that he was yet a youngster and came over with his parents. During the French and Indian War (1754-1763) Erskine [2] 2 was listed as a Lieutenant from Cumberland County, and on July 15, l763, he was commissioned an Ensign in the
2 CONC  Pennsylvania Regiment which was then under the command of John Penn. In effect what the war did was to help open up new land to the settlers, in particular the Scotch-Irish, the backwoodsmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The prospect of prosperity on this inexpensive and unsettled land was a new hope for the Scotch-Irish. It was probably in light of this prospect that Askey decided to marry in June, l764, a young nineteen-year-old girl by the name
2 CONC  of Elizabeth Baker, the daughter of Col. Robert Baker, a Franco-Cambrian by descent. Early in l769, shortly after the lands were officially opened, Ensign Askey, along with some of his companions on the 1764 expedition against th
2 CONC e Indians at Fort Duquesne, petitioned for some of this available land in remuneration for their services during the French and Indian War. They all met at Fort Augusta, Sunbury, later to divide up among themselves the 24,000 acre
2 CONC s of land they were granted along the West branch of the Susquehanna. As stated in their petition, their object was "to embody themselves on some good land at some distance from the inhabited part of the Province, where by their i
2 CONC ndustry they might procure a comfortable subsistence for themselves and by their arms, union and increase become a powerful barrier to the Province." As a part of his share of the land, on which he later settled; Askey received a 
2 CONC 288.5-acre tract, the last share of the Bald Eagle Tract, the uppermost of the officers' survey, and the most westerly according to the map of the original surveys. This area was named after Bald Eagle, a Seneca, one of the more n
2 CONC otorious savage Indian leaders in the Alleghenies.
2 CONT
2 CONT A firm advocate of freedom, like his displaced countrymen from Scotland, Askey joined the Continental Army when the British began threatening the cause of liberty; and in 1777 he was raised to the rank of Captain for the remainder
2 CONC  of the Revolutionary War (1775-1781). During the last year of the war Capt. Askey commanded a couple of his own companies, the First Company (from Fannett Township, Cumberland County) of the 1st Battalion under Col. Dunlap and la
2 CONC ter the Eighth Company of the 6th Battalion. It is difficult to tell how much fighting Askey really did or where exactly he may have fought.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, Askey stayed in the service until his discharge in June l784, now about fifty-seven years of age. He then decided to settle down with his wife and children on some of the land he acquired in Bald Eagle Valley before
2 CONC  the war. Askey's property consisted of part of what is now Northumberland County, part of Union County; the site of Mifflinburg, and part north of Bald Eagle Creek opposite the village of Howard. Askey was one of the only two off
2 CONC icers among the earliest settlers who complied with the original stipulation from the proprietors to settle upon their grants or military fiefs in order to protect the frontier.
2 CONT
2 CONT (In 1790, the First Census of the U.S. uncovered a couple more Askey’s, all of whom save one were probably not directly related to Thomas. There was a Pere in Maryland, a William in Massachusetts and a Zachariah in Virginia. There
2 CONC  was also a James Askie in Sunbury, Pennsylvania. Again, it is not safe to rely on the orthographical practices to establish any blood relationships among these Askey’s. But in Cumberland County, where Thomas had previously reside
2 CONC d, a man by the name of Alexander Askey settled at Shippensburg in June 1730. It is tempting to try to relate Alexander and Thomas, but any such relationship is unrecorded. One might recall that the name Alexander was not uncommon
2 CONC  among the Scottish Erskine’s. None of the available sources however indicate or even imply that Thomas Askey came to America with any relatives.)
2 CONT
2 CONT After settling down Askey began to take an active part in his community, viz. Bald Eagle of Mifflin County which before 1789 was known as Upper Bald Eagle of Northumberland County. In 1791, he held the office of Overseer of the Po
2 CONC or, 1793 and 1794 Constable and 1797 Fence-Viewer. When Centre Township was first formed in April 1799 Askey was made Supervisor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1807, Askey died in a log cabin on a farm north of Howard and was buried in the Lick Run Presbyterian Cemetery in Jacksonville. A stone still marks his grave there and his name also appears on the soldiers' memori
2 CONC al in front of the Court House in Bellefonte. His wife Elizabeth survived him by almost twenty-three years and now rests beside her husband in Jacksonville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Part II
2 CONT
2 CONT In the course of Thomas Askey's military career, pioneer activities, and public life he and his wife managed to raise ten children. A large family was not only inexpensive to support but also proved a distinct asset in running a f
2 CONC rontier farm. In addition to the four girls, Margaret, Anne, Rebecca, and Catharine, Askey had six boys probably in this order: James, Robert (b.1770), William, John (1772), Samuel (1776), and David (1783). Except their spouses' n
2 CONC ames, very little, virtually nothing, is known about James, William, David, or their four sisters. Robert and John both wed daughters of Col. William Evans, a Scotch-Irish settler. Robert married Polly Evans; they also had about t
2 CONC en children. It is told that Robert fought against the Indians in Ohio, but he lived and died in Curwensville, Clearfield County. John married Polly's younger sister Elizabeth or Betsey, they stayed on at the old homestead after T
2 CONC homas' death and raised eleven children there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel is indeed the most written and talked about Askey of the bunch. Leaving Howard in 1818 Samuel crossed a couple hills over the Indian path to settle in Snow Shoe where he could engage in his favorite occupation, hunting. He 
2 CONC was compared by one historian to Daniel Boone and Davy Crockett, one of the greatest pioneer hunters of Pennsylvania. Askey killed hundreds of panthers, wolves, deer, and bears—thousands of pounds of meat. The following are some o
2 CONC f the yarns Samuel himself once spun.
2 CONT
2 CONT While walking through the woods one day with a minister friend of his Samuel said:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Stop a moment while I tell you an amusing and ridiculous scrape I had with a big buck near yonder tree. It was a long shot; but when the gun cracked he fell flat in his tracks. Supposing it to be a dead shot, I approached him wit
2 CONC hout any fear of trouble. I had on a loose blouse with wide sleeves. I stooped over him, and with my left hand took hold of a horn, and with the right was about to apply the knife to his throat, when to my utter dismay and astonis
2 CONC hment, he jumped up with great violence, a prong of one of his horns went through the blouse, and he gave me a tremendous whirl, in doing which he relieved me of the blouse and departed, leaving me in my shirt sleeves to look afte
2 CONC r him going through the brush at the top of his, speed, with the blouse spread out to the wind like the flag of a conquering hero. I did not begrudge him the blouse, because I thought if I had not been relieved of it, the result w
2 CONC ould have been more serious.
2 CONT
2 CONT “A good tracking snow had fallen. I started with my dog and came on a fresh track between the Moshannon’s. I tied my dog to my body so as to keep him with me until the proper time to let him loose. The trail led to a high point on
2 CONC  the brow of the Big Moshannon Mountain, which is very steep. Passing in front of a large projecting rock, a huge panther jumped from under upon the dog, and panther, dog and myself went down the hill, sometimes the panther upperm
2 CONC ost, sometimes the dog and sometimes myself. The cord being tied with a slipknot, I succeeded in effecting a separation. The dog and panther went to the foot of the hill, when the panther ran up a tree. I looked around for my gun,
2 CONC  which I had let go, and finding it all right I went down, and having a fair shot, the panther fell dead to the ground. It was a female, and with indications of having young ones. I returned to the rock. The cavity was large enoug
2 CONC h to admit my entrance, and r brought out four young panthers as large as full-grown cats. After handling them for a short time, they fondled on me like young kittens. It was a hard day’s work, but the premiums I received for the 
2 CONC scalps rewarded me, but did not compensate me for the disabling of my dog,--he was of no use to me afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Col. John Holt and myself were returning from the Big Moshannon Licks. We had a horse loaded with venison and, when near the top of the hill, on the north side of Little Moshannon on the Indian path, my dog commenced barking on o
2 CONC ur left at or near the foot of the hill. I left Holt with the horse and on my route to the dog encountered large rocks. I crossed over one and jumped down some four or five feet at the lower side and lit upon ground so thickly cov
2 CONC ered for some distance all around with rattlesnakes, that every step I made I tramped upon them. It seemed to me like tramping over beef entrails on a butchering day. They were all moving toward a cavity under the rock. You may be
2 CONC  sure I handled myself lively. I escaped from them without injury, excepting a brief sickness of stomach, occasioned by the stench which arose from the snakes. I did not stop to count nor to kill, and have no desire ever to come a
2 CONC cross another rattlesnake den; some were of great length and thickness with many rattles. I soon found my dog, with a panther, on a tree which I shot, and returned with it to Holt, leaving the snake den off my route."
2 CONT
2 CONT This rugged backwoodsman went to his grave tattooed with the scars he incurred from his encounters with the wild denizens of the forest. Samuel, along with other Askey relatives, was interred in the Askey Cemetery in Gillentown, S
2 CONC now Shoe Township. A very short distance east down the road, across from the cemetery and on the other side, lies the foundation of the old Askey Schoolhouse which stood there for many years during the nineteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT Linn, John Blair, History of Centre and Clinton Counties. Philadelphia, 1883. Pages 10, 22, 23, 27, 29, 33,34,39, 40, 48, 57, 70, 86, 93, 105, 1009, 118, 139, 227, 271, 326, 327, 345, 348, 420, 422, 423, 424, 425, 426, 428, 468, 4
2 CONC 70, 472, 479, 539, Note illustrated Map of the Officers’ Survey 1769 by Charles Lukens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pennsylvania Archives:
2 CONT
