2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT Vol. XXIV, page 629.
2 CONT
2 CONT This good collection is largely a compilation of trivial information. The Pennsylvania State Museum in Harrisburg still has the letter Thomas Askins wrote to the members of the Council of Safety (1776-1777) requesting a Captain's 
2 CONC commission in any battalion. The museum also holds two other documents bearing Thomas' signature and verifying some of the information mentioned above. Centre Democrat, Bellefonte, March 9, 1909. The Lock Haven Express, Lock Haven
2 CONC , November 12, 1953.
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] This date may be contested, but of the various dates given in different sources 1727 is the mode and seems to be the most likely and acceptable.
2 CONT
2 CONT [2] According to family tradition, this name was changed to Askey in one or another way. "Capt. Askey wrote his name 'Thomas Erskine' but ... his name is carried upon the military records as Askey, and that is the spelling now use
2 CONC d by the family ..." (Linn's History page 326). Therefore in this case the name Askey came from Erskine, which is linguistically sound enough when one considers that America was a melting pot of diverse languages and dialects each
2 CONC  of which could not hope to be properly preserved in its peculiar orthography. That is to say that Thomas Erskine's pronunciation of his own surname may more properly have been rendered by his listeners as Askins, Asky, Ackey, or 
2 CONC Askey, as the case may be. Askey however does not in all instances come from Erskine. The form Askey may also be derived from Askie, Askew, or the suchlike, but where this is so Askey is an English name; and not Scottish as Thomas
2 CONC ' name is. Thomas Erskine could not by any stretch of the imagination have come originally from England. This should be borne in mind when one wishes to look up the Askey (Erskine) coat-of-arms. Askey will be used in place of Ersk
2 CONC ine henceforth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please make any corrections you feel are required to this page. If you have any comments or suggestions on the Askey & Erskine Wetpaint site, please email me at john_d_askey@yahoo.com. 
2 CONT
2 CONT lieutenant in the French Indian War, 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Founding Fathers of the United States are the political leaders who signed the Declaration of Independence or otherwise participated in the American Revolution as leaders of the Patriots, or who participated in drafting the Un
2 CONC ited States Constitution eleven years later. During the American Revolutionary War, the Founders were opposed by the Loyalists who supported the British monarchy and opposed independence (though most Loyalists remained in the U.S.
2 CONC  after 1783 and supported the new government).[2] Some authors draw a distinction between the Founders, who signed the Declaration of Independence in 1776 or participated in the Revolution, and the Framers, who drafted the United 
2 CONC States Constitution
2 CONT
