1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT <strong>John Erskine of Carnock</strong>, born 2nd June 1721, eldest son of John E. of Carnock, Professor of Scots Law, Edinburgh Univ., and Margaret, daugh. of the Hon. James Melvill of Balgarvie ; educated at Cupar-Fife, and Hig
2 CONC h School and Univ. of Edinburgh ; originally intended for the law, but studied divinity and was licen. by Presb. of Dunblane 16th Aug. 1743 ; ord. to Kirkintilloch 31st May 1744; trans, to Culross 21st Feb. 1753; trans, to New Gre
2 CONC yfriars 15th June 1758 ; D.D. (Glasgow 1766); trans, and adm. 9th July 1767 ; died 19th Jan. 1803. He was a classical scholar of distinction ; for many years leader of the evangelical party in the General Assembly and a master of 
2 CONC the preacher's art. He warmly supported a proposal in favour of foreign missions, which was opposed by Hamilton of Gladsmuir and the moderate party generally. His opening words, when replying to Hamilton, became famous in the hist
2 CONC ory of the mission cause. Pointing to a Bible which lay on the table, and of which he intended to make use, and employing an expressive Scottish idiom, he said, " Moderator, rax me that Bible." The best pen-portrait of him is cont
2 CONC ained in Guy Mannering (chap. 37). He marr. 15th June 1746, the Hon. Christian Mackay (died 20th May 1810), fourth daugh. of George, Lord Reay, and had issue — 
2 CONT * John, born 30th Sept. 1747, died June 1776 ; 
2 CONT * George, born 21st Aug. 1748, died in Bengal in autumn 1770 ; 
2 CONT * Mary, born 19th Feb. 1750 (marr. 29th Oct. 1773, Charles Stuart of Dunearn), died 15th April 1817 ; 
2 CONT * Margaret, born 17th Sept. 1751, died 5th Feb. 1795 ; Ann, born 30th Dec. 1752, died 18th May 1820 ; 
2 CONT * Alexander, born 28th July 1754, died 25th Feb. 1760; 
2 CONT * Robert, born 4th May 1756, died 9th April 1784; 
2 CONT * Charles, born 20th Oct. 1757, died 28th June 1782 ; 
2 CONT * Christian, born 28th June 1759; 
2 CONT * Hew, born 27th Jan., died 25th Sept. 1763 ; 
2 CONT * James Bruce, born 8th Sept. 1764, died 11th Oct. 1771 ; 
2 CONT * Harriet, born 11th Oct. 1766, died 10th Oct. 1771 ; 
2 CONT * David of Carnock, born 18th April 1770, died 16th March 1838.
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/fastiecclesiaesc01scot#page/46/mode/2up Fasti ecclesiae scoticanae: the succession of ministers in the Church of Scotland from the reformation], Vol. I, page 47
2 CONT
2 CONT ====================
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine (theologian)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Erskine (1721–1803), the Scottish theologian, was born near Dunfermline at Carnock,[citation needed] on 2 June 1721. His father was the great Scottish jurist John Erskine of Carnock and his grandfather was Colonel John Erskin
2 CONC e of Cardross who had been in William of Orange's army when it invaded England in the Glorious Revolution of 1688.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [hide] 
2 CONT * 1 Life
2 CONT * 2 Bibliography
2 CONT * 2.1 Works by John Erskine 2.1.1 Other
2 CONT * 2.2 References
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended school in Cupar then the High School of Edinburgh followed by an M.A. at Edinburgh University. He then studied law for a time but quickly changed course for a religious career and was eventually licensed by the Presbyt
2 CONC ery of Dunblane 16 August 1743. He was ordained parish minister of Kirkintilloch, near Glasgow, on 31 May 1744 and subsequently translated to Culross, in Fife on 21 February (1753). On 15 June 1758 he took up position in New Greyf
2 CONC riars Church, Edinburgh. Then, on 9 July 1767 he was called to Old Greyfriars Church taking up position in 1768, where he became the colleague of Principal Robertson, the historian. Here he remained until his death, which took pla
2 CONC ce on 19 January 1803. Erskine's writings consist chiefly of controversial pamphlets on theological subjects. His sermons were clear, vigorous expositions of a moderate Calvinism, in which metaphysical argument and practical moral
2 CONC ity are happily blended. He was very supportive of foreign missions, which he supported in the General Assembly - famously beginning a response to an opponent of such missions "Moderator, rax me (that is, hand me) that bible". His
2 CONC  books, etc., show him to have been very prolific and to have been keenly interested in the politics of the day. He was against Roman Catholic Emancipation and on the side of the American Colonies in their dispute with King George
2 CONC  III. (Scottish merchants had several days' advantage in crossing the Atlantic, and colonial harbours were often crowded with Glasgow ships). He received the degree of Doctor of Divinity from Glasgow University in 1766.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was well connected to the great religious events of the time - for example the religious revivals in Scotland, (see The 'Cambuslang Wark'), England (Methodism)and America (The Great Awakening). He obviously thought he was livin
2 CONC g in eventful times. In church politics he was the leader of the evangelical party, often seen as opponents of the Moderate Party, though his friendship with that Party's great leader, Principal Robertson belies this simple opposi
2 CONC tion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was well loved and respected and was asked to preach throughout the country. He married on 15 June 1746, the Hon. Christian Mackay (who died 20 May 1810), the fourth daughter of George Mackay, 3rd Lord Reay. They had a large fa
2 CONC mily, eight sons and four daughters, many of whom died young - at least one in Bengal. His surviving heir was David Erskine of Carnock, born 18 April 1770, died 16 March 1838
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Works by John Erskine[edit]
2 CONT * Erskine, John A fair and impartial account of the debate in the Synod of Glasgow and Air, Sixth October 1748, anent employing Mr. Whitefield. Edinburgh 1748
2 CONT * Erskine, John An attempt to promote the frequent dispensing of the Lord’s Supper Kilmarnock Printed and sold by J. Wilson ..., 1783
2 CONT * Erskine, John Considerations on the spirit of popery, and the intended bill for the relief of papists in Scotland. 1778
2 CONT * Erskine, John Discourses preached on several occasions 1801
2 CONT * Erskine, John Discourses preached on several occasions. 1798
2 CONT * Erskine, John Education of poor children recommended: : a sermon, preached in Lady Glenorchy's chapel, Edinburgh, 18 May 1774, before the managers of the Orphan-hospital, and published at their desire. / 1774 * Erskine, John Equ
2 CONC ity and wisdom of administration, in measures that have unhappily occasioned the American revolt, tried by the sacred oracles. 1776
2 CONT * Erskine, John Fatal consequences and the general sources of anarchy. A discourse on Isaiah, xxiv. 1,-5. The substance of which was preached ... before the magistrates of Edinburgh, 2d September 1792. 1793
2 CONT * Erskine, John History of the work of Redemption, containing the outlines of a body of divinity, in a method entirely new / by Jonathan Edwards; altered from the form of sermons, to that of a continued treatise by John Erskine. 1
2 CONC 808
