1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_William_Erskine,_1st_Baronet Wikipedia - Sir William Erskine, 1st Baronet]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-General Sir William Erskine, 1st Baronet (1728 – 19 March 1795) was a British Army commander and the 1st Baronet of the Erskine of Torrie creation.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Background''
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine was the son of Colonel the Honourable William Erskine of Torrie, Deputy Governor of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackness_Castle Blackness Castle, Blackness, Linlithgowshire EH49 7NH 56.00611, -3.51611], and grandson of
2 CONC  David Erskine, second Lord Cardross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine first married Magdalen Myrton, daughter of Robert Myrton of Gogar [https://www.trove.scot/place/50717 Castle Gogar, 180 Glasgow Road, Ingliston, Edinburgh EH12 9BQ 55.94306, -3.33833], and then Frances Moray, daughter of J
2 CONC ames Moray of Abercairny.
2 CONT
2 CONT His eldest son by his second wife, Frances, William, the 2nd baronet was a cavalry officer who committed suicide in Lisbon, Portugal in 1813 after a mental breakdown. He had another son, James, who was an officer in the 133rd (Fra
2 CONC ser's) Foot, and became baronet on his elder brother's death. His third son John, also became baronet, on the death of his brother James.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine joined his father’s regiment the 7th Dragoons in 1742 and served in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_Austrian_Succession War of the Austrian Succession, 16 December 1740 – 18 October 1748], seeing action at th
2 CONC e [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fontenoy Battle of Fontenoy, Fontenoy, Antoing, Belgium, 11 May 1745 50.5694, 3.4750], then in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Years%27_War Seven Years' War, 17 May 1756 – 15 Feb
2 CONC ruary 1763]. By March 1759 he was in Germany with the rank of Major in the 15th Light Dragoons, and was promoted Lieutenant-Colonel commanding the same regiment in 1762. Erskine performed exceptionally on the field at the [https:/
2 CONC /en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Emsdorf Battle of Emsdorf, Kirchhain, Hesse, Germany, 16 July 1760 50.817, 8.917] and was able to present King George III with 16 colors captured by his regiment. For his prowess, Erskine was knigh
2 CONC ted.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''American War of Independence''
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine was posted to America in 1776 as a Brigadier staff officer under Clinton, seeing action in the New York campaign and commanding the 7th Brigade at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Long_Island Battle of Long Island,
2 CONC  Long Island, Brooklyn, New York, United States 40.666, -73.966]. In 1777 he was Quartermaster-General to Lord Cornwallis. Erskine is credited with advising Cornwallis on the occasion of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_o
2 CONC f_the_Assunpink_Creek Battle of the Assunpink Creek, Trenton, New Jersey, United States 40.22167, -74.75611] (also known as 'Five Mile Run') not to put off attacking the Continental Army on the night of 2 January 1777. The Contine
2 CONC ntal forces moved away by night, fighting the Battle of Princeton on 3 January 1777.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine was made Colonel of the 80th Foot on 4 March 1777. Now a Brigadier-General, Erskine accompanied British forces as second in command under General William Tryon with the assistance of General James Agnew on an inland raid a
2 CONC gainst Patriot supply depots in Danbury, Connecticut. After successfully destroying Patriot supplies, the British forces engaged and defeated Continental Army Generals David Wooster, Benedict Arnold, and Gold S. Silliman and Patri
2 CONC ot militiamen in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ridgefield Battle of Ridgefield, Ridgefield, Connecticut, United States, 27 April 1777 41.30528, -73.50139]. Erskine served in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philad
2 CONC elphia_campaign Philadelphia campaign, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, United States, July 1777–July 1778 39.95278, -75.16361] and then, following Sir William Howe's resignation in 1778, continued as Quartermaster-General under Henry 
2 CONC Clinton. Erskine went on to lead troops at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Monmouth Battle of Monmouth, Freehold Borough, New Jersey, United States, 28 June 1778 40.256341, -74.320899] and after that campaign was give
2 CONC n command of the eastern district of Long Island. Erskine's last active duty in North America was commanding five infantry battalions and a cavalry squadron in pursuit of the Continental Army which was thought to be moving to Virg
2 CONC inia in November 1778. Promoted Major-General on 19 February 1779, Erskine sailed for London the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Wars of the French Revolution''
2 CONT
2 CONT He became Colonel-in-Chief of the 26th Foot (Cameronians) in 1782. On 28 September 1787 he was promoted Lieutenant-General. He became a baronet in 1791. After the declaration of war with revolutionary France he served on the staff
2 CONC  of the Duke of York in the Flanders Campaign from late 1793. He was present with York at the Siege of Dunkirk from 25 August 1793 to 10 September 1793, and commanded the retreat to Furnes after the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/
2 CONC Battle_of_Hondschoote Battle of Hondschoote, Hondschoote, Nord, France, 6–8 September 1793 50.9803, 2.5861]. Erskine was given command of the army during York’s absence through the Winter of 1793/1794.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the opening of the Spring campaign he was given command of York’s right wing in April 1794. He drove the French from Prémont on 16 April 1794, but was unable to join York to complete the victory at Vaux. He commanded a column s
2 CONC ent by Coburg to reinforce Clerfayt after the French breakthrough on 26 April 1794 (see [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Mouscron Battle of Mouscron, Mouscron, Belgium, 28–29 April 1794 50.733, 3.217]), then commanded a 16
2 CONC -squadron cavalry reserve under York at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tourcoing Battle of Tourcoing, Tourcoing, Nord, Hauts-de-France, France 17–18 May 1794 50.7323500, 3.1548000]. He again fought at Tournai soon af
2 CONC ter. Erskine commanded the 2nd (British) Line Corps in August 1794.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine died on 19 March 1795, aged 67, and is buried at Torryburn, Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the army he was nicknamed "Woolly" Erskine. 
2 CONT
