1 TITL Lieutenant
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Military Service:  Revolutionary War Soldier 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/LVCF-N9W/james-campbell-jr.%2C-esq.-1732-1796
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Campbell-3512
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=28946402
2 CONT
2 CONT James, a farmer descended from Scottish ancestors, is the son of David Campbell and Janet (Robertson) Campbell. He married Jane Knox on January 10th, 1760 in Blandford, Hampden County, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT James Campbell, a native of Blandford, Mass., who had been a soldier in the Revolutionary war, removed from Massachusetts with his family, 1791, and settled in the wilderness on Sugar Creek in Burlington, Bradford County, Pennsylv
2 CONC ania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married Jane Knox, their children being John, James, David, Cephas, William, Eleanor, Rachel, Cynthia, Jane, Betsy and Sally. John, James and David were soldiers in the struggle for Independence. Mr. Campbell died upon his 
2 CONC farm, December 28, 1813, in his 75th year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notice of his death says, "He was the father of 5 sons, 6 daughters, 6 sons-in-law, 93 grandchildren and 22 great-grandchildren, making in all 137. He was a member of the Methodist society and was much respected for his virtues." 
2 CONC He was of Scotch descent
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm he settled is still owned and occupied by his descendants. His wife, born February 3, 1744, died October 16, 1840. Both are buried in the family plot upon the homestead.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Died on Dec. 28, 1813, Burlington, Bradford been a soldier in the Revolutionary war, removed from Massachusetts with his family, 1791, and settled in the wilderness on Sugar Creek in Burlington, Bradford County, Pennsylvania. ane 
2 CONC Knox, their children being John, James, David, Cephas, William, Eleanor, Rachel, Cynthia, Jane, Betsy and Sally. John, James and David were soldiers in the struggle for Independence. Mr. Campbell died upon his farm, December 28, 1
2 CONC 813, in his 75th year.137. He was a member of the Methodist society and was much respected for his virtues." He was of Scotch descentsiness with his father for a few years in Blandford. James served on at least four occasions in 1
2 CONC 776-77 as a second Lieutenant in the Massachusetts Militia. Sometime after 1777 he left Blandford, stopping briefly at Salisbury, Conn., before settling in Nobletown (now Hillsdale), New York. He was living in Nobletown when his t
