1 OCCU http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Stuart, _3rd_Earl_of_Bute, Prime Minister of Great Britain between 1762 and 1763.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===John Stuart, 3rd Earl of Bute, KG, PC ===
2 CONT (25 May 1713 – 10 March 1792), 
2 CONT
2 CONT Styled Lord Mount Stuart before 1723, but known popularly as Lord Bute, he was a Scottish nobleman who served as Prime Minister of Great Britain (1762–1763) under George III, and was arguably the last important favourite in Britis
2 CONC h politics. He was the first Prime Minister from Scotland following the Acts of Union in 1707.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was played fabulously by actor Richard Cunningham (''Star Wars: Rogue One'', ''The Witcher'', ''Theory of Everything'') in the Netflix series '''''Queen Charlotte: A Bridgerton Story''''' (2023), depicting his influential rol
2 CONC e over Augusta Princess of Wales, Dowager Princess-Mother of King George III.
2 CONT
2 CONT John held the office of Hereditary Sheriff of Bute, 1723-47.1 
2 CONT John succeeded to the title of 3rd Earl of Bute [S., 1703], 28 January 1723.
2 CONT John succeeded to the title of 3rd Viscount Kingarth [S., 1703], 28 January 1723.
2 CONT John succeeded to the title of 3rd Lord Mount-Stuart [S., 1703], 28 Jan 1723.
2 CONT John succeeded to the title of 3rd Lord Cumra [S., 1703], 28 Jan 1723.
2 CONT John succeeded to the title of 3rd Lord Inchmarnock [S., 1703], 28 Jan 1723.
2 CONT John succeeded to the title of 5th Baronet Stuart of Bute [N.S., 1627], 28 January 1723. 
2 CONT John held the office of Representative Peer - Scotland, 1737-41.
2 CONT John was invested as a Knight of the Order of the Thistle (K.T.), 1738 (later resigned).
2 CONT John held the office of Groom of the Stole to HRH Frederick The Prince of Wales, 1750-51.
2 CONT John held the office of Groom of the Stole to HRH George The Prince of Wales, 1756-60. 
2 CONT John was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.), 1760.1 
2 CONT John held the office of Groom of the Stole to HM King George III, 1760-61.
2 CONT John held the office of Representative Peer - Scotland, 1761-80.
2 CONT John held the office of Ranger of Richmond Park, 1761-92. 
2 CONT John held the office of Chancellor of Marischal College - Aberdeen, 1761-92. 
2 CONT John held the office of Secretary of State for the North, 1761-62. 
2 CONT John was invested as a Knight of the Order of the Garter (K.G.), 1762.  
2 CONT John held the office of Prime Minister, 29 May 1762-15 April 1763.
2 CONT John held the office of First Lord of the Treasury, 29 May 1762-15 April 1763. 
2 CONT John held the office of Vice-Admiral of Bute, 1764-76.  
2 CONT John held the office of 1st President of the Society of Antiquaries [Scotland] 1780-92. 
2 CONT John was invested as a Fellow of the Royal College of Physicians, Edinburgh (F.R.C.P.E.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1763 John purchased the estate at Luton Hoo, Bedfordshire.  
2 CONT
2 CONT John's grandfather was Prime Minister of Great Britain under George II and occupied that position after Walpole.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===His Life===
2 CONT He was born in Parliament Close, nearby to St Giles Cathedral on the Royal Mile in Edinburgh on 25 May 1713 to James Stuart 2nd Earl of Bute, and his wife Lady Anne Campbell the daughter of Archibald Campbell 1st Duke of Argyll an
2 CONC d his wife Lady Elizabeth Campbell.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended Eton College from 1724 to 1730[2] and went on to study civil law at the Universities of Groningen (1730–1732) and Leiden (1732–1734) in the Netherlands,[3][4] graduating from the latter with a degree in civil law.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT A close relative of the Clan Campbell (his mother being the daughter of the 1st Duke of Argyll), John succeeded to the Earldom of Bute (named after the Isle of Bute) upon the death of his father in 1723. He was brought up thereaft
2 CONC er by his maternal uncles, the 2nd Duke of Argyll and Archibald Campbell 3rd Duke of Argyll (1st and only Earl of Ilay). In August 1735, he eloped with Mary Wortley Montagu, whose parents Sir Edward and Lady Mary Wortley Montagu w
2 CONC ere slow to consent to the marriage.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1737, he was elected a Scottish representative peer; despite being in London in December of that year, he did not participate in deliberations in the House of Lords.[6] Because of his support for Argyll against Walpole, he was 
2 CONC not re-elected in 1741.[7] For the next several years he retired to his estates in Scotland to manage his affairs and indulge his interest in botany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1745, Bute moved to Westminster London, where his family rented a house at Twickenham for forty-five pounds annually.[8] He met Frederick, Prince of Wales, son of King George II, in 1747 at the Egham Races and became a close fr
2 CONC iend.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Prince's death in 1751, Bute was appointed tutor to Prince George, the new Prince of Wales (later George III).[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bute arranged for the Prince and his brother Prince Edward to follow a course of lectures on natural philosophy by the itinerant lecturer Stephen Demainbray. This led to an interest in natural philosophy on the part of the young p
2 CONC rince and may have led to George III's collection of natural philosophical instruments.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bute furthermore became close to Prince Frederick's widow, Augusta of Saxe-Gotha, the Dowager Princess of Wales, and it was rumoured that the couple were having an affair. Indeed, one of the Prince of Wales' associates, John Horne
2 CONC  Tooke, published a scandalous pamphlet alluding to the liaison, but the rumours were almost certainly untrue, since Bute held sincere religious beliefs against adultery and, by all indications, appeared happily married. Though it
2 CONC  is also true that strong convictions on a matter does not preclude the chance of violating those convictions when overruled by passions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the influence he had over his pupil, Bute expected to rise quickly to political power following George's accession to the throne in 1760, but his plans were premature. It would first be necessary to remove both the incu
2 CONC mbent prime minister (the Duke of Newcastle) and arguably the even more powerful Secretary of State for the Southern Department (William Pitt the Elder).[12] The Government of the day, buoyed by recent successes in the Seven Years
2 CONC ' War, was popular, however, and did well at the general election which, as was customary at the time, took place on the accession of the new monarch.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Supported by the King, Bute maneuvered himself into power by first allying himself with Newcastle against Pitt over the latter's desire to declare war on Spain. Once thwarted in his designs against Spain by Bute and Newcastle, Pit
2 CONC t resigned his post as Secretary of State for the Southern Department. Next, Bute forced Newcastle's resignation as prime minister when he found himself in a small minority within the government over the level of funding and direc
2 CONC tion of the Seven Years' War.[14] Re-elected as a Scottish representative peer in 1760, Bute was appointed the de facto prime minister after the resignations of Pitt and Newcastle, thus ending a long period of Whig dominance.[cita
2 CONC tion needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bute's premiership was notable for the negotiation of the Treaty of Paris (1763) which concluded the Seven Years' War. In so doing, Bute had to soften his previous stance in relation to concessions given to France in that he agree
2 CONC d that the important fisheries in Newfoundland be returned to France without Britain's possession of Guadeloupe in return.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT After peace was concluded, Bute and the King decided that Britain's military expenditure should not exceed its pre-war levels, but they thought a large presence was necessary in the American colonies to deal with the French and Sp
2 CONC anish threat. They therefore charged ''the colonists'' for the increased military levels, thus catalyzing the colonial resistance to taxes which directly led to the American Revolution.[16] Bute also introduced a cider tax of four
2 CONC  shillings per hogshead in 1763 to help finance the Seven Years' War.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT The journalist John Wilkes published a newspaper, The North Briton, in which both Bute and the Dowager Princess of Wales were savagely satirised. Bute resigned as prime minister shortly afterwards, although he remained in the Hous
2 CONC e of Lords as a Scottish representative peer until 1780. He remained friendly with the Dowager Princess of Wales, but her attempts to reconcile him with George III proved futile.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the remainder of his life, Bute remained at his estate in Hampshire, where he built himself a mansion called High Cliff near Christchurch.[17] From there he continued his pursuit of botany and became a major literary and artis
2 CONC tic patron. Among his beneficiaries were Samuel Johnson, Tobias Smollett, Robert Adam, William Robertson and John Hill. He also gave considerably to the Scottish universities.
2 CONT
2 CONT He financed Alberto Fortis's travels into Dalmatia. His botanical work culminated in the publication of Botanical Tables Containing the Families of British Plants in 1785. Even after his retirement, Bute was accused by many Americ
2 CONC ans in the years leading up to the American Revolutionary War as having an undue corrupting influence over the British government.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT The flowering plant genera Butea, Stewartia and Stuartia are named after him.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1761, Bute was appointed Ranger of Richmond Park by King George III, a post he held until his death; Bute Avenue in Petersham near the park is named after him.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to historian John Naish, the 18th-century expression "Jack Boot", meaning "a stupid person", originated as a disparagement of John's performance as prime minister.[21] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bute purchased Luton Hoo, or Luton Park, from Francis Herne MP in 1763 for the sum of £94,700.[22] Recognising that the existing buildings were unsuitable, Bute commissioned the neoclassical architect Robert Adam to oversee the re
2 CONC design of the estate house.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Initial designs were unsatisfactory and, coupled with the sale of Bute House, Adams submitted new designs for a larger complex, which Bute further adjusted to include five book rooms and seven water closets.[23] The building also 
2 CONC housed an extensive art collection, particularly paintings of the Dutch and Flemish schools. A fire in March 1771 "did considerable damage" according to contemporary reports.[24] The project was completed by 1773 but not according
2 CONC  to the full plan, the second phase of which was abandoned.[25] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at his home in South Audley Street, Grosvenor Square, Westminster, from complications of a fall suffered while staying at Highcliffe, and was buried at Rothesay on the Isle of Bute.[17] 
2 CONT He died on 10 March 1792, from a fall he had a year and a half prior. He fell 30 feet (9.1 m) down cliffs in Hampshire while collecting plants.[26] He died in his mansion on South Audley Street off Grosvenor Square.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1736 he married Baroness Mary Wortley Montagu the daughter of Edward Wortley Montagu Sr. 1st Earl of Sandwich and his wife Mary Wortley Pierrepoint Countess of Sandwich Lady Montagu.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Mary had at least eleven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Mary Stuart (c. 1741 – 5 April 1824), married James Lowther Earl of Lonsdale.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Stuart Lord Mount Stuart, 4th Earl of Bute, Marquess of Bute (30 June 1744 – 16 Nov 1814).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Stuart (c.1745), married Hugh Percy, Lord Warkworth 2nd Duke of Northumberland, 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hon. James Archibald Stuart (21 Sept 1747 – 1 March 1818),[27] politician and author.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Augusta Stuart (c. 1748 – 12 Feb 1778), married Andrew Corbett.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jane Stuart (c.1748 – 28 Feb 1828), married George Macartney Earl Macrtney.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hon. Frederick Stuart (1751–1802), politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hon. Charles Stuart (Jan 1753 – 25 May 1801), soldier and politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hon. William Stuart, Archbishop of Armagh (March 1755 – 6 March 1822), Anglican prelate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Caroline Stuart (c.1763 – 20 January 1813), married The Hon John Dawson 1st Earl of Portarlington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Louisa Stuart (12 Aug 1757 – 4 Aug 1851), writer, died unmarried.[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
