1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Campbell_Fraser_of_Lovat Archibald Campbell Fraser of Lovat]
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Campbell Fraser of Lovat, (16 August 1736 – 8 December 1815) was British consul at Tripoli and Algiers, and later colonel of the 1st Inverness local militia. Upon the death of his brother, Simon Fraser (1726–1782), Archi
2 CONC bald became the 20th MacShimidh (chief) of Clan Fraser of Lovat, and sat in the House of Commons from 1782 to 1784.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Campbell Fraser was born the son of Simon Fraser, 11th Lord Lovat, by his third wife, Primrose Campbell. (She was sister of the man who would become 4th Duke of Argyll, chief of the Clan Campbell). Archibald himself was 
2 CONC named after Lovat's early patron, the 1st Duke of Argyll. The marriage was a consequence of the long-standing bond between the chiefs of Clan Fraser and Clan Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a significant age difference between the couple, however. She was 23, he was 56 and had already been twice married. The marriage was not a happy one and in 1738 she left her husband to live in Edinburgh, leaving Archibal
2 CONC d in his care.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was at school at the village of Petty, where he was educated by the parish minister, when the Battle of Culloden occurred nearby. With some school companions, he watched the battle, where he saw Fraser clansmen in the front lin
2 CONC e. He also witnessed their decimation, with many of the 300 Frasers cut down.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Lord Lovat, was subsequently tried for treason and beheaded in 1747. The title was attainted, and Archibald's half-brother Simon, Master of Lovat, was incarcerated in Edinburgh Castle for a year, although he received a
2 CONC  full pardon in 1750. Archibald was sent to Glasgow for his education by his mother and he attended Glasgow University from 1750-1752.
2 CONT
2 CONT His life in his early adult years is not well documented, and secondary sources attribute a variety of activities to him. One account suggests he adopted the name Fitzsimon and ‘distinguished himself in the sporting world’.It is a
2 CONC lso suggested he served in the Portuguese military, whilst his parliamentary biography states he was a merchant.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is documented is that he was granted a small sum of money paid annually from the attainted Lovat estate. Also, from 1764 to 1766 he was the British Consul in Tripoli, and from 1767 to 1776 the Consul in Algiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1778 he was back in London. His name appears on the membership list for the Highland Society of London.This had been recently established to 'promote the Interests of the Highlands', with its particular goals being to repeal th
2 CONC e ban on Highland dress being worn, the promotion of Gaelic language, music and literature, establishing schools, agricultural improvement, and the relief of distressed Highlanders. (His half-brother Simon was a founding member an
2 CONC d president of this organisation). He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in this same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lovat estates (but not the title) had already been restored to Simon in 1774. When he died childless in 1782, Archibald succeeded as the 20th MacShimidh. However, as Simon was indebted, the estates were vested in trustees unti
2 CONC l all debts should be paid off.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald also inherited his brother's seat of Inverness-shire in the House of Commons. He was returned unopposed following his brother's death at a by-election on 28 March 1782. He held the seat for two years. During this time hi
2 CONC s speeches were mainly on economic and Scottish affairs. In his maiden speech, on 17 June 1782, he seconded Lord Graham's motion to repeal the Dress Act, which was passed in 1746 in an effort to subdue the Highlands. This made wea
2 CONC ring the tartan or kilt illegal in Scotland. The Act was successfully repealed later in 1782.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1784 he was a member of the St. Alban's Tavern group who tried to bring Fox and Pitt together. He did not stand for re-election at the 1784 general election.
