1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_Erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Agnes Erskine (1739 – 5 October 1804) was a Scottish aristocrat and friend and trustee of Selina, Countess of Huntingdon. She became an important figure in the Countess of Huntingdon's Connexion which was a group of chur
2 CONC ches which still survives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Erskine was born in Edinburgh in 1739. the eldest child of Agnes and Henry David Erskine, 10th Earl of Buchan (1710–1767). Her mother was the daughter of Sir James Stewart, 1st Baronet (1681–1727), of Goodtrees.[1] Her brothers we
2 CONC re David Erskine, 11th Earl of Buchan, who founded the Society of Antiquaries of Scotland; Hon. Henry Erskine, a lawyer, and Thomas Erskine, 1st Baron Erskine, lord chancellor from 1806 to 1807. Her maternal uncle was Sir James St
2 CONC euart, the Jacobite political economist.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1783 view of what was the Dog and Duck and Spa Fields chapel
2 CONT She and her brothers were home schooled by their mother in religious household.[1] When she was an adult, she became a carer for her father who was living in Walcot. Her paternal grandfather has a friend of Selina the Countess of 
2 CONC Huntingdon and George Whitefield who were evangelical methodists. Lady Huntingdon's chapel was used for her father's funeral when he died at the end of 1767. The service was conducted by George Whitefield.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She became Lady Huntingdon's secretary living with her at Spa Fields in Islington.[1] Lady Huntingdon had bought a huge chapel in 1779 which attracted congregations of 2,000, but she called "her private chapel". The building had b
2 CONC een built as a Pantheon or theatre-in-the-round, and it had a huge dome. Erskine and Huntingdon lived next door in what had been the Dog and Duck, and which had been rebuilt in 1756.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1791 Selina identified four people who would look after her wealth and chapels after she died. Erskine was one of these and Erskine continued to live in their spacious home, but she did not have the funds that the Countess
2 CONC  had at her disposal to support her work. However, the chapels continued and Erskine took over the recruitment of new ministers who were required despite some congregations moving away. Erskine was reliant on grants supplied by th
2 CONC e Spa Fields Chapel committee. She died in 1804 in London and she was buried at Bunhill Fields. Her place as a Connexion truste was taken by the Reverend John Ford after her death but it converted to a trust shortly afterwards.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT A biography of her was published in 1833,[4] but the Countess did not have one for many more years as she had requested that one should not be written.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.londonremembers.com/subjects/lady-anne-agnes-erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Erskine-1336
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT The Right Honorable Ann Erskine was the eldest daughter of Henry Erskine and Agnes Steuart
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a friend and companion of Selina (Shirley) Hastings (1707-1791), Countess of Huntingdon who through "Countess of Huntingdon's Connexion" [1] established a number of non-conformist chapels. Anne and the Countess lived toget
2 CONC her at the chapel house at Spa Fields, Islington, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the Countess's death in 1791, the 64 chapels and the college were bequeathed to four trustees. One of them was Lady Ann, who was requested to occupy and reside in Lady Huntingdon's house, adjoining Spa Fields Chapel, and carry 
2 CONC on all needful correspondence, which was immense. She did this on dutifully until her own death in 1804.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Bunhill Fields Burial Ground 1873
2 CONT Ann was buried at Bunhill Fields Burial Ground, used by nonconformists. Ann was buried on 13 Oct 1804 at Bunhill Fields burial ground in Islington, London. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://grokipedia.com/page/anne_erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/54319851/ann_agnes-erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT The Right Honorable Lady Erskine, eldest daughter of the late Earl of Buchan. She was the sister of Lord Chancellor Thomas Erskine and one of the trustees of the Countess of Huntingdon. She spent 40 years helping the poor.
2 CONT
2 CONT She spent her early years in Scotland before her father moved to Bath. She became close to the Countess and took over the management of her estate building many chapels.
2 CONT
2 CONT She lived in the Countess's former home at Spa Fields in Clerkenfield for the rest of her life.
