2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Erskine,_11th_Earl_of_Buchan
2 CONT
2 CONT David Stuart Erskine, 11th Earl of Buchan, FRS, FSA Scot (1 June 1742 – 19 April 1829), styled Lord Cardross between 1747 and 1767, was a Scottish antiquarian, founder of the Society of Antiquaries of Scotland, and patron of the a
2 CONC rts and sciences.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Background and education
2 CONT Erskine was the second but eldest surviving son of Henry Erskine, 10th Earl of Buchan, by Agnes, daughter of Sir James Stewart, 1st Baronet. He was the brother of Hon. Henry Erskine and Lord Erskine. His elder sister was Lady Anne
2 CONC  Erskine who was involved with the evangelical methodists of Countess of Huntingdon's Connexion.[2] He attended St. Andrews University (1755–59) Edinburgh University (1760–62) and Glasgow University (1762–63), studying under Adam 
2 CONC Smith, and Joseph Black.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, on 15 October 1771, at Aberdeen, his cousin Margaret Fraser (c. 1746–1819), the granddaughter of the 12th Lord Saltoun.[4] They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT His main residence was Kirkhill House in Broxburn, Linlithgowshire (West Lothian).[5] He inherited the property, dating from 1590, and had it extensively remodelled in 1770–1.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT Erskine‘s active criticism helped to effect a change in the method of electing Scottish representative peers to the House of Lords.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was offered the post of Secretary at the British Embassy in Madrid in 1766–67 but declined it due to the illness of his father, who died in 1767.[3] He was also the Grand Master of Scottish Freemasons from 1782 to 1784.[4][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was friends with Benjamin Franklin, originating probably from Franklin's visit to Scotland in 1759, and met him several times in London in 1764.[7] A supporter of the American cause, he corresponded with George Washington and s
2 CONC ent him a box made from the oak that hid William Wallace after the Battle of Falkirk. The box was designed by the Goldsmith Company of Edinburgh, and the earl requested that Washington, upon his death, pass it on to the man in his
2 CONC  country who he believed should merit it best, and upon the same conditions that induced the earl to send it to Washington. In his will Washington thanked the earl for presenting the box to him along with sentiments which accompan
2 CONC ied it; feeling incapable of making the choice asked of him he bequeathed the box back to the earl.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1780 he founded the Society of Antiquaries of Scotland. As he outlined in a letter of November 1780, he wished to create a body to promote antiquarian researches in that part of Great Britain. It drew on traditions that involve
2 CONC d Sir John Scot of Scotstarvit (who preserved the cartographic work of Timothy Pont and others), Sir Robert Sibbald, and Sir John Clerk of Penicuik. The Society of Antiquaries of Scotland was formally constituted on 18 December 17
2 CONC 80. He later disagreed with some policies of the Society and withdrew in 1790.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, after withdrawing from the management of the Society of Antiquaries, he remained active in the area, contributing papers, and became involved with the Literary and Antiquarian Society of Perth in 1785. In 1794, he was ele
2 CONC cted to the American Philosophical Society.[10] Erskine was elected a member of the American Antiquarian Society in 1816.[11] He was also elected to honorary membership of the Royal Danish Academy of Sciences and Letters in 1785, 
2 CONC and of its Icelandic counterpart in 1791.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Spending much of his later time at Dryburgh House, he embellished the grounds with monuments, including commemorations of his ancestors and of Robert Burns and William Wallace. He also commissioned a cable-stayed bridge over the R
2 CONC iver Tweed at Dryburgh. He opened the Dryburgh Abbey Bridge on 1 August 1817, but it collapsed within months. A replacement was built after a redesign, but this too collapsed in 1838. A more permanent design was not found until 18
2 CONC 72, when a suspension bridge was erected.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT In later years he became increasingly eccentric, a trait which tended to obscure his talents, as was noted by Sir Walter Scott.[13] After the earl's death he was buried in the family ground at Dryburgh with his head laid the wrong
2 CONC  way, of which Sir Walter Scott said was little matter, “as it had never been quite right in his lifetime."[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT His correspondents included Horace Walpole, and he produced an Essay on the Lives of Fletcher of Saltoun and the Poet Thomson (1792), and several other writings.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is an interesting story concerning the Earl in which the writer George Dyer brought him to meet Charles Lamb at his flat in Mitre Court Buildings. Lamb was not home and his sister Mary was deeply flustered at having to greet
2 CONC  an Earl unannounced in her household. [citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT Erskine died at his residence at Dryburgh (near Dryburgh Abbey, in the Scottish Borders) in April 1829, leaving no legitimate children. The earldom passed to his nephew Henry [15] though all his non-entailed properties, including 
2 CONC Dryburgh, were passed to his illegitimate son, Sir David Erskine.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <strong>BUCHAN, DAVID STEUART, EARL OF</strong>, sixth of the house of Erskine, but the eleventh from the period when the earldom was conferred upon James Steuart, son of Sir James Steuart, the Black Knight of Lorn, by his wife Ja
2 CONC ne, Queen of Scotland, widow of James I. and mother of James II. ; born 1742; married, 1771, Margaret, eldest daughter of William Fraser, of Fraserfield, Aberdeen shire, but by her (who died 1819; had no issue. The Earl was a frie
2 CONC nd of Burns; he died 1829, and was succeeded by his nephew, Henry David Erskine.
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE:  Andrew, William Raeburn. [http://archive.org/stream/lifeofsirhenryra00andriala#page/106/mode/2up Life of Sir Henry Raeburn, R.A. With portraits and appendix]. Published by London: W.H. Allen & co. in 1886. page 106.
2 CONT
