1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir Roderick Mackenzie, Baronet [S. 1703], of Scatwell</strong>, 1st s. and h., b. probably about 1740; suc. to the Baronetcy, 13 Sep. 1756 ; Capt. in the Guards, 1762 ; rebuilt Rosehaugh house. He m. 7 April 1764, Cathar
2 CONC iue, 1st da. of Sir James Colquhoun, 1st Baronet [1786], of Luss, formerly James Grant, by Helen, sister of William, Earl of Sutherland [S.], da. of William Gordon, styled Lord Strathnaver. She d. 11 March 1804. He d. 11 June 1811
2 CONC ."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n427/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 409
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/historyofmackenz00mack#page/570/mode/2up History of the Mackenzies, with genealogies of the principal families of the name]; Alexander Mackenzie; 1894; page 571
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ARMY SERVICE'''
2 CONT
2 CONT - Captain in 3rd Regiment of Foot Guards(Scots Guards), 1762.
2 CONT - Major in Sutherland Regiment of Fencible Mens, 1779
2 CONT
2 CONT Referred to as Major Sir Roderick, dated 24th Aug 1781, by Alexander Mackenzie WS in letter, Add MS 39191
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Served in the army in early life, and rose, in 1762, to the rank of Captain in the Guards' although in an article, The Scots Magazine - Monday 07 March 1763, suggests he was reduced to Ensign in 1763.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/britishbaronetag32play/page/n377/mode/2up?q=%22sir+roderick+mackenzie%22+scatwell
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/sim_edinburgh-magazine_1762-12_6/page/644/mode/2up?q=%22sir+roderick+mackenzie%22+scatwell
2 CONT
2 CONT '''MISTRESS & OTHER CHILDREN'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Gray(unmarried) names Sir Roderick Mackenzie of Scatwell as the 'father of her FORMER child' and of her 'present pregnancy' after she has a 'relapse in adultery'. It is not known how old the former child was and if the ne
2 CONC w born survived. Not ruling out the possibility of it being Capt John Taylor Mackenzie.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Dingwall Kirk Sessions, 1782
2 CONT
