1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA.  [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A047069  DAR Ancestor # A047069]
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. John Grant
2 CONT
2 CONT  According to The Kentucky Encyclopedia, pg. 383, John Grant and two of his brothers Samuel & Squire came to Kentucky early, by 1779. John left and went to North Carolina and Virginia and then came back to Kentucky about 1784, dyi
2 CONC ng there in 1826. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. John Grant was the epitome of a frontiersman. He was innovative, brave, and a great leader of men. He was born in the Yadkin Valley to William Grant (a Scottish immigrant) and Elizabeth Boone (sister to Daniel Boone). The tim
2 CONC e of his arrivial were troubled times with the Native Americans and troubled times seemed to follow him most of his life. Between flipflopping between the Yadkin Valley and the early Washington, D.C. area with his family, little i
2 CONC s known of his early years. He served time in a Virginia outfit, amonst other things during the American Revolution and he married Mary Mosby, daughter of Samuel Mosby and Jerusha Bowels of Virginia, who probably followed them int
2 CONC o Kentucky. By 1779, John, his wife, parents, and other Grant family trailed along with Daniel Boone and his family and other settlers into Kentucky and stopped over at Boonesborough.  Later, the Grants moved on to Bryan's Station
2 CONC , which was established by more family relations. John Grant then, in order to relieve the overburden population of Bryans Station, established another station north east of Bryans, now known at Grants Station (see video and more 
2 CONC at http://frontierfolk.org/grantsfort.htm). After a near fatal attack by the American Natives, John and his family moved out of Kentucky, but had returned to Kentucky by 1784. He reestablished Grant's station, but eventually sold 
2 CONC out and went into the salt licks south of Cincinnati, where he and others removed the salt (read the following found at  http://www.nkyviews.com/campbell/campbell_grants_lick.htm, Mrs. J. B. Smith's History of Grant's Lick and Mar
2 CONC garet Strebel Hartman's History of Grant and His Salt Works). John Grant has the distinction of having also served two terms in early Kentucky legislation. He was also considered to be one of the richest men in Kentucky. He even h
2 CONC andled Daniel Boone's financial affairs when Boone had hit hard times.  After the death of his first spouse, Mary Mosby Grant in 1790, John remarried to an Eliza Buckner. With her he had one daughter, Caroline A. Grant (1800-1848)
2 CONC . Sadly, as previously stated, John hit upon hard times himself. As family lore would have it, he tried to make his way into Missouri to be with other Grants and Boones that made it there, but eventually returned to Kentucky where
2 CONC  it is said that he died on his property along the Elkhorn River. Unlike his famous uncle, John Grant's memory is mostly obscure in Kentucky history and very few people know of his contributions to the Kentucky frontier. Ray Jacks
2 CONC on Fourth Great Grandson of John Grant
2 CONT
2 CONT Col John Grant Birth: 30 Jan 1754 Yadkin, Rowan County, North Carolina, USA Death: 11 Nov 1825 (aged 71) Fayette County, Kentucky, USA Burial: Elkhorn CemeteryFayette County, Kentucky, USA Memorial #: 81211402 Bio: Col. John Grant
2 CONC  was the epitome of a frontiersman. He was innovative, brave, and a great leader of men. He was born in the Yadkin Valley to William Grant (a Scottish immigrant) and Elizabeth Boone (sister to Daniel Boone). The time of his arrivi
2 CONC al were troubled times with the Native Americans and troubled times seemed to follow him most of his life.    Between flipflopping between the Yadkin Valley and the early Washington, D.C. area with his family, little is known of h
2 CONC is early years. He served time in a Virginia outfit, amonst other things during the American Revolution and he married Mary Mosby, daughter of Samuel Mosby and Jerusha Bowels of Virginia, who probably followed them into Kentucky. 
2 CONC     By 1779, John, his wife, parents, and other Grant family trailed along with Daniel Boone and his family and other settlers into Kentucky and stopped over at Boonesborough.     Later, the Grants moved on to Bryan's Station, whi
2 CONC ch was established by more family relations. John Grant then, in order to relieve the overburden population of Bryans Station, established another station north east of Bryans, now known at Grants Station (see video and more at  h
2 CONC ttp://frontierfolk.org/grantsfort.htm).     After a near fatal attack by the American Natives, John and his family moved out of Kentucky, but had returned to Kentucky by 1784. He reestablished Grant's station, but eventually sold 
2 CONC out and went into the salt licks south of Cincinnati, where he and others removed the salt (read the following found at http://www.nkyviews.com/campbell/campbell_grants_lick.htm, Mrs. J. B. Smith's History of Grant's Lick and Marg
2 CONC aret Strebel Hartman's History of Grant and His Salt Works).     John Grant has the distinction of having also served two terms in early Kentucky legislation. He was also considered to be one of the richest men in Kentucky. He eve
2 CONC n handled Daniel Boone's financial affairs when Boone had hit hard times.      After the death of his first spouse, Mary Mosby Grant in 1790, John remarried to an Eliza Buckner. With her he had one daughter, Caroline A. Grant (180
2 CONC 0-1848).     Sadly, as  previously stated, John hit upon hard times himself. As family lore would have it, he tried to make his way into Missouri to be with other Grants and Boones that made it there, but eventually returned to Ke
2 CONC ntucky where it is said that he died on his property along the Elkhorn River.    Unlike his famous uncle, John Grant's memory is mostly obscure in Kentucky history and very few people know of his contributions to the Kentucky fron
2 CONC tier.Ray JacksonFourth Great Grandson of John Grant Gravesite Details Actual place of burial is lost. Ray Jackson is a descendant of John Grant via his son, William Mosby Grant.
2 CONT
