3 CTRY Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "VI. <strong>Captain John Mackenzie, sixth of Kincraig</strong>, second surviving son of Roderick Ban, VII. of Redcastle, born there in 1751. He served in Lord Macleod's Regiment (now 71st Highlanders), and was wounded at Gibralta
2 CONC r. His descendants, since the death of Roderick, IX. of Redcastle in 1798 without issue, carried on also the representation of the main line of that family. He married Mary, daughter of the Rev. Colin Mackenzie, minister of Fodder
2 CONC ty, with issue — 
2 CONT * Roderick, his heir and successor. 
2 CONT * Colin, Lieutenant 71st Regiment, killed in action at Vittoria, on the 21st of June, 1813, without issue. 
2 CONT * John, who died without issue, on the 20th of August, 1822, off St. Helena, coming home from Java. 
2 CONT * Kenneth Francis, Colonel 64th Bengal Native Infantry, who married on the 6th of January, 1832, Margaret, daughter of the Rev. Thomas Taylor, D.D., of Tibbermore, with issue
2 CONT * Hector, Major H.E.LC.S., who died unmarried. 
2 CONT * Hugh, late Colonel 2nd Bengal Europeans, who married, first, Anne, daughter of Thomas Duncan, Advocate, Aberdeen, with issue
2 CONT * Charles Fitzgerald, H.E.LC.S., who married the Hon. Mrs Fergusson, daughter of Lord Kirkcudbright, and died, without issue, on the 5th of September, 1850. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain John had also 
2 CONT * Maxwell, a natural son, Lieutenant-Colonel 71st Regiment, killed at Bayonne in 1813, to whom and his brother Colin a monument by Chantry is erected in Rosskeen Church. 
2 CONT * Mary, who on the 28th of January, 1813, married Major-General Sir Donald Macleod. 
2 CONT * Johanna Charlotte Menzies, who died unmarried in 1794. 
2 CONT * Margaret, who married Donald Macintyre, Calcutta, with issue
2 CONT * Jane Petley, who died young. 
2 CONT * Isabella, who married, first. Captain Allan Cameron, with issue; and secondly. General Sir Hugh Fraser, K.C.B., of Braelangwell, with issue
2 CONT * Elizabeth Jane, who died unmarried in 1832. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain John's widow died at Park House, Dingwall, on the 4th of January, 1838. He having died at Kincraig on the 29th of April, 1822, aged 72 years, when he was succeeded by his eldest son."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/historyofmackenz00mack#page/544/mode/2up History of the Mackenzies, with genealogies of the principal families of the name]; Alexander Mackenzie; 1894; page 545
