2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/cc4rz/cunningham07.php Stirnet: Cunningham 07]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_William_Cunynghame,_4th_Baronet Wikipedia: Sir William Cunynghame, 4th Baronet]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Augustus Cunynghame of Livingstone, 4th Baronet of Milncraig (1747–1828) was a Scottish politician who sat in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Commons_of_the_United_Kingdom House of Commons 51.499889, -0.124
2 CONC 667] of Great Britain from 1774 to 1790.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Cunynghame was the only surviving son of Sir David Cunynghame, 3rd Baronet and his wife Lady Mary Montgomerie, daughter of Alexander Montgomerie, 9th Earl of Eglinton, and was born on 19 April 1747. He matriculated at [https://en.
2 CONC wikipedia.org/wiki/Christ_Church,_Oxford Christ Church, St Aldate's, Oxford OX1 1DP 51.750199, -1.255853] on 6 December 1766. After he succeeded his father in the baronetcy on 10 October 1767, he undertook a Grand Tour. His first 
2 CONC wife was Frances Myreton daughter of Sir Robert Myreton, 2nd Baronet whom he married on 21 October 1768. She died on 14 November 1771, and he went abroad for three years to Italy, Paris and Vienna.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Political career''
2 CONT
2 CONT At the 1774 general election Cunynghame was returned as Member of Parliament for Linlithgowshire with the agreement of the Hopetoun interest. In June 1779 he was appointed Clerk of the Green Cloth and held the post until March 178
2 CONC 2. He was returned again as MP for Linlithgowshire in 1780 and 1784. He made a second marriage to Mary Udney, daughter of Robert Udney of Udney, Aberdeen on 22 June 1785. In parliament and outside he campaigned strongly for Scotti
2 CONC sh interests. He was defeated in the 1790 general election and did not re-enter Parliament
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Later life and legacy''
2 CONT
2 CONT Cunynghame was receiver of the land tax in Scotland from June 1806 to March 1807. He died on 17 January 1828 and was succeeded by his son David. He had two other sons by his first wife and four sons and three daughters by his seco
2 CONC nd wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://electricscotland.com/history/sport/hunt/chapter1.htm The History of the Linlithgow and Stirlingshire Hunt 1775-1910]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD86%2f960&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=Frances+Cunynghame&ko=a&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 23 January 1769: Instrument of Sasine under the hand of James Miller, clerk, of the diocese of St. Andrews, notary public, following upon the precept of sasine in a contract of marriage (dated 21 October 1768) between Sir William 
2 CONC Augustus Cunynghame of Livingston, Baronet, and Mrs. Frances Myrton daughter and only child in life of Sir Robert Myrton of Gogar, Baronet, whereby Sir William bound and obliged himself to invest  [infeft] his spouse in an annuity
2 CONC  of £550 sterling (restricted to £350 in the event of there being an heir-male alive at the dissolution of the marriage by the death of Sir William, and to £450 if there is an heir female alive) furth of 
2 CONT # the lands and barony of Livingstone and the temple lands of Newyear-field as described in No. GD86/815; 
2 CONT # the lands of Nether Dechmont lying within the barony of Calderclear, regality of Dalkeith and sheriffdom of Edinburgh; 
2 CONT # the town and lands of Wester Whitburn extending to a 50 shilling land as well property as superiority, 
2 CONT # one-half of the lands and barony of Easter Whitburn and of the lands of Little Blackburn, in the parish of Whitburn and sheriffdom of Linlithgow; (4) the proportion of the lands of Little Blackburn purchased lately from the trus
2 CONC tees of John Carmichael of Whitehill, with the mansionhouse and yards adjacent thereto (sometime possessed by the said John Carmichael and John Beugo younger, of Cowhill and afterwards by William Hamilton and others) and with libe
2 CONC rty of casting and winning peats for the use of the tenants of said lands within the Moss of Meikle Blackburn lying in the parish of Whitburn, barony of Blackburn and sheriffdom of Linlithgow; and 
2 CONT # the lands of Sir William in the sheriffdom of Ayr. Sasine given by Sir William ex propriis suis manibus, on 23 January 1769, to John Gray, his factor, as attorney for Mrs. Frances Myrton
2 CONT
2 CONT Witnesses, James Hay, writer in Edinburgh, and William Daes, servant to Sir William. Sir William "in token of the verity of all and sundry the premisses" signs the instrument.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000045/17681022/009/0003 British Newspaper Archive: Caledonian Mercury Saturday, 22 October 1768 Page 3 Edinburgh]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Yesternight, Friday, 21 October 1768 was married at Gogar, Sir William Augustus Cunynghame of Livingstone, Baronet, to Miss Frances Myrton, daughter of Sir Robert Myrton of Gogar, Baronet. [https://www.trove.scot/place/50717 trove
2 CONC .scot: Castle Gogar, 180 Glasgow Road, Ingliston, Edinburgh EH12 9BQ 55.94306, -3.33833]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/search-records/church-registers/church-births-baptisms#form&record_type=opr_births&church_type=Old%20Parish%20Registers&surname=Cun%2Aham%2A&surname_so=wild&forename_so=exact&from_year=1
2 CONC 768&to_year=1771&parent_names=Frances&parent_names_so=exact&parent_name_two_so=exact&sort=asc&order=Date&field=year Scotland's People: Old Parish Registers: Births and Baptisms]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible list of 3 children of William Augustus Cunynghame, Baronet, and Frances Myrton from first: 2 July 1769 David Cunynghame, Baronet to last: 19 November 1771 Francis Cunynghame
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2 July 1769 birth or baptism of David Cunynghame, Baronet, son of William Augustus Cunynghame, Baronet and Frances Myrton [child 1], in the parish of Livingston
2 CONT * 24 August 1769 birth or baptism of David Cunnigham, son of Sir William Cunnigham and and Frances Myreton [child 1], in the parish of Canongate
2 CONT * 29 July 1770 birth or baptism of Robert Cunynghame, son of William Augustus Cunynghame, Baronet/ and Frances Myrton [child 2], in the parish of Livingston
2 CONT * 19 November 1771 birth or baptism of Francis Cunynghame, (U) [son] of William Augustus Cunynghame, Baronet/ and Frances Myrton [child 3], in the parish of Livingston
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000545/17700401/008/0025 British Newspaper Archive: The Scots Magazine, April 1770 Page 25]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [Page 24] Mungo Campbell's Trial, continued. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Monday, 26 February 1770. Mr Maclaurin, for the panel, ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Whereupon Mr George Muir, principal clerk of court, being called upon by the court, and asked, "Whether what was set forth by Mr Maclaurin was the truth?" - he informed the court, that after he had made up a lift of assizers in th
2 CONC e usual manner, to be laid before the judges for their consideration, he being [Page 25] then confined to the house by indisposition, Mr John Mackenzie, writer to the signet, agent for the prosecutors, happened to call upon him; a
2 CONC nd he having showed him the list for the jury he had made up, which then consisted of more than forty-five persons, Mr Mackenzie observing the names of Sir William Cunningham and Sir Robert Myreton among the jurymen, told him, he 
2 CONC thought these improper persons to be in the list of jurymen, on account of their near connection with the Earl of Eglinton, the private prosecutor; ''Sir William Cunningham'' being nephew to the Earl of Eglinton, and Sir Robert My
2 CONC reton father-in-law to Sir William. Whereupon he struck out the names of these two gentlemen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000174/18280119/013/0003 British Newspaper Archive: Morning Post Saturday, 19 January 1828 Page 3]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Died, in Saville Row, on Thursday, 17 January 1828 [instant], Sir William Augustus Cunynghame, of Milncraig, Bart.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/D201448 Will of Sir William Augustus Cunynghame of Milncraig, Ayrshire | The National Archives]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://archive.org/details/viewofpoliticals00adamrich/page/34/mode/2up?q=William+Cuninghame View of the political state of Scotland in the last century - a confidential report on the political opinions, family connection
2 CONC s, or personal circumstances of the 2662 county voters in 1788 edited by Sir Charles Elphinstone Adam Published 1887 Page 34]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ayrshire 147. Sir William Cuninghame of Livingstone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Member for Linlithgowshire; but in this County will go with his uncle, Lord Eglinton, in support of Sir Adam Fergusson
