1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Hay,_15th_Earl_of_Erroll Wikipedia: James Hay, 15th Earl of Erroll]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hay, 15th Earl of Erroll (20 April 1726 – Friday, 3 July 1778) styled Lord Boyd from 1728 to 1746, was a Scottish nobleman and the son of William Boyd, 4th Earl of Kilmarnock.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born James Boyd at Falkirk on 20 April 1726. James was the eldest son of William Boyd, 4th Earl of Kilmarnock and Lady Anne Livingston, and from 1728 to 1746, he was known by the courtesy title of Lord Boyd while his father
2 CONC  was Earl of Kilmarnock.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was only daughter of James Livingston, 5th Earl of Linlithgow, a Jacobite attainted for his role in the 1715 Rising, and Lady Margaret Hay (the second daughter of John Hay, 12th Earl of Erroll).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Career''
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1745 Jacobite Rebellion his father sided with the Young Pretender, despite both James and his brother William then holding commissions under George II; James in the army, William in the navy. Remaining loyal to the Hano
2 CONC verians, James then served at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Culloden Battle of Culloden 16 April 1746 57.47722, -4.09250], fighting on the opposite side to his father. During the rout following the Jacobite defeat, 
2 CONC the Earl was captured and taken into the government camp, dishevelled and bareheaded, where he was reportedly recognised by James, who placed his own hat upon his father's head. This was the last time they were to meet, as the Ear
2 CONC l was then transported to London where he was tried for treason and executed four months later; forfeiting all his lands and titles and thus depriving James of his inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1751, however, although the Earldom was abolished, James was permitted to inherit the Kilmarnock estates. These included [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dean_Castle Dean Castle, Dean Road, Kilmarnock, Ayrshire KA3 1XB 55.6233, -
2 CONC 4.4839], the former family seat which had been gutted by a fire in 1735. Trying to recoup some of his father's debts (which he had also inherited), James sold the ruined castle to the 13th Earl of Glencairn. From 1751 to 1752, he 
2 CONC served as Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Later life''
2 CONT
2 CONT On Saturday, 19 August 1758, he succeeded his maternal great-aunt, Mary Hay, 14th Countess of Erroll as the 15th Earl of Erroll, simultaneously changing his surname from Boyd to Hay, as he and his descendants were henceforth known
2 CONC . Along with the title Earl of Erroll, he also held the ceremonial hereditary office of Lord High Constable of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1770 and 1774, he served as a Tory Representative Peer in the House of Lords, and from 1770 to 1778, he was Lord of Police for Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Personal life''
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 September 1749, he married Rebecca Lockhart, the daughter of Alexander Lockhart, Lord Covington. Before her death in 1761, they were the parents of one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lady Mary Hay (born 1754), who married Gen. John Scott of Balcomie in 1770, divorced in 1771.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1762, he married Isabella Carr (1747–1808), daughter of Sir William Carr of Etal, Northumberland. Together, they were the parents of twelve children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lady Charlotte Hay (1763–1800), who married Rev. William Holwell, Vicar of Menheniot, in 1797.
2 CONT * Lady Isabella Anne Hay (1765–1793)
2 CONT * Lady Augusta Hay (1766–1822), who married George Boyle, 4th Earl of Glasgow.
2 CONT * George Hay, 16th Earl of Erroll (1767–1798), who married Elizabeth Jemima Blake, the sister of Joseph Blake, 1st Baron Wallscourt.
2 CONT * Lady Harriet Jane Hay (1768–1812)
2 CONT * Lady Margaret Hay (1769–1832), who married Charles Cameron in 1789.
2 CONT * Lady Maria Elizabeth Hay (1771–1804), who married Rev. George Moore, Rector of Wrotham, eldest son of Most. Rev. John Moore, Archbishop of Canterbury, in 1795.
2 CONT * William Hay, 17th Earl of Erroll (1772–1819), who married three times and had eleven children.
2 CONT * Lady Frances Hay (1773–1806)
2 CONT * Lady Flaminia Hay (1774–1821), who married Capt. George James in 1809.
2 CONT * Lady Jemima Hay (1776–1822)
2 CONT * Hon. James Hay (died 1797)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Erroll died on Friday, 3 July 1778 at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Callendar_House Callendar House, Callendar Road, Falkirk, Stirlingshire FK1 1YR 55.99464, -3.76711], aged fifty-two, and was succeeded by his eldest son, Ge
2 CONC orge. His widow died Thursday, 3 November 1808.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Descendants''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Erroll's grandson, William Hay, 18th Earl of Erroll, was created Baron Kilmarnock in the peerage of the United Kingdom in 1831.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000073/17620424/005/0001 British Newspaper Archive: Oxford Journal Saturday, 24 April 1762 Page 1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT We hear a Treaty of Marriage is on Foot between the Right Hon. the Earl of Errol, Lord High Constable of Scotland, and Miss Carr, Daughter of William Carr, Esq; of Etal, in the County of Northumberland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000045/17620809/005/0001 British Newspaper Archive: Caledonian Mercury Monday, 9 August 1762 Page 1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Newcastle, Saturday, 31 July 1762. We are informed, that on Sunday last, 25 July 1762, the Right Honourable, the Earl of Errol, was married at Ford, in Northumberland, to Miss Carr, daughter of William Carr of Etal, Esq., a beauti
2 CONC ful and accomplished young lady, with a fortune of £10,000. [possibly] [https://maps.nls.uk/geo/explore/#zoom=16.8&lat=55.64882&lon=-2.11289&layers=257&b=ESRIWorld&o=100&marker=55.648509,-2.113400 Etal House, Etal, Northumberland 
2 CONC TD12 4TL 55.648509, -2.113400]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000545/17780601/012/0052 British Newspaper Archive: The Scots Magazine June 1778 Page 52 Deaths]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Deaths: Wednesday, 3 June 1778. At Callendar house, James Hay, Earl of Errol, Hereditary Lord High Constable of Scotland. Besides Errol, which came his mother, his Lordship was the representative of two other Earldoms which came h
2 CONC er, Livingston Earl of Linlithgow, and Livingston Earl of Callender, also of Boyd Earl of Kilmarnock, by his father; these three are now extinct by forfeiture. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By his lady, a daughter of Mr Carr of Etal in Northumberland, to whom he was married Tuesday, 10 August 1762, and who survives, he has left issue, three sons and nine daughters. He is succeeded in honours and estate by his eldest 
2 CONC son George Lord Hay, now at Harrow school. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coronation_of_George_III_and_Charlotte Coronation of George III and Charlotte, Westminster Abbey, London, 22 September 1761] [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westminster_Abbey Westminster Abbey, Dean's
2 CONC  Yard, London, SW1P 3PA 51.49944, -0.12750] His Lordship was accounted the handsomest man in the island. — "At his present Majesty's coronation, he attended in his robe, and by accident neglected to take off his cap when the King 
2 CONC entered. He apologised for his negligence in the most respectful manner; but his Majesty, with great complacency, entreated him to be covered, for he looked upon his presence at the solemnity as a very particular honour; and the E
2 CONC arl actually continued during the solemnity with his cap upon his head."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0003222/17780616/006/0002 British Newspaper Archive: Chester Courant Tuesday, 16 June 1778 Page Saturday Night's Post: London June 11:James Hay, Earl of Errol]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hay, Earl of Errol, Baron Hay, of Stanes, and Hereditary High Constable of Scotland, whose death is mentioned in the preceding page, was the eldest son of William Boyd, late Earl of Kilmarnock, (beheaded for high treason, in
2 CONC  1746,) and then called Lord Boyd, by his wife Lady Anne, daughter of James, Earl of Linlithgow and Callendar, by his wife the Lady Margaret, youngest sister of Charles 14th Earl of Errol, and succeeded his mother, the Countess of
2 CONC  Errol, in 1747, when he took the surname of Hay, and at the coronation of the present King, officiated as Lord High Constable of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Rebecca Lockhart, Daughter of Alexander Lockhart, Esq. by whom he had one daughter, Lady Mary Hay. Her ladyship dying in 1764, he married secondly, Miss Carr, daughter of William Carr, of Etal, in Northumberland, Lady C
2 CONC harlotte, and a son, born in May 1767, to whom his Majesty, by proxy, stood Godfather. His Lordship has left two brothers, Charles and William.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/273066928/james-hay findagrave: Lord Boyd James Hay (1726-1778)]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Name: James Hay
2 CONT * Born: 20 April 1726, Falkirk, Scotland
2 CONT * Died: Wednesday, 3 June 1778 (aged 52), Falkirk, Scotland
2 CONT * Buried: [Abbey of Holyrood, Canongate, Edinburgh EH8 8DX 55.95315, -3.17160], Scotland
2 CONT * Plot: The King's Palace of Holyroodhouse
2 CONT * About:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hay was the 15th Earl of Erroll; Lord High Constable of Scotland; Rector of the University of Glasgow; an MP; Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of Scotland; and Lord of Police for Scotland. He was styled Lord Boyd. He was the 
2 CONC son of William Boyd, 4th Earl of Kilmarnock. Lord Boyd was born and died at Callendar House, Falkirk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1758 James inherited the Earldom of Errol from his maternal great-aunt, Mary Hay, 14th Countess of Erroll (Memorial 163940587). As Earl of Erroll, he changed his surname from Boyd to Hay, as he and his descendants were hencefor
2 CONC th to be known.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 September 1749, he married Rebecca Lockhart, the daughter of Alexander Lockhart. Their daughter was:
2 CONT #Lady Mary Hay (b. 1754), who married Gen. John Scott of Balcomie in 1770.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1762, he married Isabella Carr (1747–1808), daughter of Sir William Carr of Etal. Their children included :
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lady Charlotte Hay (1763–1800), who married Rev. William Holwell, Vicar.
2 CONT # Lady Isabella Anne Hay (1765–1793)
2 CONT # Lady Augusta Hay (1766–1822), who married George Boyle, 4th Earl of Glasgow
2 CONT # George Hay, 16th Earl of Erroll (1767–1798), who married Elizabeth Jemima Blake
2 CONT # Lady Harriet Jane Hay (1768–1812)
2 CONT # Lady Margaret Hay (1769–1832), who married Charles Cameron in 1789.
2 CONT # Lady Maria Elizabeth Hay (1771–1804), who married Rev. George Moore, son of Most. Rev. John Moore, Archbishop of Canterbury, in 1795.
2 CONT # William Hay, 17th Earl of Erroll (1772–1819), who married three times
2 CONT # Lady Frances Hay (1773–1806)
2 CONT # Lady Flaminia Hay (1774–1821), who married Capt. George James in 1809.
2 CONT # Lady Jemima Hay (1776–1822)
2 CONT # Hon. James Hay (d. 1797)
2 CONT
