1 NOTE {geni:about_me} notę by Natalia Gurko:
2 CONT
2 CONT =Katarzyna Poniatowska of the Ciołek coat of arms was the daughter of Kazimierz Poniatowski, Grand Chamberlain of the Crown, and Apolonia Ustrzycka.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a niece of King Stanisław August Poniatowski and his sister Izabela Branicka, née Poniatowska, who played a special role in young Katarzyna’s life. According to specialists from the Museum of King Jan III’s Palace at Wilan
2 CONC ów, Katarzyna was under Izabela’s care from the age of five. Much of her childhood and youth was spent with her aunt and at her court in Białystok. Izabela was especially attached to her young ward, and their relationship became m
2 CONC uch closer than the usual bond between an aunt and niece. This may have been connected with the fact that Izabela had no children of her own.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Branicki court in Białystok was one of the most important magnate courts in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. Izabela paid great attention to the education and upbringing of her niece, who remained under her care.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarzyna died at a very young age. Her early death was a great personal loss for Izabela. A publication by the Wilanów Palace Museum states that the death of her young ward left Izabela in despair. The strength of their bond can 
2 CONC also be seen in a decision Izabela made later in life: she wished to be buried beside Katarzyna after her own death. Her wish was fulfilled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarzyna was buried in the Founders’ Crypt of the old Parish Church of the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary in Białystok. During archaeological research in the crypt in 1983, her richly decorated Rococo-style coffin was disc
2 CONC overed. It bore the gilded Ciołek coat of arms of the Poniatowski family and an elegium, a commemorative funerary inscription made on a gilded metal plate. These elements allowed the researchers to identify Katarzyna’s burial.
2 CONT
2 CONT In their publication on the archaeology of Białystok, Małgorzata and Maciej Karczewski give a Polish translation of the Latin text of the elegium, prepared by Professor Jerzy Urwanowicz:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Katarzyna Poniatowska, daughter of His Highness the Prince, Grand Chamberlain of the Crown, a model of the beauty of her sex, an example of the virtue of royal blood, taken from the earth and given to heaven. She had barely begun
2 CONC  her life, to the comfort of all, when she was already deprived of it, to the sorrow of all. On 1 April in the Year of Our Lord 1773, in the seventeenth year of her flourishing life.”
2 CONT
2 CONT This inscription is especially valuable for Katarzyna’s biography because it was placed directly on her coffin. It not only gives a date but also shows how she was remembered: as a young member of the royal family whose early deat
2 CONC h was seen as a loss to everyone around her.
2 CONT
2 CONT The date on the funerary elegium, 1 April 1773, differs from 17 March 1772, the date given in the Polski Słownik Biograficzny. The authors of the archaeological publication also note this difference but do not explain its cause. S
2 CONC ince the primary source for the date 17 March 1772 has not yet been found, while 1 April 1773 appears directly on Katarzyna’s funerary inscription, the date recorded in this profile is 1 April 1773. The question of the conflicting
2 CONC  dates remains open.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarzyna’s exact place of death has not yet been established by documentary evidence. It is reasonable to suppose that she died in Białystok, probably at Branicki Palace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Izabela kept the memory of Katarzyna throughout the rest of her life. When Izabela died in Białystok in 1808, she was buried, according to her wish, beside her beloved ward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on the following publications:
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarzyna Król, Dobra, szlachetna, światła. Izabela z Poniatowskich Branicka (1730-1808), Muzeum Pałacu Króla Jana III w Wilanowie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Małgorzata Karczewska, Maciej Karczewski, Archeologia Białegostoku, section on burials and the Founders’ Crypt, including photographs of Katarzyna Poniatowska’s coffin and elegium; Latin inscription translated by Prof. Jerzy Urwan
2 CONC owicz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zofia Zielińska, Poniatowski Kazimierz h. Ciołek, Polski Słownik Biograficzny, vol. XXVII.
