1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jan Rustem (also known as Jonas Rustemas; 1762–1835) was a prominent painter and educator of Armenian-French descent who became one of the most influential figures in the 19th-century Vilnius art world. He is prima
2 CONC rily celebrated as the leading portraitist of the Polish-Lithuanian nobility and intelligentsia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Early Life & Arrival:''' Born in Constantinople (now Istanbul), he was brought to the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth as an orphan around 1774 by Prince Adam Kazimierz Czartoryski.
2 CONT * '''Academic Leadership:''' For decades, he was a cornerstone of Vilnius University, serving as a professor of sketching (1811) and painting (1819). He taught a generation of Romantic artists, including the famous Ukrainian poet 
2 CONC Taras Shevchenko.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Artistic Legacy:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Portraits: He painted nearly all the notable figures of his time in Vilnius, including members of the Radziwiłł and Oginski families. 
2 CONT * "Fantasy Cards": One of his most unique contributions was his set of "fantasy cards" (kartušiukai), where playing card suits were integrated into intricate drawings. 
2 CONT * Theater & Society: Beyond canvas, he designed costumes and scenery for the Vilnius Theater and organized popular "living pictures" for social gatherings. 
2 CONT * Personal Trademark: He was often seen wearing a distinctive red Turkish fez, which became a signature of his "exotic" appearance to contemporaries.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rustem died in 1835 and is buried in the crypt of a church near the Dūkštas manor in modern-day Lithuania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_Rustem Wikipedia: Jan Rustem]
