1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Gordon, 9th Marquess of Huntly KT (28 June 1761 – 17 June 1853), styled Lord Strathavon until 1795 and known as The Earl of Aboyne from 1795 to 1836, was a Scottish peer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Huntly was the son of Charles Gordon, 4th Earl of Aboyne, and Margaret, daughter of Alexander Stewart, 6th Earl of Galloway. After succeeding to the title of Earl of Aboyne in 1794 after the death of his father, he also succeeded 
2 CONC to the title of Marquess of Huntly after his distant cousin, the 5th Duke of Gordon, died in 1836.
2 CONT
2 CONT Orton Hall, Peterborough, Cambridgeshire (now a hotel) was the seat of the Marquess of Huntly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Huntly was a keen cricketer who made four known appearances in first-class matches from 1785 to 1792 (he was styled Lord Strathavon on the scorecards). He was a member of the White Conduit Club and an early member of Marylebone Cr
2 CONC icket Club (MCC), but he was mainly associated with Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 April 1791, he married Catherine Cope, daughter of Sir Charles Cope, 2nd Baronet, and they had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Charles Gordon, 10th Marquess of Huntly (1792–1863)
2 CONT # Catherine Susan Gordon, Lady Chesham (1792–1866), married Charles Cavendish, 1st Baron Chesham
2 CONT # Reverend Lord George Gordon (1794–1862)
2 CONT # Admiral Lord John Frederick Gordon-Hallyburton (1799–1878)
2 CONT # Major Lord Henry Gordon (1802–65)
2 CONT # Cecil James Gordon (1806–78)
2 CONT # Lady Mary Gordon (d. 1825)
2 CONT # Lt.-Col. Francis Arthur Gordon (1808–57)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wikipedia contributors. "<em>[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Gordon,_9th_Marquess_of_Huntly George Gordon, 9th Marquess of Huntly]</em>." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia.
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft#page/112/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. I, page 105
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft#page/558/mode/2up Th Scots peerage], Vol. IV, page 559
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT George Gordon married Catherine Cope on April 4, 1791, when he was 29 years old. He later had a relationship with Ann Cole Eliott resulting in 5 children. See: https://www.genealogieonline.nl/en/baker-england-roots/P3313.php 
2 CONT
2 CONT GORDON, GEORGE, ninth Marquis of Huntly (1761–1853), son and heir of Charles, fourth earl of Aboyne, and Lady Margaret Stewart, third daughter of Alexander, sixth Earl of Galloway, was born at Edinburgh on 28 June 1761. When Lord 
2 CONC Strathaven he entered the army as ensign in the 1st regiment of foot guards, and was promoted in 1777 to a company in the 81st highland regiment of foot. In 1780 he was one of the aides-de-camp to the Earl of Carlisle, then lord-l
2 CONC ieutenant of Ireland. In 1782 he had a troop in the 9th regiment of dragoons, and in March 1783 he was constituted major of an independent corps of foot, which was reduced at the peace of 1784. He was promoted lieutenant-colonel o
2 CONC f the 35th foot in 1789, but exchanged with Lieutenant-colonel Lennox (subsequently Duke of Richmond) for his company in the Coldstream guards, after a dispute between the latter and his royal highness the Duke of York, then colon
2 CONC el of the Coldstreams. Lord Strathaven quitted the army in 1792, and was appointed colonel of the Aberdeenshire militia in 1798. He succeeded his father as Earl of Aboyne 28 Dec. 1794. At the general election of 1796 he was return
2 CONC ed to parliament as one of the sixteen representatives of the peerage of Scotland. He was again chosen in 1802, 1807, and 1812. On 11 Aug. 1815 he was created a peer of the United Kingdom by the title of Baron Meldrum of Morven, a
2 CONC nd thenceforward took his seat in the House of Lords in his own right. He was made a knight of the Thistle in 1827. In 1836, on the extinction of the male line of the elder branch of his family by the death of George, fifth duke o
2 CONC f Gordon [q. v.], he succeeded to the dignities of marquis and earl of Huntly. He was a tory in politics, and voted in the majority for Lord Lyndhurst's motion on the Reform Bill, which led to the temporary resignation of Earl Gre
2 CONC y's ministry on 7 May 1832. The marquis married in 1791 Catherine, second daughter of Sir Charles Cope, and with this lady he acquired the estate of Orton Longueville, Huntingdonshire, which he very considerably enlarged by purcha
2 CONC sing in 1803 the two adjoining parishes of Chesterton and Haddon. The marquis died at his residence in Chapel Street, Grosvenor Square, London, on 17 June 1853. He left a family of six sons and three daughters, and was succeeded b
2 CONC y his eldest son, the tenth marquis, who sat for some years in the House of Commons, first for East Grinstead, and afterwards for Huntingdonshire. Source: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Gordon,_George_(1761-1853)_(DNB00)
