1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.masshist.org/collection-guides/view/fa0343
2 CONT
2 CONT >Edward Hutchinson Robbins (1758-1829), son of Nathaniel Robbins and Elizabeth Hutchinson, was born in Milton, Mass. Graduating from Harvard College in 1775, he became a lawyer, a delegate to the Massachusetts Constitutional Conve
2 CONC ntion, and a state representative. After serving as speaker of the Massachusetts House of Representatives from 1793 to 1802, he became lieutenant governor of Massachusetts under Governor Caleb Strong from 1802 to 1806. In 1811 he 
2 CONC was appinted judge of probate for Norfolk County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Robbins married Elizabeth Murray (1756-1837), daughter of James Murray and Barbara Bennet, in 1785, and had five daughters and two sons, one of whom was James Murray Robbins (1796-1885). He invested in the purchase and settlement
2 CONC  of lands in Passamaquoddy, now in Maine, and his name is perpetuated in the town of Robbinston on the St. Croix River.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * [https://collections.mfa.org/objects/34638 Museum of Fine Arts, Boston]  Mrs. Edward Hutchinson Robbins (Elizabeth Murray) By Chester Harding (American, 1792–1866) 1827 MEDIUM/TECHNIQUE Oil on canvas DIMENSIONS 91.44 x 71.12 cm 
2 CONC (36 x 28 in.) CREDIT LINE Gift of Margaret A. Revere and Anna P. Revere ACCESSION NUMBER 1984.503 NOT ON VIEW COLLECTIONS Americas CLASSIFICATIONS Paintings PROVENANCE The artist; to Judge and Mrs. Edward Hutchinson Robbins, the s
2 CONC itter and her husband; to James M. Robbins, their son; to Edward H. R. Revere, 1898; to Margaret A. and Anna P. Revere, his nieces and great-great granddaughters of the sitter, 1952, to MFA, 1984, gift of Margaret A. Revere and An
2 CONC na P. Revere.
