1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000222126594846
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126424888&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dutch Authorities agreed to the appointment of a Resident-Agent for the English East India Company (EEIC) at the Cape, whose task would be to arrange supplies for St. Helena where the EEIC were stationed at the time as point o
2 CONC f refreshment on the way to the East. "Appointed to this post soon afterwards, John Pringle took up his duties at the Cape in April, 1794.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126669884&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126858822&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126484945&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126500000&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126753869&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pringle dies in June 1815 at 46 years old 
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222126745841&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Samkin, G. (2010). Trader sailor spy: The case of John Pringle and the transfer of accounting technology to the Cape of Good Hope. Accounting History, 15(4), 505–528. doi:10.1177/1032373210373132 
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Pringle, the illegitimate son of John Pringle was born to Elizabeth Taylor on 3 February 1769 in Edinburgh (IOR/J/1/12, p.40). Pringle’s grandfather, John Pringle, represented Scotland (1707–8) and Selkirkshire (1708–29) as 
2 CONC a member of parliament in Westminster (Furber, 1931; Sedgwick, 1970), before being raised to the bench of the Court of Session as Lord Haining (Arkin, 1960; Hancock, 2005). Pringle’s father, the younger son of Lord Haining, was in
2 CONC volved in the Madeira wine trade (Hancock, 2005) where he amassed “an ample fortune” (Pringle, 1933, p.175) before entering parliament in June 1765 where he represented Selkirkshire until 1786.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information about Pringle’s early life is sketchy. Although records of his early schooling no longer remain, the existence of schools in every Scottish parish (Mepham, 1988) would suggest that his formative education occurred ther
2 CONC e. After his early schooling, Pringle obtained a commercial education from the Uxbridge Academy in West London,2 run from the Uxbridge Common home of its principal, the Reverend William Rutherford. While the length of time Pringle
2 CONC  spent at the Uxbridge Academy is uncertain, Mepham (1988, p.19) explains that commercial training provided by mercantile schools in Dundee and Aberdeen took three years. It is, therefore, reasonable to assume that the training pe
2 CONC riod at the Uxbridge Academy was similar in length. After his commercial training, Pringle applied to the EIC for a position as a writer. Pringle’s application followed a well-established format that had existed since 1682 (Foster
2 CONC , 1926). When supplying the requisite certificate to the EIC, Rutherford was able to make the following observations on Pringle’s scholastic ability: These certify that Mr John Pringle at my school and under my care went this a re
2 CONC gular course of Arithmetic and Bookkeeping and made commendable progress in other branches of education (IOR/J/1/12, p.38). Pringle’s petition to the Court of Directors was read on 4 March 1789.3 He emphasized his education in wri
2 CONC ting and accounts and “humbly hopes himself qualified to serve your Hons. Abroad” (IOR/J/1/12, p.38).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early employment with the East India Company and appointment to the Cape'''
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pringle served as a writer in Bombay from 1789 to 1793, before returning to England on the grounds of ill-health. Shortly after his return to England, Pringle was appointed agent to the Cape by the EIC’s Secret Committee (see
2 CONC  Philips [1940] for a discussion of the functioning of this committee)..."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the agent from 1794 to 1815
2 CONT
2 CONT ============================
