1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/77272520/william-graham
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://www.geni.com/projects/Graham-Name-Study-Williamsburg-South-Carolina/55785 Graham Name Study, Williamsburg, South Carolina]
2 CONT
2 CONT A Patriot of the American Revolution for SOUTH CAROLINA with the rank of LIEUTENANT.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A046836  DAR Ancestor # A046836]
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Grahams of Horry County" by Harmon Dock Graham:
2 CONT
2 CONT ....the first John Graham had a son named William Graham (1748-1824), brother of the second John Graham mentioned above. He was married to Elizabeth Bellamy and one of their sons was William Bellamy Graham. The following is a copy
2 CONC  of an article in a Georgetown, South Carolina newspaper, telling of his (William Bellamy's) untimely death.
2 CONT
2 CONT WINYAH OBSERVER Georgetown, S. C., \Vednesday, November 25, 1846 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Casualty - - Mr. William B. Graham, of Horry, came to town on Thursday last in a four oared boat to procure some family supplies, and departed again at an early hour the same evening. The wind was high and his boat so heavily lad
2 CONC ed that she filled after getting into the bay near the old fort and immediately capsized. There were five persons in the boat and of the number a stranger by the name of Smith, who was allowed his passage up the Waccamaw, on his w
2 CONC ay to Richmond, Virginia. Mr. Graham and Mr. Smith were drowned. The other white men and two negroes were saved. Mr. Graham was a very respectable and influential citizen of Horry, and has left surviving him a wife and seventeen c
2 CONC hildren. Three or four of his sons came down the river on Sabbath last in search of his body. Our bay is a dangerous sheet of water in bad weather, and it should not be attempted to be crossed in any other than large and good boat
2 CONC s. The annual casualties should be a warning to strangers and all." Note: William Bellamy Graham's body was found and he was buried in the Old Graham Cemetery on Mitchell Swamp." 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Graham, Sr., b. Dec-03-1748, Horry County, South Carolina. He married Elizabeth Bellamy, in Horry County, South Carolina, b. Horry County, South Carolina, (daughter of John Bellamy and Elizabeth Jordan) d. Horry County, So
2 CONC uth Carolina, buried: Old Graham Cemetery, near Mitchell. William died Jul-14-1824, Horry County, South Carolina, buried: Old Graham Cemetery, near Mitchell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT * William Bellamy Graham b. Oct-11-1792. 
2 CONT * Susannah Graham b. 1800. 
2 CONT * Abraham J. Graham b. Mar-08-1801. 
2 CONT * Elizabeth Ann Rebecca Graham, b. Horry County, South Carolina. She married James C. McQueen, in Horry County, South Carolina. Elizabeth died Horry County, South Carolina. 
2 CONT * Peggy Ann Graham, b. Horry County, South Carolina. 
