1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT The Petition of Stephen Barfield of the County of Bladen - Humbly 
2 CONT
2 CONT representeth That your Petitioner has two illegitimate Sons bearing the names 
2 CONT
2 CONT of Jesse Hatcher and Allen Somerset who is now both minors, and is desirous 
2 CONT
2 CONT that their names should be alterd to that of Jesse Barfield and Allen Barfield 
2 CONT
2 CONT which your petitioner prays may be done by legislative Authority, by having an 
2 CONT
2 CONT Act of the General Assembly pass'd for that purpose, and their names confirmd 
2 CONT
2 CONT under the same Regulations that all other names similiar to those is done by 
2 CONT
2 CONT your Honorable body and your Petitioner as in duty bound will every pray. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st Nov. 1800 Stephen Barfield 
2 CONT
2 CONT On reverse: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen Barfield 
2 CONT
2 CONT Petition 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the House of Commons 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Decem'r 1800 read & referred 
2 CONT
2 CONT to the Comm'e appointed to 
2 CONT
2 CONT Consolodate the Several Bills 
2 CONT
2 CONT for alteration of names 
2 CONT
2 CONT By order 
2 CONT
2 CONT J Hunt CHC 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Senate Decm'r 1st. 1800 
2 CONT
2 CONT Read & refered as 
2 CONT
2 CONT by the House of Commons 
2 CONT
2 CONT By order 
2 CONT
2 CONT M Stokes Clk 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1779 Stephen received a grant of three hundred acres of land located on the North Side of Gapway Swamp in Bladen County. Gapway Swamp is located in the extreme western part of present day Columbus County, near what was formerly
2 CONC  the Old Georgetown District of South Carolina, now Horry County, South Carolina. During the Revolution this particular area was a hotbed of war activity. Many other Barfields lived in the area as well. (Source: Bladen County Heri
2 CONC tage Book) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Federal Census of 1800 lists Stephen as being Captain Stephen "living in Captain Barfield's district" of Bladen indicating he was in the Militia. Stephen had died by 1823, leaving four minor children, Gabriel, Hiram, Nancy and
2 CONC  Susan who were reared by Shadrach Howell of Howellsville, Robeson County, N. C. Stephen had six children. (Source: Jimmy D. Howell)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://digitalcommons.coastal.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1148&context=irq
2 CONT
2 CONT Independent Republic Quarterly, 2008, Vol. 42, No. 1-4 Horry County Historical Society
2 CONT
2 CONT Extensive information about the Johnstone family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cites name of husband of Isabella Johnstone as "Luke Barefield." From the IRQ citation above:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabell Johnston (sister of Gilbert II) married Luke Barfield (1730-1788), and two of their children are known: Stephen Barfield (1750-1823) and Susannah Barfield (1760-unknown). One land grant, dated 12 November 1779, was made to
2 CONC  Luke Barfield for 300 acres on the south side of Gapway, joining the "Provence Line." In the same year, Stephen Barfield received a grant of 300 acres of land located on the north side of Gapway Swamp in Bladen County and another
2 CONC  grant for 100 acres on both sides of Gapway Swamp. The 1790 census of Wilmington District, Bladen County, North Carolina, shows Stephen Barfield living alone and owning six slaves. He was a captain in the militia and became polit
2 CONC ically active in Columbus County (formed in 1808), North Carolina, where county documents state that court was ordered to be held at his home until the completion of a county courthouse. North Carolina General Assembly Records, Bo
2 CONC x 3, dated Nov/Dec 1800, contains the record of a petition made by Stephen Barfield of the County of Bladen to change the names of his two illegitimate sons to Jesse Barfield and Allen Barfield. Son, Jesse, was known as "Jesse Hat
2 CONC cher,” and son, Allen, was known as "Allen Summersett." Stephen Barfield later married Allen's mother, Mrs. Frances Summersett. Frances was deceased by 1810 when Stephen Barfield married a woman named Sarah with whom he had four c
2 CONC hildren. Stephen died by 1823 in Robeson County, North Carolina, leaving his four minor children: Susannah, Nancy, Hiram and Gabriel, who were reared by Shadrach Howell of Howellsville, Robeson County, North Carolina. Stephen Barf
2 CONC ield's sister Susannah married her first cousin, Hugo Johnston, and convinced her father-in-law Gilbert Johnston II to write down information in 1790 detailing family information.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
