1 NAME Francis Wemyss /Charteris of Amisfield/
2 GIVN Francis Wemyss
2 SURN Charteris of Amisfield
2 _MARNM Charteris Douglas
1 NAME Francis Wemyss Charteris, 8th Earl of Wemyss
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 15 APR 1772
1 DEAT
2 DATE 28 JUN 1853
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Francis Douglas, 8th Earl of Wemyss
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Wemyss Charteris Douglas, 8th Earl of Wemyss, 4th Earl of March (15 April 1772 – 28 June 1853), known as the Earl of March from 1810 to 1826 and as the Earl of Wemyss and March from 1826 to 1853, was a Scottish peer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wemyss was the son of Francis Wemyss Charteris, Lord Elcho (1749-1808), and the grandson of Francis Charteris, de jure 7th Earl of Wemyss. In 1810 he succeeded his second cousin twice removed William Douglas, 4th Duke of Queensber
2 CONC ry and 3rd Earl of March to the Earldom of March, as the lineal heir male of the aforementioned Lady Anne Douglas, sister of the first Earl of March. In 1821 he was created Baron Wemyss, of Wemyss in the County of Fife, in the Pee
2 CONC rage of the United Kingdom, which entitled him to an automatic seat in the House of Lords. In 1826 he obtained a reversal of the attainder of the earldom of Wemyss and became the eighth Earl of Wemyss as well. From 1821 to 1853 he
2 CONC  served as Lord-Lieutenant of Peeblesshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 31 May 1794, he married Margaret Campbell and they had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Charlotte Charteris (d. 1886) Lady Louisa Antoinetta Charteris (d. 1854) Lady Harriet Charteris (d. 1858) Lady Eleanor Charteris (1796–1832) Francis Wemyss-Charteris, 9th Earl of Wemyss (1796–1883) Hon. Walter Charteris (1797
2 CONC –1818) Lady Margaret Charteris (1800–1825) Lady Katherine Charteris Wemyss (1801–1844)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT This article does not cite any references or sources. Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (August 2008) Francis Wemyss Charteris Douglas, 8th 
2 CONC Earl of Wemyss, 4th Earl of March (15 April 1772 – 28 June 1853), known as the Earl of March from 1810 to 1826 and as the Earl of Wemyss and March from 1826 to 1853, was a Scottish peer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wemyss was the son of Francis Wemyss Charteris, Lord Echo (1749-1808), and the grandson of Francis Charteris, de jure 7th Earl of Wemyss. In 1810 he succeeded his second cousin twice removed William Douglas, 4th Duke of Queensberr
2 CONC y and 3rd Earl of March to the Earldom of March, as the lineal heir male of the aforementioned Lady Anne Douglas, sister of the first Earl of March. In 1821 he was created Baron Wemyss, of Wemyss in the County of Fife, in the Peer
2 CONC age of the United Kingdom, which entitled him to an automatic seat in the House of Lords. In 1826 he obtained a reversal of the attainder of the earldom of Wemyss and became the eighth Earl of Wemyss as well. From 1821 to 1853 he 
2 CONC served as Lord-Lieutenant of Peeblesshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 31 May 1794, he married Margaret Campbell and they had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Charlotte Charteris (d. 1886) Lady Louisa Antoinetta Charteris (d. 1854) Lady Harriet Charteris (d. 1858) Lady Eleanor Charteris (1796–1832) Francis Wemyss-Charteris, 9th Earl of Wemyss (1796–1883) Hon. Walter Charteris (1797
2 CONC –1818) Lady Margaret Charteris (1800–1825) Lady Katherine Charteris Wemyss (1801–1844)
2 CONT
