1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Updated from [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-40001/familysearch-family-tree?itemId=882016684&action=showRecord FamilySearch Family Tree] via father [http://www.geni.com/profile-34647930921 Giacomo 
2 CONC Fagnani] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 8 2015, 18:29:51 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Seymour-Conway,_Marchioness_of_Hertford
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Emilia Seymour-Conway, Marchioness of Hertford (born Maria Emilia Fagnani; 24 August 1771 – 2 March 1856), nicknamed Mie-Mie, was a British noblewoman of Italian descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Parentage
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate daughter of Costanza Brusati, by marriage Marchesa Fagnani, her true paternity remained surrounded in controversy.
2 CONT
2 CONT During their long trips in northern Europe, Marchese Giacomo II Fagnani and his wife Costanza Brusati met in England Henry Herbert, 10th Earl of Pembroke, an English aristocrat well-known for his amorous intrigues with Italian wom
2 CONC en. The three began to travel together, and the Earl of Pembroke began a secret affair with the Marchesa Fagnani.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 1769 Costanza Brusati (who left her husband) and the Earl of Pembroke arrived to London, where he introduced her to a friend, William Douglas, Earl of March, who was among the best-known bon vivants of the reign o
2 CONC f King George III and was known as "Old Q" because that letter was painted on the door of his carriage. Soon after, the Earl of March and Costanza Brusati also began a love affair.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 August 1771 at the White's club of St James's, the Earl of March sent a letter to his friend George Selwyn (a prominent politician) that the previous night the Marchesa Fagnani gave birth a daughter named Maria Emilia, and t
2 CONC hat he was the father. Selwyn took custody of the newborn and brought her up as his own daughter, arising rumors that in fact he could be the real father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Custody
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after August 1771, Costanza Brusati returned with her husband who recognized Maria Emilia (known among the family as Mie-Mie) as his own; however, the child remained in England. They later had two children: Federico (born 
2 CONC in 1775) and Antonia Barbara Giulia Faustina Angiola Lucia (nicknamed Antonietta; born in 1778), later by marriage (Signora la) Marchesa Arese.
2 CONT
2 CONT Costanza, to please her parents-in-law Federico II Fagnani and Rosa Clerici, in 1777 asked that the Earl of March intervene to try to return her daughter to Italy. However, he refused to intercede before Selwyn was nevertheless fo
2 CONC rced to hand over Mie-Mie in Paris so that her mother could bring her back to Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1786, after the death of Federico II Fagnani, George Selwyn arrived in Italy to convince Costanza to give him back Mie-Mie. At the end, the Marches accepted, and allowed George Selwyn to appoint her daughter as heiress of his e
2 CONC state; in addition, the Earl of March (since 1778 Duke of Queensberry) left much of his fortune to Maria Emilia when he died in 1810.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 May 1798, Maria Emilia married The Earl of Yarmouth (this being the courtesy style of the son and heir of) the 2nd Marquess of Hertford. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Frances Maria Seymour-Conway (d. 1822).
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Richard Seymour-Conway, 4th Marquess of Hertford (1800–1870).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Henry Seymour-Conway (1805–1859).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that when King George III was insane, he announced that he was going to take Lady Yarmouth as his mistress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Separation from husband and promotion in title
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite her wealth, the Seymour-Conway family never accepted Maria Emilia due to her illegitimate origin, and at the end this affected her marriage: after the birth of her last child, she moved to Paris, where she remained for the
2 CONC  rest of her life. Because she never divorced her husband, when her father-in-law died in 1822 she became the Marchioness of Hertford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT The intricate custody battle for Mie-Mie and especially the controversial existence that she lived for many years in London and Paris had considerable echo in the aristocratic and upper-class society of her time. William Makepeace
2 CONC  Thackery parodied her husband as the Marquess of Steyne in his masterpiece, Vanity Fair.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-40001/familysearch-family-tree?itemId=882016684&action=showRecord FamilySearch Family Tree] via father [http://www.geni.com/profile-34647930921 Giacomo Fagnani] by [htt
2 CONC p://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 8 2015, 18:29:51 UTC''
