1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT <strong>Douglas, Earl of Selkirk, Thomas</strong>. 20/06/1771-08/04/1820. Ref: 436. Male.
2 CONT * Titles and British Honours: Lord Daer 1797-9, 5th Earl of Selkirk (1799), Lord Lieutenant, Stewartry of Kirkcudbright 1807-20.
2 CONT * Place of Birth: St Mary's Isle, Kircudbrightshire.
2 CONT * Place of Death: Pau, France.
2 CONT * Place of interment: Orthez, France.
2 CONT * Profession: Landowner, Originator of Highland Settlements in Canada.
2 CONT * Appointments Held: Representative Peer, 1806-18; 1803 Founded settlement on Prince Edward's Island; 1811 Red River settlement.
2 CONT * Marital Status: Married Jean Wedderburn.
2 CONT * Father: Dunbar Hamilton Douglas 4th.Earl of Selkirk FRSE 1722-99.
2 CONT * Siblings: Basil William Douglas, Lord Daer FRSE 1763-1794.
2 CONT * Other Family: Brother-in-law of Sir James Hall of Dunglass FRSE 1761-1832 and Sir James Montgomery of Stanhope 2nd Baronet, FRSE 1766-1839.
2 CONT * References: DNB 15, 1888, 350-3; Anderson 3, 430-1; The Scots Peerage, 7, 1910, 523; Shapin 418.
2 CONT * Memberships: FRS (1808).
2 CONT * Date of Election: 22/01/1798.
2 CONT * Proposers: Dugald Stewart, Andrew Coventry, John Playfair. (Minute 08/01/1798, NLS Acc10,000/4 & Acc 10,000/12) see notes.
2 CONT * Notes: Proposed as Lord Daer 1798.
2 CONT * Fellow Type: OF.
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://www.royalsoced.org.uk/cms/files/fellows/biographical_index/fells_indexp1.pdf Biographical index of former fellows of the Royal Society of Edinburgh, 1783-2002], pt. 1. A-J, page 265
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * Wikipedia contributors, "<em>[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Douglas,_5th_Earl_of_Selkirk Thomas Douglas, 5th Earl of Selkirk]<em>", Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas Douglas, 5th Earl of Selkirk FRS FRSE was a Scottish peer. He was noteworthy as a Scottish philanthropist who sponsored immigrant settlements in Canada at the Red River Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Saint Mary's Isle, Kirkcudbrightshire, Scotland, the seventh son of Dunbar Douglas, 4th Earl of Selkirk, and his wife Helen Hamilton (1738-1802), granddaughter of Thomas Hamilton, 6th Earl of Haddington. His brother
2 CONC  was Basil William Douglas, Lord Daer.
2 CONT
2 CONT His early education was at the Palgrave Academy, Suffolk. As he had not expected to inherit the family estate, he went to the University of Edinburgh to study to become a lawyer. While there, he noticed poor Scottish crofters who 
2 CONC were being displaced by their landlords. Seeing their plight, he investigated ways he could help them find new land in the then British colonies. In 1794, on the death of his brother Basil, Thomas became Lord Daer. After his fathe
2 CONC r's death in 1799, Douglas, the last surviving son (two brothers died in infancy, two died of tuberculosis and two died of yellow fever), became the 5th Earl of Selkirk.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1798 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of Edinburgh his proposers being Dugald Stewart, Andrew Coventry, and John Playfair.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Thomas unexpectedly inherited the Selkirk title and estates in 1799, he used his money and political connections to purchase land and settle poor Scottish farmers in Belfast, Prince Edward Island, in 1803 and Baldoon, Upper C
2 CONC anada in 1804. In 1804, he was in Halifax and became a member of the North British Society. He travelled extensively in North America, and his approach and work gained him some fame; in 1807 he was named Lord-Lieutenant of Kirkcud
2 CONC bright, Scotland, and in 1808 was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of London.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to continue his work re-settling Scottish farmers, Selkirk asked the British government for a land grant in the Red River Valley, a part of Rupert's Land. The government refused, as the Hudson's Bay Company (HBC) had been
2 CONC  granted a fur trading monopoly on that land. However, Selkirk was very determined, and together with Sir Alexander Mackenzie bought enough shares in HBC to let them gain control of the land. This position of power, along with his
2 CONC  marriage connections (his wife Jean was the sister of Andrew Wedderburn, a member of the HBC governing committee) allowed him to acquire a land grant called Assiniboia to serve as an agricultural settlement for the company. As pa
2 CONC rt of the agreement for the land grant, Selkirk agreed to supply the Hudson's Bay Company with 200 men each year. He also agreed that the settlers would not be allowed to participate in the fur trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT As part owner of HBC, Selkirk also wanted to stop the North West Company (NWC) from competing with HBC for furs in the region. By placing the Red River Colony astride the trade routes used by the NWC coureurs des bois, Selkirk cou
2 CONC ld cut off the easy flow of furs. However, the local Métis people who already inhabited the area had long-standing ties with the NWC, and refused to accept Selkirk's control over the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first colonization attempt started in 1812, consisting of 128 men led by the new governor, Miles Macdonell. Arriving late in the season they had just arrived and built homes when the winter cut off any hope of planting, and th
2 CONC e colony became reliant on the support of the Métis. Even with a full growing season the next year, the colony never thrived. Because of a shortage of food in 1814, Macdonell issued the Pemmican Proclamation, prohibiting the expor
2 CONC t of food from the entire area. The Métis, who made a living selling pemmican to the NWC traders, responded by arresting Macdonell and burning the settlement
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Semple was appointed as governor of the Red River Colony. By 1816, the violence intensified between the Métis and the newcomers, which resulted in the Battle of Seven Oaks, causing the deaths of 21 of Lord Selkirk's men, in
2 CONC cluding the newly appointed governor, and one Métis. NWC partners were accused of having aided the Métis attackers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selkirk and his men responded to the Battle of Seven Oaks by seizing Fort William, a trading post that belonged to the North West Company. In the aftermath, Selkirk was ordered to appear in court in Montreal and was charged with f
2 CONC our separate offences, all of which related to the alleged unlawful occupation of Fort William. Selkirk reportedly spent most of his acquired fortune defending himself in court, shortly before his death in 1820 at Pau, France. The
2 CONC  two companies were merged in 1821.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selkirk's colonizing ambitions have been memorialized in the names of the City of Selkirk and the Village of East Selkirk, as well as the Winnipeg neighborhood of Point Douglas, the city's Fort Douglas Park on Waterfront Drive (wh
2 CONC ere Fort Douglas once stood) and Winnipeg's Selkirk Avenue. The City of Selkirk is served by the Lord Selkirk Regional Comprehensive Secondary School, which is administered by the Lord Selkirk School Division.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Métis peoples cite Lord Selkirk's intrusion as the period in time their identity as a people came into existence. The Métis existed prior to the confrontations with Lord Selkirk's men but their armed resistance to foreign encr
2 CONC oachment became a rallying point for their shared identity. A flag and a national anthem were born during this period in time. A Manitoba Historical Plaque was erected in Winnipeg, Manitoba by the province to commemorate Lord Selk
2 CONC irk's role in Manitoba's heritage.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of seven, Thomas was almost kidnapped by John Paul Jones.
2 CONT In 1778, John Paul Jones, in the sloop Ranger, was cruising between Scotland and Ireland looking for prizes. Benjamin Franklin had suggested that he might capture a British nobleman to exchange for American prisoners. Having been 
2 CONC born near the Selkirk estates, Jones selected the elder Lord Selkirk.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the last moment, Jones decided not to go himself, but to assign the duty to two lieutenants and a boatload of sailors. As the Americans approached the Selkirk mansion, a governess saw them coming and removed young Thomas to saf
2 CONC ety. The Americans knocked on the front door and were greeted by the butler. Lady Selkirk came from the breakfast room to see what the fuss was about. She invited the American officers into the drawing room, told the butler to mak
2 CONC e tea and to find some whisky for the sailors who were waiting outside. When they explained that they had come to kidnap her husband, Lady Selkirk replied that unfortunately Lord Selkirk was not at home. When Lieutenant Wallingfor
2 CONC d suggested that instead they might take the young gentleman they saw on the way to the house, Lady Selkirk replied that they would have to kill her first. After more discussion Lady Selkirk suggested that, so that their mission w
2 CONC ould not be a complete failure, they might steal the family silver. The officers allowed as how that might be the best solution, so Lady Selkirk ordered the butler to provide the American gentlemen with what they needed. He filled
2 CONC  a sack half full of coal, filled the top half with silverware and presented it to the officers. After drinking a toast to Lady Selkirk, they returned to their ship and presented their captain with his sack full of coal and silver
2 CONC ware.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jones wrote Lady Selkirk a flowery letter of apology, proposing himself to buy back the booty from the Navy and return it to the Selkirks. Lord Selkirk wrote back that he could not possibly countenance the return of his silver wit
2 CONC hout the consent of the Continental Congress. The objects, which became the subject of protracted legal negotiations, were returned seven years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Selkirk married Jean Wedderburn-Colville, sister to Andrew Colville, in 1807, and had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunbar Douglas, 6th Earl of Selkirk (1809-1885) Lady Isabella Helen Douglas (1811-1893), married Charles Hope Lady Katherine Jean Douglas (1816-1863), married Loftus Wigram
2 CONT
2 CONT On his death in Canada, his heir and successor Dunbar was only 10 years old and thus Selkirk's estates were put into trust and managed by four executors named in his will. The Board of Trustees consisted of Andrew Colville (Colvil
2 CONC e) of Achiltrie and Crommie, John Hallbrith (Halkett) of Waring, Adam Maitland of Dundrennan, and Sir James Montgomery, 2nd Baronet.
