2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p10/2002/2580/53444838046e25c0/200px-SirWilliamForbes6thBt_original.jpg?hash=5ae3bcdd44792b0555e0f1b2db1abbacf5aac91b013056a0e639f5e7c751f6da.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE "Sir William was born in Edinburgh and served an apprenticeship with the banking firm Coutts. He derived considerable wealth from his career as an influential banker and made donations to charitable institutions and individuals in
3 CONC  Edinburgh. He is shown wearing the order of the baronetcy of Nova Scotia, a title inherited from his father and still worn by the Forbes descendents today. Among his friends were the writers Sir Walter Scott and Samuel Johnson, t
3 CONC he philosopher Edmund Burke and Sir Joshua Reynolds, who painted this portrait."
3 CONT
3 CONT https://www.nationalgalleries.org/art-and-artists/2460/sir-william-forbes-pitsligo-1739-1806-banker-and-author
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/40/71/9c/52/5344483af8b7b047/1_102_original.jpg?hash=3b427f6666aeedd9f3cda8d6e3ac812a398d66a32a845af4dfda24c963f940c9.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL CII. - The Right. Hon. The Earl of Haddington, Sir William Forbes of Pitsligo, Bart., and Sir James Hunter Blair, Bart.
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/36/55/84/b1/5344483af89f666f/1_076_original.jpg?hash=f5bb3d1a691443b4a9b99c613dee2c41a8514dfc582ec66fa4e12f77a1346dca.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL LXXVI. - Sir William Forbes of Pitsligo, Banker in Edinburgh.
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/10/ff/5a/46/53444838aec3c8b5/sir_william_forbes_of_pitsligo_and_monymusk_sixth_baronet_original.jpg?hash=d9e0d12f3a5bfae0cbf3789da18ef85a694de6cc4685556a064a07b0a5b0a251.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 NOTE Sir William Forbes, sixth baronet of Pitsligo (1739-1806) was a banker, author and generous philanthropist. Beginning his career as an apprentice to the Coutts brothers in Edinburgh, reportedly spending only one night out of Edinb
3 CONC urgh during his first five years, he was fully admitted to the firm in 1763. Forbes became an influential voice in the Merchant’s Company of Edinburgh and frequently acted as financial advisor to William Pitt the Younger (1759-180
3 CONC 6), who offered him several parliamentary seats and, in 1799, an Irish peerage, but Forbes refused these honours. His paternal grandmother, Mary Forbes, was a sister of Alexander, 4th Lord Forbes of Pitsligo (1678-1762) (see lot 1
3 CONC 09), attainted after the Jacobite rising of 1745, and Forbes made it is his duty to restore the title and estate. 
3 CONT Sir William inherited the baronetcy of Nova Scotia from his father. When he succeeded to the arms of Pitsligo in 1781, he brought his philanthropic endeavours, already felt widely through Edinburgh, to Aberdeenshire, where he laid
3 CONC  out the village of New Pitsligo and made ample provision for its residents. Forbes was known as a convivial conversationalist in both Scotland and London, where he was a member of Samuel Johnson's Literary Club and a friend of Si
3 CONC r Joshua Reynolds, for whom he also sat (National Galleries Scotland, Edinburgh)
3 CONT
3 CONT https://www.sothebys.com/en/auctions/ecatalogue/2017/two-great-scottish-collections-l17317/lot.310.html?locale=en
2 NOTE The date of 1789 is just a guess.  He looks about 50 to my eye.
