1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir James Carnegie, Baronet [S. 1663], of Pittarrow</strong>, 1st s. and h., 6. about 1715 ; suc. to the Baronetcy, 3 April 1729i He, on the death of his cousin James, 5th Earl of Southesk [S.], in 1729, would have succee
2 CONC ded to that title, but for the attainder thereof in 1716. He was a Captain in the army, and fought at Pontenoy, in 1745, and at Culloden, in 1746 ; was M.P. for co. Kincardine (four Paris.), 1741 till his death in 1765. In 1764 he
2 CONC , for £36,870, purchased Kinnaird and other family estates in Forfarshire which had been forfeited by the Earl his cousin abovementioned, directing the estate of Pittarrow to be sold. He m., 5 July 1752, Christian, 1st da. of Davi
2 CONC d Doig, of Cookstoun, by Magdalen Stmmers, heiress of Balyordie. He d. 30 April and was hur. 4 May 1765. His widow d. 4 Nov. 1820, aged 91."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524390#page/n355/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1903; Vol. III; page 337
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun08pauluoft#page/82/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. VIII, page 83-85
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT "On 5 July 1752, he married Christian Doig, eldest daughter of David Doig, and by her had two daughters and four sons. Carnegie died and was buried at Stamford, Lincolnshire. He was succeeded in the baronetcy by his oldest son Dav
2 CONC id."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_James_Carnegie,_3rd_Baronet
