1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for SOUTH CAROLINA (Soldier).  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A134911  DAR Ancestor #  A134911]  
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Armstrong was born on c. 1742 in Ballymena, County Antrim, Ulster Province, Ireland to William Denus Armstrong and Margaret Hamilton Armstrong.  Samuel married Mary Ann Boyd on 1764 in Ulster Provinde, Northern Ireland and 
2 CONC had 9 children:  William Armstrong, Sr., Samuel Armstrong, Jr., David Armstrong, Hugh Armstrong, James Armstrong, John H. Armstrong, Martha Armstrong, Mary Ann Armstrong, and Matthew Armstrong.  He passed away BEFORE September 12,
2 CONC  1809 in Abbeville County, South Carolina, United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  SOUTH CAROLINA ROSTER: Armstrong, Samuel b. 1742-Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in an independent company of rangers under Captain Robert Ellison during 1775. S.C.H.&G., I, 196
2 CONT
2 CONT SAMUEL ARMSTRONG was born in 1742 at Canonbie, On the Border, Scotland.(alt. place of birth)
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel arrived in South Carolina on December, 1767 from Ballimena, County of Antrim, Kingdom of Ireland aboard  the ship "EARL OF DENEGAL". The ship's Captain was Duncan Ferguson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with Samuel, who was twenty five, was his wife, Mary Boyd, twenty three, their first son, William, two, and Sam's mother, Margaret Hamilton, sixty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1768, Samuel was awarded 200 acres and his mother 100 acres, all on Waterbee Creek, Craven County, South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel lived in Abbeville County, South Carolina, and lived to sire nine children. He joined the "South Carolina Rangers", under the command of Captain Robert Ellison, in 1775 and served in the War of Independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel died in Sept. 1809, in Abbeville, County, SC. He left the following will, dated 26 Aug. 1809, proved 11 Sep. 18 09, Will Book, page 380, Abbeville Dist., SC.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL OF SAMUEL ARMSTRONG
2 CONT
2 CONT * In the name of God Amen. I Samuel Armstrong, being in a low state of health but sound of mind and memory, calling to mind the mortality of the body, do make and publish this , my last will and testament in manner following, viz.
2 CONC  First I give my Soul to God, Who gave it, and my body to be decently buried in a Christian manner. 
2 CONT * Next, I allow all my just debts to be paid.
2 CONT *  Next, I give and bequeath to my loving wife, two cows and calves and all my household furniture during her life. 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my son John Armstrong, one hundred acres of land where he now lives , he paying my beloved wife ten dollars and one wagon load of corn yearly during her life. 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my son David Armstrong, one hundred acres of land whereon he now lives, he paying my beloved wife ten dollars and one wagon load of corn yearly during her life, and at my wife's decease, my son Joh
2 CONC n Armstrong and my son David Armstrong is to pay my son Matthew Armstrong twenty five dollars each.
2 CONT *  Next, I give and bequeath unto my son William Armstrong, one dollar, 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my son Hugh Armstrong, one dollar. 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my son James Armstrong, one dollar. 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my daughter '''Martha Furlong''', one dollar. 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my daughter, '''Mary Emerson''', one dollar . 
2 CONT * Next, I give and bequeath unto my son, Samuel Armstrong , one hundred acres of land, where he now lives, my beloved wife to have the benefit of the dwelling house during her life and I allow my son Samuel Armstrong to attend tha
2 CONC t piece of ground I formally attended, one half in corn and the other half in small grain, yearly, for my wife and also to give her a wagon load of corn yearly during her life and at my decease, I allow an inventory to be taken of
2 CONC  all my movable property and at my loving wife's decease, for it to be sold and an equal division of the money to be made between my sons '''John, David and Samuel Armstrong and my daughter, Mary Emerson'''. 
2 CONT * Next, I appoint Abraham Livingston , my son David Armstrong and my son Samuel Armstrong, Executors of this, my last will and testament. 
2 CONT * Lastly, I give and bequeath unto my beloved wife, all my stock of hogs. Signed sealed and acknowledged, this to be my last will and testament, this twenty sixth day of August in the year of our Loard, one thousand eight hundred 
2 CONC and nine, in the preasence of Abraham Livingston, Robert Russell, Wm. Black Jr.
2 CONT *his Samuel X Armstrong
2 CONT * mark
2 CONT * Expend: Oct 27, 1826.
2 CONT * To cash paid for Matilda V. armstrong , board, $9.00 Feb. 6, 1826. 
2 CONT * To cash paid Watt Bowie for Eliza Armstrong, $14.56 1/4 Feb. 6, 1826.
2 CONT *  To cash paid James Armstrong of his share. Feb. 24, 1827. 
2 CONT * Paid Ord. for guardianship to Matilda and Amanda Armstrong, $3.00 
2 CONT * Paid John Wier for boarding John Armstrong , $3.00 Jan. 1, 1824.
2 CONT *  Paid Mary Ann Armstrong legacy in part, $105.88
2 CONT * Samuel Armstrong had a note for $6.00 on Matthew, Martin and Henry Emerson. Also a note on William Emerson, $5.00.
2 CONT
2 CONT ARMSTRONG MINORS
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 Feb. 1827, Samuel Huston, John Power and Patrick John son bound unto Moses Taggart, Ord. Abbeville Dist, the sum of $2000.00. Samuel L. Houston was guardian of Matilda Vasti Armstrong and Amanda Zena Armstrong, minors under 
2 CONC 14 years. Eliza Armstrong has also been listed as a minor. These are his Granddaughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
