1 NAME James /Clerk/ of Penicuik, 3rd baronet
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Clerk
2 NSFX of Penicuik, 3rd baronet
2 _MARNM Clerk
1 NAME Sir James Clerk of Penicuik, 3rd baronet
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 DEC 1709
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 FEB 1783
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 3RD BARONET
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Clerk succeeded his father in 1755 but was managing his father's coal interests at Loanhead by 1750. His own taste for architecture first appears in 1753 in the form of a new library and a few rooms added to Newbiggin Ho
2 CONC use. Ambitious but unrealised designs also exist for a south facing combined library and bath house in the form of the Roman Pantheon.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1760 Sir James' parents had died and almost at once the new Baronet set about remodeling Newbiggin.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first Sir James intended to incorporate some parts of Newbiggin in his new designs for the new house but gradually the project changed and it was decided to rebuild the house entirely. In 1761 Newbiggin was demolished and work 
2 CONC began on the replacement building. The design of this ambitious Palladian house was the result of a collaboration between Sir James Clerk and John Baxter Senior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Clerk died in 1782 and was succeeded by his brother George 4th Baronet, and then by a nephew John 5th Baronet. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Source: http://www.penicuikhouse.co.uk/history.aspx ]
2 CONT
2 CONT FROM ELECTRIC SCOTLAND
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Clerk, the third baronet, son of the second, married Elizabeth, daughter of the Rev. John Cleghorn, but dying in 1782 without issue, was succeeded by his brother Sir George Clerk-Maxwell, fourth baronet, of whom also a n
2 CONC otice is subsequently given. He married Dorothea, daughter of his uncle William Clerk-Maxwell, Esq., by his wife Agnes Maxwell, heiress of Middleby in Dumfries-shire, and had five sons and four daughters. He died in 1784, and was 
2 CONC succeeded by his eldest son, Sir John Clerk, who died in 1798. He married Mary, daughter of Mr. Dacre of Kirlington in Cumberland, but had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Source: http://www.electricscotland.com/history/nation/clerk.htm ]
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE:
2 CONT
2 CONT [This person was added as referenced in a detailed explanation of the Clerk family on electricscotland.com at http://www.electricscotland.com/history/nation/clerk.htm ]
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Its creator was Sir James Clerk, who, having lived long in Italy, was full of classical ideas, which he applied to the stately house he raised. It took the form of an "oblong square" with an Ionic pedimented portico, and was evide
2 CONC ntly a handsome and spacious abode. Fate, how- ever, decreed that Sir James Clerk should not build for posterity, and his house was burned down before it had acquired even middle age. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SCENIC SPLENDOURS. (1925, April 22). The Advertiser (Adelaide, SA : 1889-1931), p. 18. Retrieved January 4, 2011, from http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article46200345
