1 NAME Sir George Clerk-Maxwell of Penicuik, 4th baronet
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 31 OCT 1715
2 ADDR
3 CITY Edinburgh
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 JAN 1784
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_George_Clerk-Maxwell,_4th_Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Baron's precepts to his son George when at school in Cumberland are worthy of Polonius. He advises him to be kind to his companions, as he could not tell what they might be able to do for him thereafter, and to be sure to take
2 CONC  the opportunity of "learning the English language," in which the Baron himself regrets his own deficiency. "You have nothing else to depend on but your being a scholar and behaving well." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT This George Clerk Maxwell probably suffered a little from the world being made too easy for him in early life. Such a misfortune was all but inevitable, and the Baron seems to have done his best to obviate it by good counsel; but 
2 CONC the current of circumstances was too strong. George was in the habit of preserving letters, and from those received by him before succeeding to Penicuik it is possible to form a tolerably full impression of the man. In some respec
2 CONC ts he resembled John Clerk Maxwell, but certainly not in the quality of phlegmatic caution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His imagination seems to have been dangerously fired by the "little knowledge " of contemporary science which he may have picked up when at Leyden with his elder brother James (see p. 19,11.18, 25). We find him, while laird of Dum
2 CONC crieff, near Moffat, practically interested in the discovery of a new " Spaw," and humoured in this by his friend Allan Ramsay, the poet:—by and by he is deeply engaged in prospecting about the Lead Hills, and receiving humorous l
2 CONC etters on the subject from his friend Dr. James Hutton, one of the founders of geological science, and author of the Theory of the Earth.1 After a while he has commenced active operations, and is found making fresh proposals to th
2 CONC e Duke of Queensberry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then to the mines there is added some talk of a paper manufactory, and the Duchess playfully "congratulates" his "good self on every new sprouting up manufacture by means of so good a planter and planner." But by this time it has 
2 CONC been found advisable to add to his studies some more certain source of income, and he applies for the Postmastership, and (through the Duke of Q.) obtains an office in the Customs. In this, as in all relations of life, he seems to
2 CONC  have won golden opinions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT And ere he succeeded to Penicuik, the loss of Middlebie proper and Dumcrieff had doubtless taught the lesson of prudence which his father the Baron had vainly tried to impress upon his youth. The friendship of Allan Bamsay and the
2 CONC  affectionate confidence of the "good Duke and Duchess of Queensberry," sufficiently indicate the charm which there must have been about this man.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT The betrothal of George Clerk and Dorothea Clerk Maxwell is said by the Baron (in a special memorandum) to have been in accordance with her mother's dying wish. (Dorothea was seven years old when Agnes Maxwell died!) They were mar
2 CONC ried privately when he was twenty and she was seventeen, but "as they were too young to live together," he was sent to join his brother James at Leyden. After a short interval the marriage was declared, and they lived very happily
2 CONC  at Dumcrieff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir George Clerk Maxwell was a Commissioner of Customs, and a trustee for the. improvement of the fisheries and manufactures of Scotland. His brother, John Clerk of Eldin, was the author of the well-known work on Naval Tactics. He
2 CONC  was the father of John Clerk, a distinguished lawyer in Edinburgh, afterwards Lord Eldin, a Lord of Session....
2 CONT
2 CONT .....
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir George Clerk Maxwell died in 1784, and was succeeded by his eldest son, Sir John, who married Mary Dacre, but died without children in 1798. His brother James was a lieutenant in the East Indian Navy, and married Janet Irving 
2 CONC of Newton. He died in 1793, leaving two sons and a daughter. The elder son, afterwards the Right Honourable Sir George Clerk,2 succeeded to Penicuik on the death of his uncle, Sir John Clerk, in 1798; and the younger son John succ
2 CONC eeded to the property of Middlebie, which descended to him from his grandmother, Dorothea, Lady Clerk Maxwell, and took the name of Maxwell. He married in 1826 Frances, daughter of Robert Cay of Charlton, and had one son, James Cl
2 CONC erk Maxwell, born in July 1831, and died in Nov. 1879.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The life of James Clerk Maxwell:with a selection from his correspondence and occasional writings and a sketch of his contributions to science (Google eBook) By Lewis Campbell, William Garnett Macmillan, 1882
2 CONT
2 CONT From p. 16..
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=d2oOAAAAIAAJ&source=gbs_navlinks_s
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT I think he changed his name because he acquired land through his wife... "...under the deed any heir adopting another titel than that of Maxwell of Middlebie was declared to forfeit the right to succeed..."
2 CONT
2 CONT And it also says he is "3rd baronet"
2 CONT
2 CONT See p 251 of Memoirs of Sir John Clerk
2 CONT
