2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir George Clerk Maxwell was a Commissioner of Customs, and a trustee for the. improvement of the fisheries and manufactures of Scotland. His brother, '''John Clerk of Eldin''', was the author of the well-known work on Naval Tacti
2 CONC cs. He was the father of John Clerk, a distinguished lawyer in Edinburgh, afterwards Lord Eldin, a Lord of Session.
2 CONT
2 CONT .....
2 CONT
2 CONT The following account of Mr. Clerk of Eldin is from the above-mentioned biographical notice of Dr. James Hutton :
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mr. Clerk of Eldin was another friend with whom, in the formation of his theory, Dr. Hutton maintained a constant communication. Mr. Clerk, perhaps from the extensive property which his family had in the coal-mines near Edinburgh
2 CONC , was early interested in the pursuit of mineralogy. His inquiries, however, were never confined to the objects which mere situation might point out, and through his whole life has been much more directed by the irresistible impul
2 CONC se of genius, than by the action of external circumstances. Though not bred to the sea, he is well known to have studied the principles of naval war with unexampled success; and, though not exercising the profession of arms, he ha
2 CONC s viewed every country through which he has passed with the eye of a soldier as well as a geologist. The interest he took in studying the surface no less than the interior of the earth; his extensive information in most branches o
2 CONC f natural history; a mind of great resource and great readiness of invention; made him to Dr. Hutton an invaluable friend and coadjutor. It cannot be doubted that, in many parts, the system of the latter has had great obligations 
2 CONC to the ingenuity of the former, though the unreserved intercourse of friendship, and the adjustments produced by mutual suggestion, might render those parts undistinguishable, even by the authors themselves. Mr. Clerk's pencil was
2 CONC  ever at the command of his friend, and has certainly rendered him most essential service."
2 CONT
2 CONT In another place the same writer says :—
2 CONT
2 CONT "Some excellent drawings were made by Mr. Clerk, whose pencil is not less valuable in the sciences than in the arts." 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Clerk, Esq., of Eldin, fifth son of Sir John Clerk of Penicuik, and brother of Sir George, the grandfather of John Clerk Maxwell, was the author of certain suggestions on Naval Tactics, which had the credit of contributing to
2 CONC  the victory gained over the French by Lord Eodney's fleet off Dominique in 1782. It is fair to add that Sir C. Douglas claimed to have independently hit on the new method of "breaking the enemy's line." But John Clerk never relin
2 CONC quished his pretensions to the merit of the discovery, and his book on Naval Tactics in the edition of 1804 1 is a thick octavo volume, with many woodcut illustrations and diagrams. He used to sail his mimic navies, trying various
2 CONC  combinations with them, on the fish-ponds at Penicuik....
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The life of James Clerk Maxwell:with a selection from his correspondence and occasional writings and a sketch of his contributions to science (Google eBook) By Lewis Campbell, William Garnett Macmillan, 1882
2 CONT
2 CONT From p. 16..
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=d2oOAAAAIAAJ&source=gbs_navlinks_s
