1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Melville Leslie, son of David [Leslie], 8th Earl of Leven , Treasurer, CCS Since 1805 the family has borne the name of Leslie-Melville.
2 CONT
2 CONT GEOGRAPHICAL NAMES           
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT EARLS OF. LEVEN AND MELVILLE The family of Melville which now holds these two earldoms is descended' from Sir John Melville of Raith in Fifeshire. Sir John, who was a member of the reforming party in Scotland, was put to death for
2 CONC  high treason on the 13th of December 1548; he left with other children a son Robert (1527-1621), who in 1616 was created a lord of parliament as Lord Melville of Monymaill. Before his elevation to the Scottish peerage Melville ha
2 CONC d been a stout partisan of Mary, queen of Scots, whom he represented at the English court, and he had filled several important offices in Scotland under her son James VI. The fourth holder of the lordship of Melville was George (c
2 CONC . 1634-1707), a son of John, the 3rd lord (d. 1643), and a descendant of Sir John Melville. Implicated in the Rye House plot against Charles II., George took refuge in the Netherlands in 1683, but he returned to England after the 
2 CONC revolution of 1688 and was appointed secretary for Scotland by William III. in 1689, being created earl of Melville in the following year. He was made president of the Scottish privy council in 1696, but he was deprived of his off
2 CONC ice when Anne became queen in 1702, and he died on the 20th of May 1707. His son David, 2nd earl of Melville (1660-1728), fled to Holland with his father in 1683; after serving in the army of the elector of Brandenburg he accompan
2 CONC ied William of Orange to England in 1688. At the head of a regiment raised by himself he fought for William at Killiecrankie and elsewhere, and as commanderin-chief of the troops in Scotland he dealt promptly and effectively with 
2 CONC the attempted Jacobite rising of 1708. In 1712, however, his office was taken from him and he died on the 6th of June 1728. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon Alexander Leslie, later 7th Earl of Leven died 6 Jun 1728 accompanied his father to Holland 1683; raised a regiment of foot guards for the service of William , Prince of Orange 1689; 
