1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '#Health Warning#
2 CONT The only evidence, that Lady Mary might be George's mother Mrs Walker, is that Lady Mary's grandson Baron Thiebault in his write up of the family history suggests that a George was her first born and an Aunt remembered in 1826 (60
2 CONC  years later) attending his baptism in Edinburgh, in fact Alexander was the first born in Edinburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Natural son of George Musgrave and ...... Walker of Devonshire. Of 48 Devonshire Street, in the parish of St. Marylebone, Co. Middlesex; 6 Gordon Square in the parish of St. Pancras, Co. Middlesex; Apsley End House, Shillington, C
2 CONC o. Bedford; and Borden Hall, Co., Kent. (Shillington Manor burned down 1915). He was J.P. and High Sheriff of Bedfordshire 1828. Married 19 August 1791 at "St. George's", Margaret, daughter of James Kennedy of "St. George's", Hano
2 CONC ver Square, Co. Middlesex.? They eloped when she was at school in Paris, and from the tales, they had a very gay time while on the Continent. They were in Paris several months before 21 June 1791 (the flight of the French King Lou
2 CONC is XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette from Paris for Prussia via Belgium before being arrested at Varennes on 23 June and returned to Paris). She died 19 Sept 1859 at 6 Gordon Square, London, aet. 90. M.I. Borden Church chancel rail, 
2 CONC "Margaret, wife of George Musgrave, entered into her promised rest on 20 day of September AD 1859, in the sixty eighth year of wedlock and the 90th of her age. Through all the days of this eventful pilgrimage her kindly affectiona
2 CONC te nature evinced how love and goodness sweeten life and mitigate its cares and sufferings. She exhibited a bright pure example; and she had this done she fell asleep". In 1816 he was acknowledged by his father, where upon he appl
2 CONC ied for and was granted by Patent No. .... (? Private Act of Parliament) the Coat of Arms and the name Musgrave. The Coat of Arms is not the same as the Musgraves of Edenhall, but has a wavy gold strip round the outside to indicat
2 CONC e illegitimacy. (The Musgrave of Edenhall Arms are blue, while those of Shillington and Borden are red, the same as his remote forebear who was summoned to Parliament as a Baron for one year only). Died 27 June 1861, aged 92, at 6
2 CONC  Gordon Square, in the parish of St. Pancras, Co. Middlesex, buried in the family vault (Yew Tree) at Borden, Co. Kent. (Uncle Kit saw it summer 1971). M.I. Borden Church chancel rail, "Only son of above named George Musgrave (3) 
2 CONC and inheritor of his estates in this Parish (Borden) and in the County of Bedford, attained to the advanced age of 92 years, and was laid in his own new tomb under the Yew Tree in this (Borden) churchyard. In his long and honourab
2 CONC le life, he held both Military and Civil Commissions in which his noble character reflected the excellence of his private worth, and he died on the 27 June 1861 bequeathing to his surviving children that which "is rather to be cho
2 CONC sen than riches".
