1 NOTE {geni:about_me} My current “working assumption”, having read the family history by Baron Thiébault, is that George and Elizabeth may well have been twins. Their arrival  would have put considerable pressure on all concerned and th
2 CONC at all parties agreed that these twins would be brought up in a Musgrave household with James Walker agreeing that they could keep his surname but the Musgrave’s were free to choose their first names. Birth dates were varied or un
2 CONC recorded. Both Elizabeth and George in the 1841 census give their age as 70! If the quoted birth date for George, 8th of Sept, is correct then the year cannot be 1769 if Lady Mary is his mother so 8/9/1770 works being a year and a
2 CONC  quarter after David’s birth ( Sir Edward Hales being David’s father) and two and a half years before Isabelle’s birth. From Baron Thiébault’s history Isabella looked the most like Dr James Walker despite her toying with the idea 
2 CONC that she had royal blood and the Duke of Cumberland being her father. This hints at a reconciliation after the twins' birth however when Mary Ann and Leslie came along it was all too much and Dr James left for Jamaica never to ret
2 CONC urn. After that Lady Mary had two more daughters Sophie and Julie. An amusing anecdote in the history is that when Isabelle was getting married in France, agents were dispatched back to London to obtain a copy of her birth certifi
2 CONC cate but they could only get an authorised copy of a William Walker’s birth so that was the name she got married under!
