1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_William_Erskine,_2nd_Baronet Wikipedia: Sir William Erskine, 2nd Baronet]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Erskine, 2nd Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT Major-General Sir William Erskine, 2nd Baronet (Friday, 30 March 1770 – 1813) was an officer in the British Army, served as a member of Parliament, and achieved important commands in the Napoleonic Wars under the Duke of Wellingto
2 CONC n, but ended his service in insanity and suicide.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Lieutenant-General Sir William Erskine, 1st Baronet and his second wife, Frances Moray. He succeeded to the baronetcy on his father's death in 1795.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Early career''
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine was commissioned into the 23rd foot 1785, and transferred to the 5th Dragoons as a lieutenant in 1787, and in 1791 became captain of the 15th King's Light Dragoons (the unit his father had served in with distinction) on We
2 CONC dnesday, 23 February 1791. His first active service was in Flanders 1793–95, during the French Revolutionary Wars, when he acted as aide-de-camp to his father. In 1794 he was made lieutenant-colonel. and fought at the Battle of Vi
2 CONC llers-en-Cauchies, where a handful of English and Austrian cavalry routed a much larger force of French infantry and cavalry.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's death in 1795, Erskine became baronet. He represented Fife in Parliament in 1796 and 1802–1805. Despite being "blind as a beetle", according to a fellow officer, in 1808, Erskine received promotion to major general
2 CONC . When he heard Erskine was being shipped to Portugal, Wellington complained that he "generally understood him to be a madman." The administrators of the army at Horse Guards responded that, "No doubt he is sometimes a little mad,
2 CONC  but in his lucid intervals he is an uncommonly clever fellow; and I trust he will have no fit during the campaign, though he looked a little wild as he embarked."
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Peninsular War''
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1811 campaign in Portugal, Erskine took over the command of the famous Light Division in the absence of Robert Craufurd. He soon developed a reputation for rashness. Wellington wrote, "It is impossible to trust to his j
2 CONC udgment in any critical case."
2 CONT
2 CONT While pursuing Marshal Andre Massena's retreating French army, several sharp actions were fought at Pombal, Redinha, Casal Novo and Foz do Arouce between the Light Division and Marshal Michel Ney's rearguard. At Casal Novo on Thur
2 CONC sday, 14 March 1811, Erskine advanced his men along the main road in fog without proper scouts. When the fog suddenly cleared, his leading elements found themselves facing elements of Jean Marchand's division deployed in line with
2 CONC  artillery support. This carelessness cost the Light Division 155 killed and wounded, while Marchand lost only 55 men.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Battle of Sabugal, the fog and Erskine's bungling saved General Jean Reynier's isolated French corps from destruction. Wellington assigned Erskine with the Light Division and some cavalry to cut in behind Reynier's open lef
2 CONC t flank while four divisions attacked in front. The hapless Erskine, who was very nearsighted, issued a set of foolish orders then promptly got lost in the fog with the cavalry. The leaderless Light Division covered itself with gl
2 CONC ory in the subsequent action, but the French escaped from Wellington's trap.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Battle of Fuentes de Onoro, Erskine's 5th and Alexander Campbell's 6th Divisions covered the Siege of Almeida. After the French relief army was turned back, the French garrison slipped out of the fortress in the night a
2 CONC nd marched straight through the blockading force to freedom. On this occasion, an exasperated Wellington said, "I have never been so distressed by any military event as by the escape of even a man of them." This time Erskine was o
2 CONC nly one of several officers who blundered. Aware that he could not dismiss Erskine because of the man's political influence, Wellington tried to place Erskine in positions where he could do little damage.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wednesday, 19 June 1811, Erskine led four mounted regiments in the newly organized 2nd Cavalry Division in Rowland Hill's corps. He soon relinquished command, but reassumed his post on Wednesday, 8 April 1812. Soon after, he 
2 CONC was declared insane and cashiered. He took his own life in Lisbon in 1813 by jumping out of a window, reportedly with the last words, "Now why did I do that?"
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_William_Erskine,_1st_Baronet Wikipedia - Sir William Erskine, 1st Baronet]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-General Sir William Erskine, 1st Baronet (1728 – 19 March 1795) was a British Army commander and the 1st Baronet of the Erskine of Torrie creation....
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine first married Magdalen Myrton, daughter of Robert Myrton of Gogar, and then Frances Moray, daughter of James Moray of Abercairny.
2 CONT
2 CONT His eldest son by his second wife, Frances Moray, William Erskine, the 2nd baronet was a cavalry officer who committed suicide in Lisbon, Portugal in 1813 after a mental breakdown. He had another son, James Erskine, who was an off
2 CONC icer in the 133rd (Fraser's) Foot, and became baronet on his elder brother's death. His third son John Erskine, also became baronet, on the death of his brother James.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/record-results?search_type=people&event=%28B%20OR%20C%20OR%20S%29&record_type%5B0%5D=opr_births&church_type=Old%20Parish%20Registers&dl_cat=church&dl_rec=church-births-baptisms&surname=E
2 CONC rskine&surname_so=soundex&forename_so=exact&from_year=1768&to_year=1795&parent_names=William&parent_names_so=exact&parent_name_two=Murray&parent_name_two_so=exact&record=Church%20of%20Scotland%20%28old%20parish%20registers%29%20Ro
2 CONC man%20Catholic%20Church%20Other%20churches&sort=asc&order=Date&field=year Scotland's People: Old Parish Registers - Births and Baptisms]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 31 March 1770 birth or baptism of William Erskine, son of Sir William Erskine and and Frances Murray [child 2], in the parish of Torryburn
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000045/17950423/023/0003 British Newspaper Archive: Caledonian Mercury Thursday, 23 April 1795 Page 3 Benevolence to the poor]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT We hear from Inverkeithing, that the poor of that parish have been, during the inclemency of the winter, liberally supported from the following donations:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir W. Erskine of Tory, Bart. £5 5s. 0d.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/record-results?search_type=people&dl_cat=legal&dl_rec=legal-wills-testaments&surname=Erskine&surname_so=exact&forename=William&forename_so=exact&from_year=1813&to_year=1815&record_type=w
2 CONC ills_testaments Scotland's People: Wills and Testaments]'''
2 CONT
