1 NAME Robert /Haldane/
2 GIVN Robert
2 _MARNM Haldane
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 28 FEB 1764
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 DEC 1842
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Haldane Robert Haldane] (February 28, 1764 - December 12, 1842), was a Scottish churchman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Haldane was born in London, the son of James Haldane of Airthrey House and his wife Katherine Duncan. His younger brother James Alexander Haldane was also a clergyman . After attending classes at Dundee Grammar School and in the R
2 CONC oyal High School and University of Edinburgh, in 1780 he joined HMS Monarch of which his maternal uncle, Adam Duncan, was in command. In the following year he was transferred to HMS Foudroyant on board of which, during the night e
2 CONC ngagement with the French ship Pegase, he greatly distinguished himself. Haldane was afterwards present at the relief of Gibraltar, but at the peace of 1783 he finally left the Royal Navy, and soon afterwards settled on his estate
2 CONC  of Airthrey, near Stirling.
2 CONT
2 CONT His tutor was David Bogue of Gosport. The earlier phases of the French Revolution excited a sympathy which induced him to avow his strong disapproval of the war with France. As his over-optimistic visions of a new order of things 
2 CONC to be ushered in by political change disappeared, he began to direct his thoughts to religious subjects. Resolving to devote himself and his means wholly to the advancement of Christianity, his first proposal for that end, made in
2 CONC  1796, was to organize a vast mission to Bengal, of which he was to provide the entire expense; with this view the greater part of his estate was sold, but the British East India Company refused to sanction the scheme, which there
2 CONC fore had to be abandoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1797 Haldane sold his castle, left the Church of Scotland and travelled around Scotland preaching. In December of that year he joined his brother and some others in the formation of the "Society for the Propagation of the Gospe
2 CONC l at Home," in building chapels or "tabernacles" for congregations, in supporting missionaries, and in maintaining institutions for the education of young men to carry on the work of evangelization. He is said to have spent more t
2 CONC han £70,000 in the course of the following twelve years (1798-1810). He also initiated a plan for evangelizing Africa by bringing over native children to be trained as Christian missionaries. In 1816 he visited the continent, and 
2 CONC first at Geneva and afterwards in Montauban (1817) he lectured and interviewed large numbers of theological students with remarkable effect; among them were Malan, Frédéric Monod and Jean-Henri Merle d'Aubigné. Returning to Scotla
2 CONC nd in 1819, he lived partly on his estate of Auchengray and partly in Edinburgh, and like his brother took an active part, chiefly through the press, in many of the religious controversies of the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1816 he published a work on the Evidences and Authority of Divine Revelation, and in 1819 the substance of his theological prelections in a Commentaire sur l'Epitre aux Romains. Among his later writings, besides numerous pamphl
2 CONC ets on what was known as "the Apocrypha controversy," are a treatise On the Inspiration of Scripture (1828), which passed through many editions, and a later Exposition of the Epistle to the Romans (1835), which has been frequently
2 CONC  reprinted, and has been translated into French and German.
