1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Robert_Laurie,_6th_Baronet Wikipedia: Sir Robert Laurie, 6th Baronet]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Admiral Sir Robert Laurie, 6th Baronet of Maxwelton KCB (Friday, 25 May 1764 – Friday, 7 January 1848) was an officer of the Royal Navy who served during the French Revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars. He rose through the ranks afte
2 CONC r his entry, fighting as a lieutenant under Howe at the Glorious First of June, and being wounded in the action. Shortly after he served in the West Indies and off the American coast, where he operated successfully against enemy r
2 CONC aiders and privateers, he was rewarded with the command of the frigate HMS Cleopatra, and in 1805 fought an action with a superior French opponent, Ville de Milan. He was forced to surrender his ship after several hours of fightin
2 CONC g, but so heavily damaged the Frenchman that both she and the captured British vessel were taken shortly afterwards when another British frigate HMS Leander, arrived on the scene. Rewarded for his valour and honourably acquitted f
2 CONC or the loss of his ship, he served throughout the rest of the Napoleonic Wars. He rose to flag rank after the end of the wars, eventually dying in 1848 with the rank of Admiral of the White. He inherited a baronetcy in 1804, but t
2 CONC his became extinct upon his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Family and early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Laurie was born on Friday, 25 May 1764, the son of Sir Robert Laurie and his wife Mary Elizabeth Ruthven. He entered the navy in 1780, spending 10 years as midshipman before being promoted to lieutenant in 1790. He was a li
2 CONC eutenant aboard the 90-gun second rate HMS Queen and fought in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glorious_First_of_June Glorious First of June, North Atlantic Ocean, 1 June 1794 47.400, -17.450], during which action he was wounde
2 CONC d.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Command''
2 CONT
2 CONT He received a promotion in June the following year, rising to the rank of commander and being given the sloop HMS Zephyr. He served in the North Sea before being ordered to the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leeward_Islands Leewar
2 CONC d Islands, Caribbean Sea, North Atlantic Ocean 16, -61] towards the end of 1796. While sailing there he came across the 12-gun privateer Refléche and captured her on Sunday, 8 January 1797. He went on to take part in the reduction
2 CONC  of Trinidad in February 1797 [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invasion_of_Trinidad_%281797%29 Invasion and Capture of Trinidad, Trinidad, 21 February 1797 10.46056, -61.24861], making several other captures of privateers during the
2 CONC  rest of the year; the 4-gun Vengeur des Français on 16 June, the 6-gun Légère on 6 July and the 2-gun Va-Tout on 8 July. On Tuesday, 17 July 1798 Laurie received a promotion to post-captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was removed into HMS Andromache in 1799 and spent the next several years serving on the North American and Jamaica stations. He took a Spanish gunboat off Cuba on Sunday, 22 March 1801 in company with the 32-gun HMS Cleopatra, 
2 CONC and after a spell in the Bahamas in 1803, returned to the English Channel in 1804. Laurie then transferred to take command of the Cleopatra in summer 1804. Laurie succeeded to the baronetcy on Monday, 10 September 1804 with the de
2 CONC ath of his father, the fifth baronet. Cleopatra spent some time in the West Indies, and was homeward bound in February 1805.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Fighting the Ville de Milan''
2 CONT
2 CONT While sailing off Bermuda on 16 February 1805 Cleopatra sighted a sail; this was the 40-gun French frigate Ville de Milan which had sailed from Martinique on 28 January 1805 under Captain Jean-Marie Renaud, bound for France with i
2 CONC mportant despatches. Despite identifying his quarry as a superior opponent, Laurie ordered a chase. Renaud had orders to avoid combat and pressed on sail to escape Laurie. The chase covered 180 miles and lasted until the following
2 CONC  morning, when Renaud reluctantly came about to meet the Cleopatra, which was overhauling the Ville de Milan. The engagement began in earnest at 2.30pm, and a heavy cannonade was maintained between the two frigates until 5pm, when
2 CONC  the Cleopatra had her wheel shot away and her rudder jammed. The Ville de Milan approached from windward and ran aboard the Cleopatra, jamming her bowsprit over the quarterdeck of the British ship and raked her decks with musket 
2 CONC fire. The British resisted one attempt to board, but on being unable to break free, were forced to surrender to a second boarding party. The Cleopatra had 22 killed and 36 wounded, with the loss of her foremast, mainmast and bowsp
2 CONC rit. The Ville de Milan had probably about 30 killed and wounded, with Captain Renaud among the dead. She also lost her mainmast and mizzenmast. Three days were spent transferring a prize crew and prisoners, and patching up the sh
2 CONC ips, before the two got underway on 21 February 1805.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, on 23 February 1805 they were discovered by the 50-gun HMS Leander, under Captain John Talbot. Leander ran up to them, whereupon they separated. Talbot chased Cleopatra, brought her to with a shot and took possession. The
2 CONC  freed crew reported the situation to Talbot, and left him to pursue the fleeing Ville de Milan. Talbot soon overtook her and she surrendered without a fight. Laurie took back command of the Cleopatra and all three ships sailed to
2 CONC  Halifax, where the Ville de Milan was taken into service as HMS Milan. Laurie's engagement with the superior opponent had initially cost him his ship, but had rendered her easy prey to any other Royal Navy frigate in the vicinity
2 CONC . Had he not brought her to battle, the Ville de Milan could have easily outsailed the Leander or even engaged her on fairly equal terms. Instead the damage and losses incurred in breaking down the Cleopatra had left her helpless 
2 CONC to resist.
2 CONT
2 CONT A court-martial honourably acquitted Laurie of any blame for the loss of his ship, and the Patriotic Fund presented him with a 100-guinea sword 'as a well-merited compliment to his great bravery and skill'. Laurie was duly appoint
2 CONC ed to command the Milan. He was then appointed to command the 74-gun third rate HMS Ajax towards the end of 1811, and spent the rest of the war in the Mediterranean.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Later life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Laurie was promoted to Rear-Admiral of the Blue on Thursday, 19 July 1821, Rear-Admiral of the White Friday, 27 May 1825, Rear-Admiral of the Red Thursday, 22 July 1830, Vice-Admiral of the White Tuesday, 10 January 1837, Vice-Adm
2 CONC iral of the Red (date unknown) and Admiral of the Blue on Monday, 9 November 1846. He was nominated a Knight Commander of the Bath in 1836. He died unmarried with the rank of Admiral of the White on Friday, 7 January 1848 at his s
2 CONC eat of [https://britishlistedbuildings.co.uk/200342758-maxwelton-house-glencairn Maxwelton House, 1 Maxwelton Mains, Moniaive, Dumfries and Galloway  DG3 4DX 55.1874, -3.8516], Dumfriesshire. He had no issue, and the baronetcy bec
2 CONC ame extinct upon his death. Maxwelton House later passed to his grand-nephew Emilius Bayley, 3rd Baronet Bayley who changed his name to Laurie and the name of the Baronetcy to Laurie.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://archive.org/details/cu31924092524408/page/n351/mode/2up?view=theater&q=Laurie Complete Baronetage 1665 to 1707 by George Edward Cokayne Volume 4 Published 1904 Page 332]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT VI. 1804 to 1848
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Laurie, Baronet [S. 1685], of Maxwelton to aforesaid, only son and heir, born Friday, 25 May 1764; entered the Royal 1848. Navy, distinguishing himself as Lieutenant of "The Queen," in Admiral Howe's action, 1794; as al
2 CONC so, off Cuba, in 1801, and in the action with the Ville de Milan in 1805, (a) becoming eventually 1821 Admiral; succeeded to the Baronetcy, Monday, 10 September 1804; K.C.B., Tuesday, 13 September 1831. He died unmarried at Maxwel
2 CONC ton House, Friday, 7 January 1848, and was buried Friday, 14 January 1848, at Glencairn, in his 84th year, when the Baronetcy became extinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote: His nephew, John Minet Fector, to whom on Monday, 22 April 1844, he had disponed the Maxwelton estate, succeeded him therein, and took the name of Laurie, Saturday, 12 February 1848. He was only son of the Baronet's only
2 CONC  sister Anne Laurie, born Tuesday, 13 June 1769, who married Saturday, 22 February 1794, John Minet Fector, of Updown, Kent, a Banker at Dover, and was living a widow in 1848. Her son, the said John Minet Laurie, of Maxwelton, die
2 CONC d without issue [s.p.], Monday, 24 February 1868, aged 55.]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Probably [https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/search-records/church-registers/church-births-baptisms#form&record_type=opr_births&church_type=Old%20Parish%20Registers&surname=Laurie&surname_so=exact&forename=Robert&forename_so=ex
2 CONC act&from_year=1764&to_year=1764&parent_names=Robert&parent_names_so=exact&parent_name_two_so=exact&county=DUMFRIES Scotland's People: Old Parish Registers: Births and Baptisms]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 5 June 1764 birth or baptism of Robert Laurie, son of Robert Laurie, in the parish of Dumfries
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0002437/18480424/020/0006 British Newspaper Archive: Monthly Times Monday, 24 April 1848 Page 6]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The late Admiral Sir Robert Laurie, Bart., K.C.B., of Maxwelton House, Dumfries, North Britain, who died on Friday, 7 January 1848 [last], at the age of eighty-four, had executed his will in April 1844, at which time he was blind,
2 CONC  and has at various periods since added four codicils, to all of which he has signed his name, the will and the three first codicils being executed in the presence of four witnesses besides two notaries. The same were registered i
2 CONC n the Court at Dumfries on Monday, 6 March 1848 [last], and proved in London on Friday, 31 March 1848, by the sole executor, John Minet Laurie, Esq. (heretofore Fector), his nephew, and the son of the late John Fector, Esq., banke
2 CONC r, Dover, to whom he has conveyed the lands and estates of Maxwelton and others, and appointed him residuary legatee of the heritable and real property, subject to the following annuities and bequests: —
2 CONT
2 CONT To Lady Laurie 600l. a-year, to include the allowance to which she is entitled as the widow of a Flag Officer; to Miss Anne Hall, 70l. a-year, in consideration of the high opinion he entertained of her valuable services to his fam
2 CONC ily, which is to be free of any allowance while remaining with Lady Laurie; a legacy of 1,000l. to his sister, Mrs. Anne Wortley Montague Fector; and bequests to his three nieces, and other legacies.
