0 @N6000000003615703378@ NOTE Note: 1542?-1583, Scottish nobleman; cousin to James VI of Scotland (later James I of England). Bo rn and reared in France, he succeeded his father as seigneur d'Aubigny in 1567. In 1579 he wa s sent to Scotland by the Guise famil
1 CONC y to restore French influence and weaken Protestantism . He soon won the friendship of the young King James, was admitted to the council, and was cr eated successively earl (1580) and duke (1581) of Lennox. Although Lennox publicl
1 CONC y proclaime d his conversion to Protestantism in 1580, he was suspected (with reason) of complicity i n a projected Spanish invasion of England to release Mary Queen of Scots. This, with Lennox' s part in the arrest and execution 
1 CONC (1581) of the earl of Morton, led to the Protestant nobles ' seizure of James in the raid of Ruthven (1582). Against both his own and the king's wishes , Lennox was forced to leave Scotland. He died shortly thereafter.
