0 @N6000000003904550893@ NOTE
1 CONT !NAME: 1st Duke of Lennox.
0 @N6000000003043297103@ NOTE Created Duke of Lennox in 1579.
0 @N6000000003904550875@ NOTE
1 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> Duke of Lennox Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 9FLH-QW
0 @N6000000003904550877@ NOTE
1 CONT 1542?-1583, Scottish nobleman; cousin to James VI of Scotland (later James I  of England). Born and reared in France, he succeeded his father as seigneur d'Aubigny in 1567. In 1579 he was sent to Scotland by the Guise family to re
1 CONC store French influence and weaken Protestantism. He soon won the friendship of the young King James, was admitted to the council, and was created successively earl (1580) and duke (1581) of Lennox. Although Lennox publicly proclai
1 CONC med his conversion to Protestantism in 1580, he was suspected (with reason) of complicity in a projected Spanish invasion of England to release Mary Queen of Scots. This, with Lennox's part in the arrest and execution (1581) of th
1 CONC e earl of Morton, led to the Protestant nobles' seizure of James in the raid of Ruthven (1582). Against both his own and the king's wishes, Lennox was forced to leave Scotland. He died shortly thereafter.
0 @N6000000003904550879@ NOTE
1 CONT 1st Duke of Lennox
0 @N6000000003904550881@ NOTE
1 CONT He was the Duke of Lennox.
0 @N6000000003904550883@ NOTE
1 CONT Acceded: 5 AUG 1581 Interred: Aubigny Lord d'Aubigny 6th. The Complete Peerage vol.VII,pp.602-604.
0 @N6000000003904550885@ NOTE
1 CONT [chastity10374.ged]
1 CONT EVEN: 1st Duke of Lennox. 7th Lord of d'Aubigney Military Service
0 @N6000000003904550887@ NOTE
1 CONT Esme was a great favourite of James VI.
0 @N6000000003904550889@ NOTE
1 CONT Son of John Stuart and Anne de la Queuille; m.1 Katherine de Balsac; father of: 1. Henrietta who m. George Gordon 2. Ludovic who m. Sophia Ruthven, Jean Campbell, and Frances Howard 3. Esm ̌who m. Katherine Clifton 4. Mary who m. 
1 CONC John Erskine [Dave Utz <utz@aol.com]
1 CONT  Son of John, 3rd son of the 3rd Earl of Lennox (and so first cousin to Henry, Lord Darnley), Esm ̌Stewart grew up in France and succeeded his father as the Seigneur D'Aubigny. This 'fascinating Frenchman' arrived in Scotland in 1
1 CONC 579, was warmly welcomed by the young and largely friendless James VI, and quickly became his closest confidant and, some say, his lover. In 1580 James made him Earl of Lennox (the title having been resigned by the aged Robert Ste
1 CONC wart), and in 1581 created him Duke of Lennox. That same year Lennox and his associates secured the arrest of ex-Regent Morton on a charge of involvement in Darnley's murder; Morton was executed and Lennox elected to the Privy Cou
1 CONC ncil. His influence over the young King and their increasing intimacy raised a 'Popish Scare' among militant Presbyterians, who suspected him of being a Catholic spy acting for the French and Spanish governments. Even James's sign
1 CONC ature of the Negative Confession was not enought to allay their fears. In 1582 William Ruthven, 1st Earl of Gowrie and leader of the militant Presbyterians, captured the King and forced him to sign an order banishing the Duke of L
1 CONC ennox, who returned to France, where he died the following year. His son Ludovic became 2nd Duke of Lennox and hsi daughter married the 1st Marquis of Huntley. [Collins Encyclopaedia of Scotland, p. 901]
