0 @N6000000004671862473@ NOTE Stanley Howard
1 CONT (1677-1736) of Canterbury in Kent is the first of the Howard family whose history can be traced. His father went to Ireland it is thought to take part in the war then carrying on between King James II and the English forces under 
1 CONC William III and was never afterwards heard of. He is supposed to have resided in Berkshire. Stanley being thus left a child, perhaps an orphan, was brought up by his mother's relations, and apprenticed to a Woolcomber at Hitchin i
1 CONC n Hertfordshire, and when out of his time married a young woman of that place a friend, named Mary Whittenstall. Whether Stanley Howard began business at Hitchin is not known, but they moved to London, and he carried on the busine
1 CONC ss to which he had been brought up - that of a Woolcomber- in Hoxton Square, then an outskirt of the town, and probably in the seventeenth century part of a village.
1 CONT After a few years they removed to Canterbury, where they spent the remainder of their days. They had a numerous family, some of whom died young, but not much is known respecting them. The subjects of the present memoranda are thei
1 CONC r son Robert and his descendants, more especially of his second son, Robert.
0 @N6000000002176689569@ NOTE Succeeded his G-grandfather Louis XIV, and he in turn was succeeded by his
1 CONT grandson, Louis XVI. Louis XV's failure to provide a strong leadership and
1 CONT badly needed reforms contributed to the crisis that brought on the French
1 CONT Revolution. His phophecy was reported to have been: "After me, the deluge"
1 CONT was fulfilled in the overthrow of the French monarchy less than two decades
1 CONT later. Having gain the crown at age 5, France was governed by regency 'til
1 CONT 1723 by Philippe II, duc d' Orleans.
