0 @N6000000001404070925@ NOTE The quotes below appear in the typewritten Keith Papers I have labeled as Keith #8 and obviously compiled (and sometimes written) by Lora Francis. The Information aparently came from two books.
1 CONT
1 CONT (from another book)
1 CONT "James Keith, b. 1696 d. 1758, second son of William the nineth Earl Marisichal, born near Petershead, Scotland at the Castle ---he was commonly called Marschall in the service of Prussia under Fredrich the Great and was gtreatly 
1 CONC beloved by him."
1 CONT
1 CONT " Note: There is no record of marriage but by common law he had children - Charles, William and Alexander.  Eva Merthers, the mother, once it was spelled Eve Merthens and one place Merthers."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT "James Keith: b. in  Scotland in 1696; having an elder brother, he was entitled to no other designation but simply that of his name; he was carefully educated and early became proficient in such studies as were proper for young ge
1 CONC ntlemen of his rank; went to Aberdeen College, which was founded by his great grandfather; at the age of nineteen, the sound of the martial trumpet made him abandon the softer delights which he there enjoyed in the society of the 
1 CONC muses. He participated in many wars between England and Scotland; killed in battle, 1758, while attacking the Prussians; was a great scholar. When we take a review ofhis life, we are confounded with the great variety of his grave 
1 CONC exploits, and we scarcely are able to follow him through than number of places where he acquired renown. That which ought to render his memory forever precious is that he was a hero extremely humane; never omitting to do anything 
1 CONC in his power that might soften and alleviate the calamities of war, or lessen the number of its miseries."
0 @N6000000003216083404@ NOTE 5th Bart.  "became in right of his mother possessed of the estates of Manor Hamilton. This gentleman was chancellor of the Exchequer, and Speaker of the House of Commons, and on 22 Apr 1730, he was sworn one of the lord justices o
1 CONC f the kingdom."
1 CONT Sir Ralph Gore, of Belle Isle, county Fermanagh, served as High Sheriff of Leitrim in 1710 and later as Speaker of the Irish House of Commons. His mother was Hannah Hamilton of Manor Hamilton, county Leitrim. McParlan includes Joh
1 CONC n Gore of Woodford on a list of "resident gentlemen of property" in 1802. Robert Johnston Gore offered for sale lands at Tumonaghan as well as the house and demesne at Woodford in the Landed Estates Court in June 1865. The Gore es
1 CONC tate at Woodford still amounted to over 500 acres in the 1870s. By 1906 the house and c.100 acres of untenanted land was the property of Emily Upperton. Members of the Gore family served as High Sheriffs of Leitrim in 1677, 1734, 
1 CONC 1774 and 1775.
0 @N6000000001877487371@ NOTE Lord Resident of Council of Scotland
1 CONT Secretary of State
0 @N6000000004671862457@ NOTE Robert Howard
1 CONT (1706-1793) Son of Stanley Howard married Elizabeth Cullen and settled at Folkestone, then a small fishing town and a noted place for smuggling, the facilities of intercourse with the French Coast, and the concealment of the traff
1 CONC ic holding out inducements to this illicit and demoralizing practice.
1 CONT Robert and Elizabeth Howard lived in a house about the centre of town - then as now, very irregular having narrow steep streets in a part of it - and but a mean appearance at the best, though the surrounding fine hills covered wit
1 CONC h verdure and affording pasture for peculiarly handsome flocks of a small race of sheep and the white cliffs with their green summits sloping down to the beach, have always given the old town pleasing environs.
1 CONT Robert Howard's trade was that of a worker in tin to which were added many goods in brass and iron, The shop was well situated for the retail trade - and the worthy pair thus commencing life were frugal and industrious. A blessing
1 CONC  seemed to attend their honest endeavours - which it is believed has continued to the present day in their successors, those who have walked by the same rule and minded the same thing. Their family consisted of three sons and one 
1 CONC daughter, namely Sarah, Thomas, Robert and John.
