0 @N6000000003216083588@ NOTE Died without issue. Member of Parliament for co. Donegal. He assumed the surname St. George upon inheriting the estates of his maternal ancestors, his mother being first co-heir, and eventually sole heir of her brother, SIr Richar
1 CONC d St. George of Dunmore
0 @N6000000003216083422@ NOTE 6th Bart.
1 CONT "was a distinguished military officer, having obtained the command of a battalion at the battle of Laffeldt, 2 July 1747, when only a captain, owing to the fall of his senior officers, distinguished himself so highly that he recei
1 CONC ved the thanks of the Duke of Cumberland, on the following day, at the head of his regiment. he subsequently represented the co. Donegal in parliament and was elevated to the peerage of Ireland, 30 June 1764, by the title of 'Baro
1 CONC n Gore, of Manor Gore, co. Donelgal' On 25 August 1768 he was advanced to the 'Viscounty of Belleisle' and created 'Earl of Ross', 4 January, 1772. In 1788, the Earl, who had attained the rank of lieut.-general, was appointed comm
1 CONC ander-in-chief Ireland, in the absence of Lieut.-General Pitt."
1 CONT
1 CONT From The Porter Papers (Public Record Office of Northern Ireland) summary published at http://proni.nics.gov.uk/records/private/porter.htm
1 CONT "The Belleisle estate came on the market in 1830. Since the Plantation, it had belonged to the Gore family, baronets, of Manor Gore, co. Donegal. The last of this branch of the Gore family was Sir Ralph Gore, 6th Bt. and one and o
1 CONC nly Earl of Ross (1725-1802). He had greatly ornamented the pleasure grounds, particularly with follies and garden buildings designed by the well-known Thomas Wright, but the main house was still the modest lodge built by his fath
1 CONC er in ca. 1720. Lord Ross had died without legitimate issue, leaving Belleisle to his natural daughter, Mary, wife of Sir Richard Hardinge, 1st Bt. The rental of the Belleisle estate was just over £2,200 in 1828, so the Hardinges 
1 CONC were far from affluent. For whatever reason, in 1809, the let the house and demesne of Belleisle and the islands immediately surrounding it. Lady Hardinge died in 1824 and Sir Richard in 1826. His nephew and successor, the Rev. Si
1 CONC r Charles Hardinge, 2nd Bt. of Tunbridge, Kent, had no connection with Ireland and presumable no interst in Belleisle. Accordingly, in 1830, Sir Charles and his trustees sold the entire Belleisle estate consisting of the manors of
1 CONC  Belleisle and Carrick, together with a small leasehold addendum acquired by Sir Ralph Gore, 4th Bt, in 1724, to the Rev. John Grey Porter of Kilskeery for £68,000."
1 CONT
1 CONT Description of Belleisle estate:
1 CONT "The deed of conveyance, dated 1 May 1830 provides the following comprehensive description of the townlands and other component's oof Porter's purchase: ' all that and those the manor or reputed manor of Carrick estate with severa
1 CONC l subdenominations thereof, viz.: Carrick, Ballymacmanus, Beragh, Behon, Beacho, Carrowkeel, Clogtogal, Coolbuck, Cloone, Crichamuack, Crogh, Crigaragallan, Derryclane, Derrihoney, Drumdron, Derryclawer, Aghedereoghlisk, Ederaglus
1 CONC h, Fahort, Fanghart, Farragh, Foxhill, Gorteene, Inish, Killee, Killingoan, Kildermot, Lagnaught, Lisreagh, Liskellaw, Lisbellaw, Portslinnagh, Motiernon, Mogairah, Mullybrill, Stony Park, Mayonragn, Shanco, Snow Hill, Tallymacall
1 CONC , Topitnow, Track, Tatygar, Barniskie, Lanehill, Kiew, Loughie, Glasdrommond, Drumbrughas, Conlenowrane, Aughvagh and Tatenmallard, all situate, lying and being in the parish of Cleenish and the barony of Tyrkennedy in the co. of 
1 CONC Fermanagh aforesaid...and also all other the messuages lands and hereditments being part or appendant to the said manor and reputed manor with the several subdenominations thereof...viz: Aughekillymade, Derryholaght or Blackrock, 
1 CONC Derryharney, Innishbeg and Belle Isle, all situate, lying and being in the parish of Cleenish, baronies of Tyrkennedy and Magherastephana and county of Fermanagh aforesaid...'"
