0 @N6000000002188440175@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      Louis XVI, King of France from 1774 to 1792, he was the third son of the dauphin (Louis) and Marie Josèphe of Sachsen, grandson and successor of King Louis XV.  In 1770 he married the Austrian Princess Marie Antoinette. His e
1 CONC arly attempts to enact reforms and to appoint competent and upright ministers met with general approval, but his character was unsuited to provide the leadership needed to control the complex social and political conflict smolderi
1 CONC ng in France. Shy, dull, and corpulent, he preferred the hunting field and his locksmith's workshop to the council chamber; indecisiveness made him subject to the poor advice of his intimates.  The reforms begun by his able minist
1 CONC ers A. R. J. Turgot and Chrétien de Malesherbes were opposed by the court faction, including Marie Antoinette. A more important obstacle to Turgot's plans was the opposition of the parlements, which were revived after the dismissa
1 CONC l of René de Maupeou. Turgot was dismissed in May, 1776, and Louis appointed in October of 1776 Jacques Necker director of the treasury. The King supported most of Necker's reforms and economies, but the costly French intervention
1 CONC  in the American Revolution more than canceled the savings, and Necker's borrowing greatly swelled the debt. Necker's attempt to gain greater control over policy by courting public opinion was rebuffed at court, and he resigned in
1 CONC  protest in May, 1781.  Necker's successors, Charles Alexandre de Calonne (1783-1787) and Étienne Charles Loménie de Brienne (1787-1788), were unable to ward off bankruptcy. When the interest-bearing debt had risen to a huge figur
1 CONC e, the king convoked in 1787 the Assembly of Notables and asked their consent to tax the privileged classes. The notables made a few minor reforms but refused to consent to taxation, referring this to the States-General.  Louis fi
1 CONC nally convoked the States-General in 1789. Necker, restored in 1788, prevailed upon Louis to double the number of deputies from the third, or popular estate. This increase, however, would be meaningless if the estates met separate
1 CONC ly and voted as units rather than as individuals; the nobles (first estate) and the clergy (second estate), could still outvote the third estate. The King's opposition to the combined meeting of the estates and his procrastination
1 CONC  on this issue led the third estate to proclaim itself a National Assembly, thus signaling the end of absolutism in France. Louis ordered the estates to meet and vote separately, but he was forced on June 27, 1789 to yield and all
1 CONC ow the estates to sit together and vote by head.  Shortly afterward Louis sent troops to Paris, where he suspected the French Guards of being too sympathetic to the assembly. Rumors circulated that the king intended to suppress th
1 CONC e assembly, and the dismissal of the popular Necker provoked the storming of the Bastille on July 14, 1789. Louis again had to capitulate; he ordered the withdrawal of the royal troops, reinstated Necker, and accepted the new nati
1 CONC onal red, white, and blue cockade. Despite his outward acceptance of the revolution, Louis allowed reactionary plotting of the queen and court, and in August refused to approve the abolition of feudal rights. In October of 1789, a
1 CONC  crowd marched on Versailles and forced the royal family to return to Paris, where they were confined in the Tuileries palace. Louis's position, further compromised by the plots of émigré circles, was definitively ruined when the 
1 CONC royal family attempted in June of 1791 to flee France in disguise. They were apprehended at Varennes, and their attempted flight was considered proof of their treasonable dealings with foreign powers. Louis was forced to accept th
1 CONC e constitution of 1791, which limited his power, but preserved the royal veto and his power to appoint ministers.  After his return he was in communication with Austria and Prussia, urging them to rescue him. In 1792 the early rev
1 CONC erses of the French army in the war with Austria and Prussia and the Duke of Brsunschweig's threat to destroy Paris if the royal family were harmed infuriated the Paris sans-culottes. The King and his family were imprisoned in the
1 CONC  Temple on Aug 10, 1792. In September, simultaneously with the defeat of the Prussians at Valmy, the Convention declared a Republic. Incriminating evidence against Louis was later found, and he was tried by the Convention. Found g
1 CONC uilty by a unanimous vote, he was sentenced to death by a vote of 361 to 288, with 72 calling for a delay. He was guillotined on Jan. 21, 1793, facing death with courage
0 @N6000000002322350147@ NOTE He was the first king after the Monarchy was restored with the abdication of
1 CONT Napoleon I in 1814. On Napoleon's return in 1815, he fled to Belgium, but
1 CONT later that year he was restored again after the Battle of Waterloo. He lived
1 CONT in exile in several countries after the coming of the French Revolution. His
1 CONT brother Charles X later succeeded him to the French crown.
0 @N6000000002176689546@ NOTE He was the first king after the Monarchy was restored with the abdication of
1 CONT Napoleon I in 1814. On Napoleon's return in 1815, he fled to Belgium, but
1 CONT later that year he was restored again after the Battle of Waterloo. He lived
1 CONT in exile in several countries after the coming of the French Revolution. His
1 CONT brother Charles X later succeeded him to the French crown.
0 @N6000000002188440238@ NOTE Louis XVIII, King of France from 1814 to 1824, was the brother of King Louis XVI. Known as the Count of Provence, he fled France in 1791 to Koblenz from the French Revolution and intrigued to bring about foreign intervention again
1 CONC st the revolutionaries. He was recognized as King by the émigrés after the death in 1795 of King Louis XVII. He passed his exile on the Continent and in England. With the assistance of Charles de Talleyrand, he was restored in 181
1 CONC 4 to the French throne by the allies after their entry into Paris. He adopted a conciliatory policy toward the former revolutionists and granted a constitutional charter. Forced to flee once more on the news of the return of Emper
1 CONC or Napoleon I, he returned with the allies in 1815 after the defeat at Waterloo had ended Napoleon's rule of a Hundred Days. His chief ministers were at first moderates Armand Emmanuel, Duke of Richelieu, and Élie Decazes—but the 
1 CONC ultraroyalists, led by Louis's brother, the Count of Artois (later Charles X), triumphed after the assassination in 1820 of the count's son, Charles Ferdinand, Duke of Berry. Louis, then old and suffering from gout, allowed the ul
1 CONC traroyalists to take control. The new ministry headed by the Count of Villèle was thoroughly reactionary. Electoral laws were revised to increase the influence of the wealthy classes, and civil liberties were curbed. This trend co
1 CONC ntinued and was intensified during the reign of his successor, King Charles X.
0 @N6000000002240844147@ NOTE He was the first king after the Monarchy was restored with the abdication of
1 CONT Napoleon I in 1814. On Napoleon's return in 1815, he fled to Belgium, but
1 CONT later that year he was restored again after the Battle of Waterloo. He lived
1 CONT in exile in several countries after the coming of the French Revolution. His
1 CONT brother Charles X later succeeded him to the French crown.
0 @N6000000002244433962@ NOTE He was the first king after the Monarchy was restored with the abdication of
1 CONT Napoleon I in 1814. On Napoleon's return in 1815, he fled to Belgium, but
1 CONT later that year he was restored again after the Battle of Waterloo. He lived
