1 CONT WILLIAM JOHNSON
1 CONT OBITUARY
1 CONT Photocopy sent by William Johnson to Steve Harrison, 19 Jan 1988.
1 CONT From "Western Standard" newspaper, Mercer County, Ohio, 21 Sep 1852:
1 CONT "DIED. On Sunday morning, September 19th, 1852, at the residence of Elihu Compton, Esq. in Dublin township, WILLIAM JOHNSON, Sr., aged 72 years and 8 months. Mr. Johnson was a native of Lexington, Ky., and at an early day removed 
1 CONC to Clark county Ohio, where he continued to reside till about 18 months ago, when he removed to his county, and made his home with his son-in-law, Mr. Compton."
1 CONT In a letter from William Johnson to Steve Harrison, 19 Nov 1987, he says that: "William Johnson, at his death, owned 3 properties. One was in Clark Co., Ohio. One was in Mercer Co., Ohio and one was in Jay Co., Ind. Deeds by his c
1 CONC hildren on sale of property in Clark co. prove the switch of name from Johnston to Johnson."
0 @N6000000003016387699@ NOTE From Bedford County, TN Family History Book:  One of the earlier Hix ancestors was John Hix Sr. of Campbell County, VA.  He was married to a lady named Sibeller and fathered several children.  Their names were Rebecca, Sarah, Libb
1 CONC y, Sibeller, Martha, John, Elizabeth, Sabra and William. Years later, Sabra's will indicated four other sisters:  Annfidila, Mildred, Jerusha and Barsheba.
1 CONT William Hix married Alcey Dews, Daughter of John and Elizabeth Dews of Fairfax Co., VA in Oct. 1800.  Their son, Demarquis Dews Hix, was born in January 1802 in Fairfax County.  Demarquis was very young when his mother died and hi
1 CONC s father remarried and had other children.
1 CONT Four children of John and Sibella:  John, Elizabeth, Sabra and William, with their families, pioneered to Bedford County, TN before 1813.  The Hix brothers bought land on Big Flat Creek for $311 fromNewton Cannon, who later became
1 CONC  the 10th Governor of TN in 1835.  Sabra Hix Hubbard and William Hix bought land in 1811 and set up the first tanning yard in the Flat Creek Community. 
1 CONT Demarquis Dews Hix came to Flat Creek with his father's family and in 1823 he married Malinda Stewart, daughter of Joshua Yates Stewart.  That same year he also bought land and built a home.  The house was located on Old Flat Cree
1 CONC k and Shelbyville Turnpike, a few yards north of what is now New Hope Road.    Demarquis Dews and Malinda Hix were parents of 12 children, all of whom reached maturity.  A saying at the time around Flat Creek was:  DD Hix, in a fi
1 CONC x, three born, three married in 1846.
1 CONT
1 CONT See book for additional information.
